#capitolo(numero: "", etichetta: [Introduction], titolo: [Why I Wrote This Book])

There’s a moment during aligner treatment when we realize the problem isn’t the planned tooth movement. The patient isn’t wearing their aligners as agreed, skips their orthodontic elastics, or stops wearing their retainer. We can explain once again what they need to do. But if that explanation hasn’t worked so far, why would it work today?

I wrote this book to answer a question that has stayed with me for years: #strong[What can I actually do when a patient doesn’t follow instructions?]; I wanted to move beyond simply recognizing the problem and find a way to address it during the appointment, when the patient is in front of me and I have to decide what to say and what next step to suggest.

For a while, I thought the difficulties I was having were mostly my fault. Maybe I didn’t know how to communicate effectively. In Italy, at least in the conversations I had with other orthodontists, we talked a lot about tooth movements, protocols, and clinical outcomes, but little about what to say when a patient didn’t follow instructions.

Then I discovered #emph[Aligner Insider];, Barry Glaser’s Facebook group. Reading his posts, Adam Goodman’s posts, and discussions among colleagues with far more experience than I had, I realized they faced the same problems. Many worked in the United States, where communication with patients gets a great deal of attention. Yet they, just like me, were looking for better words and strategies. In short, #emph[it’s the same story everywhere];. That discovery made me feel less alone and prompted me to share what has worked in my practice over the years.

The scripts you’ll read grew out of eighteen years of experience with aligners. They aren’t phrases I dreamed up at a desk. They’re the result of real conversations, successful attempts, and failed attempts. Over time, I changed the questions I asked, the explanations I gave, and the ways I presented the consequences of a choice, trying to understand what actually helped a patient get back on track with treatment.

I also noticed that many of those conversations kept recurring. The patients and their explanations changed, but I often found myself facing the same problems and having to decide all over again how to address them. So I began organizing the responses that had worked, trying to understand not just which words to use, but when to say them, what to check, and what to do at the next appointment. That’s how the scripts became part of protocols: pathways that help my team and me stay on the same course. They don’t eliminate the need to assess each case individually, but they give us a clearer starting point and leave fewer decisions to the fatigue or rush of the day.

In these pages, you’ll also find a few communication tactics. Sometimes I use what I would honestly call a #emph[white lie];: a simple reason that appeals to what matters to that patient, even though it isn’t a scientific explanation of the problem. I’ve chosen to say so openly because I don’t want those phrases mistaken for clinical evidence. They must never be used to conceal a risk, distort a diagnosis, or prevent the patient from making an informed choice. Their purpose is to help the patient do something useful that they already know they should do but struggle to keep doing over time.

#strong[No script works for everyone every time, and none guarantees 100% adherence.]; The strategies I suggest need to be chosen and adapted to the social and cultural context in which we work. You don’t need to use them all or repeat them word for word: they can give you an idea for how to handle a situation you haven’t been able to resolve until now.

I’d like this book to be a tool you keep close at hand in your daily practice: a collection of ideas to choose from, adapt, and put to the test. #strong[The goal is to leave the appointment with one more way to help the patient stay on track and bring treatment to a conclusion you both agree on.];

#capitolo(numero: "1", etichetta: [Chapter 1], titolo: [The Day I Realized Moving Teeth Wasn’t Enough], epigrafe: [“The only true voyage of discovery would be not to visit strange lands but to possess other eyes.”], fonte: [Marcel Proust])

If you’ve decided to read this book, you probably already use clear aligners or are seriously considering adding them to your clinical practice. Either way, before I tell you my story, I’d like to ask you a question.

Have you ever gone home after a day at work with a case you couldn’t stop thinking about?

Maybe it was a treatment you had planned with extreme care. You’d spent hours on the plan, checked every detail, revised the digital treatment setup several times, and when you finally clicked the approval button, you were convinced you’d done an excellent job. Then the patient comes back a few months later. You ask how things are going, and they give you the answer every orthodontist hears at least a dozen times a day:

#dialogo[#emph[“Great, Doctor.”];]

You get them settled in the chair, begin the exam, and within seconds you realize something is off. I’ve had that feeling many times myself. For years, though, whenever I faced a situation like that, my reaction was always the same: look for a technical explanation. I thought I’d made a mistake in the treatment plan, underestimated a tooth movement, chosen an ineffective attachment, or designed biomechanics that weren’t sophisticated enough. I was convinced that if I studied more, I’d find the answer.

I didn’t know I was asking the wrong question.

#sezione[Let’s Start at the Beginning]

When I think back to the young man I was more than twenty years ago, I realize I held a belief that was as simple as it was reassuring: to become a good orthodontist, I just had to keep learning. It wasn’t a naive or superficial thought. On the contrary, it drove everything I did. It gave me an extraordinary sense of control, because it meant my future depended almost entirely on how much effort I was willing to put into my education.

If something didn’t work as I’d expected, the explanation always seemed the same to me: I hadn’t learned everything I needed to know yet. If I ran into a difficulty, it meant there was a book I hadn’t read, a conference I hadn’t attended, or a colleague I could still learn something from. Even when I saw others getting better results than I was, I told myself they had simply discovered something I didn’t know yet and that, sooner or later, I’d come to understand it too.

Looking back now, I realize that belief stayed with me through much of my career and, deep down, is still with me. I believe that devoting time to study is one of the greatest ways an orthodontist can show respect for their patients. What has changed over time isn’t how much I value education, but what the word learning means to me. At first, I was convinced that learning meant accumulating technical knowledge. It would take me many years to understand that there was another kind of learning, perhaps even more important, that no one had ever told me about.

When I was taking my first steps in the profession, orthodontics was undergoing a profound transformation. Of all the innovations of those years, one captured my attention completely: clear aligners. Some people regarded them with curiosity, others with deep skepticism, and still others considered them little more than an esthetic alternative to brackets on the social six. I, on the other hand, immediately had the sense that something much more important was happening. For the first time, it wasn’t just a treatment tool that was changing: the very way orthodontists thought was changing.

With fixed braces, you could continually adjust course during treatment. With aligners, you had to envision the outcome before you even began. It was a radical shift in perspective that forced you to plan for the future rather than simply manage the present, and it was probably this intellectual challenge that made me fall in love with them right away.

Deep down, I’ve always been drawn to difficult things. Not because I liked making life harder for myself, but because whenever I encountered something that seemed impossible, the same question would arise in me: #emph[“How can I make it work?”];

#sezione[Searching for the Answer]

It was that curiosity that drove me to immerse myself in the world of aligners. I underlined every scientific article, read every book with a pencil in hand, and treated every conference as an opportunity I couldn’t miss. If I heard about a colleague who had developed an interesting approach to biomechanics or a speaker who could tackle a problem from a different perspective, I immediately started looking into it, wherever they were. Over the years, that spirit took me across much of the world. I wasn’t interested in collecting certificates to hang on the wall. I wanted to come home with at least one insight that, on Monday morning, could make even one treatment better than I would have delivered the previous Friday.

Every trip was an investment of time, energy, and money. It meant leaving the practice, rearranging my work, giving up time with my family, spending hours in airports, enduring endless flights, and often getting home Sunday evening only to be back at the practice Monday morning. Yet I never saw any of it as a sacrifice. It was simply the price I was happy to pay to keep growing.

Deep down, I was convinced that if I kept gathering all those fragments of experience, sooner or later I’d complete the puzzle. I truly believed that somewhere there was a formula that could make every treatment perfectly predictable, and that my job was simply to find it.

Then I began to experience something I think everyone who genuinely loves their work has in common: the more I learned, the more questions I had. Every answer inevitably raised another question, every certainty was challenged by the next patient, and whenever I thought I had finally understood a subject in depth, a case came along that forced me to rethink everything.

Of course, back then I didn’t take all this in stride the way I do today. Whenever a treatment took a different course from the one I had envisioned, my reaction was almost automatic: I was convinced the mistake was mine. If the outcome didn’t match what I had planned, it meant I still lacked some skill. In my mind, the solution was always the same: study more, take another course, read another book, board another plane to listen to someone who might have already found the answer I was still looking for.

That belief had helped me grow, but it also concealed a small trap: I thought every problem must have a technical solution. Without realizing it, I was looking for biomechanical answers to questions that belonged to an entirely different realm.

#sezione[I Must Be the Problem]

Meanwhile, my practice was growing, my cases were becoming more numerous and complex, and the first invitations to speak at conferences began to arrive. I loved teaching because preparing a lecture forced me to organize my thoughts and understand what I was doing even better. Yet just as my career seemed to offer more and more validation from the outside, I still had a feeling inside that was hard to explain.

The first time someone gave it a name, I wasn’t in a medical office, at a conference, or in a session with a psychologist. I was at home, having dinner with two friends.

Alice and Federico are the founders of Geldis, a company born from an idea closely connected to my work: creating a gel specifically for the daily cleaning of aligners. Over time, though, our relationship had grown well beyond work, and we had become friends.

I don’t remember exactly how the conversation started. We were probably talking about work, courses, or one of the many conferences where I spoke. At some point, I told them something I had always considered a quirk of my personality.

I explained that, despite my years of experience, I still didn’t feel completely comfortable stepping onto a stage. It wasn’t fear of public speaking, and it wasn’t uncertainty about the material, either: I prepared every presentation with almost obsessive care and knew the subjects I covered inside and out. It was something else.

I could be standing before a thousand or two thousand people who had come to hear me—colleagues who had paid for a course, traveled, and devoted a day of their lives to learning something from me. During breaks, they would stop me, ask questions, and tell me how something I’d suggested had changed the way they planned treatments. Yet part of me kept wondering whether I really deserved to be there. When I finished telling them, Alice looked at me as if the explanation were obvious.

#dialogo[#emph[“Enzo, you have impostor syndrome.”];]

Those words struck me because I had always thought of an impostor as someone who deliberately deceives others, claims skills they don’t have, or occupies a position they don’t deserve. I wasn’t trying to deceive anyone. I studied, worked, and prepared every talk with almost obsessive care. But Alice wasn’t saying I was an impostor. She was saying that, despite all the evidence to the contrary, part of me still feared I was one.

Over the following days, I began reading about it. I learned that the phenomenon had been described in the late 1970s by psychologists Pauline Clance and Suzanne Imes in accomplished, highly capable women who, despite what they had achieved, struggled to recognize their success as a result of their own abilities. They might attribute their successes to luck, good timing, help from others, or the extraordinary effort they had made to compensate for perceived shortcomings, while difficulties took on disproportionate weight and seemed to confirm their feared inadequacy at once.

I recognized that pattern immediately. An invitation to a conference could be due to my relationship with a company; a successful lecture, to the almost obsessive preparation I had put into it; compliments from colleagues, to their generosity. I could always find an explanation that diminished my accomplishments. A clinical difficulty, on the other hand, needed no interpretation: in my mind, it was my fault.

Today, the literature tends to use the term impostor phenomenon because it isn’t an illness or a psychiatric diagnosis, but a recurring way of interpreting oneself. It has also been observed among physicians, dentists, and other health care professionals—fields where high standards and real responsibilities make it essential to keep questioning yourself.

That mindset had a positive side, too. It had pushed me to study, travel, learn from more experienced professionals, and not settle for the first answers I found. The problem arose when self\-criticism stopped helping me understand a difficulty and became a judgment on my professional identity.

There is an enormous difference between asking why a tooth movement didn’t occur and asking whether we’re really capable of using aligners. The first is a clinical question: it allows us to gather information, examine the causes, and adjust course. The second turns a single episode into a verdict on our entire worth as professionals.

That was when I understood how much that feeling had shaped my work in the practice, too. When a treatment didn’t go as planned, I focused almost all my attention on what I could control directly: my own work. What the patient told me remained in the background as an accepted fact, and if they said they had followed all my instructions, the conclusion seemed inevitable: I had made a mistake.

It was like assessing whether a drug worked without knowing whether the patient had taken it. In my daily practice, I risked changing the treatment before checking that basic premise.

#sezione[The Answer Was Right in Front of Me]

That realization led me to look more closely at certain cases that still didn’t add up. The more experience I gained, the more I realized that the cases I couldn’t explain were almost never the most complex ones. I would treat extremely difficult malocclusions that progressed exactly as I had planned, while seemingly simple treatments appeared to stall for no logical reason.

At first, I dismissed it all as a string of coincidences. Then I began paying closer attention and, almost without realizing it, started looking elsewhere. For years, I had watched almost exclusively the teeth, the scans, the attachments, the biomechanics, and the planned movements. All my attention was focused there because I was convinced that was where all the answers lay. Instead, I realized that the variable I kept looking for wasn’t on the computer screen; it was sitting in the chair, right in front of me.

It wasn’t the teeth. It was the people.

I remember one morning perfectly. I had two follow\-up appointments scheduled back\-to\-back. The patients were two young men about the same age, with very similar malocclusions, nearly identical treatment plans, and the same amount of time since their last visits. Clinically, the two cases looked like carbon copies.

The first came in. He greeted me enthusiastically and, before I could even ask how things were going, pulled a bag of orthodontic elastics out of his pocket.

#dialogo[#emph[“Doc, I think I’ve gone through more than expected. Could you give me a few extra packs?”];]

I examined him and found exactly what I expected to see. Treatment was going according to plan. A few minutes later, the second young man came in. He was polite, too, and smiling. When I asked him the same question, he answered with the same confidence:

#dialogo[#emph[“Everything’s great. I wear my elastics all the time.”];]

I examined him, and it took me only a few seconds to see that treatment wasn’t progressing as I’d expected. Of course, that didn’t prove he wasn’t wearing his orthodontic elastics; there could be several explanations. Until then, though, my mind would have automatically started looking for a biomechanical cause. This time, I caught myself asking a different question: what if the problem wasn’t the treatment plan?

I didn’t immediately look for an answer. I simply kept observing, and over the following months I began to recognize similar situations with surprising frequency. The faces, ages, and malocclusions changed, but certain behaviors seemed to repeat. Some patients were extremely meticulous, while others couldn’t remember an instruction they had received only a short time earlier; some asked questions until they understood exactly what to do, while others nodded during the visit, only to return for their next appointment without having followed the instructions; some parents made their child’s treatment a shared project, while others seemed to lose all interest in how it was going after the first appointment.

That was when I began to understand that trust and verification weren’t incompatible. The relationship between clinician and patient necessarily rests on trust, and we can’t turn every appointment into an interrogation, but checking doesn’t mean accusing. A sound clinical decision can’t depend solely on information we haven’t been able to verify.

Small incidents began to take on a different meaning: an aligner I expected to have been worn for weeks that looked new, a pack of orthodontic elastics that was still unopened, a patient who couldn’t remember which aligner they were wearing or who came to an appointment without bringing it. None of those details, on its own, was enough to establish how well the patient had followed the instructions, but taken together, they forced me to ask how many times I had looked for a biomechanical solution to a behavioral problem.

#sezione[Beginning to See the People]

From that point on, I began to look at patients differently. Of course, I still analyzed photographs, scans, occlusal relationships, and planned movements, but more and more of my attention was shifting elsewhere.

I noticed that people to whom I gave exactly the same instructions reacted in completely different ways. Some wanted to know every detail, while others seemed annoyed by explanations that went on too long; some needed reassurance, while others mainly wanted to know how long it would take and what would happen if something didn’t work. Some followed instructions with almost obsessive precision, while others, despite understanding them perfectly, adapted them to fit their lives until they became something quite different from what I had prescribed.

During my years at university and in specialty training, I had studied anatomy, biomechanics, craniofacial growth, diagnosis, cephalometrics, materials, and treatment planning. I had learned to measure angles with almost obsessive precision and to spend hours discussing the inclination of a root or planning a movement of a few tenths of a millimeter. But no one had ever taught me how to recognize the patient who would nod throughout the appointment and then follow only half the instructions they had been given, or how to understand why two people to whom I had explained exactly the same thing could behave so differently.

It was as if all our training stopped right at the door to the practice. Beyond that point lay territory left almost entirely to personal experience, made up of trial, error, and intuition.

That was when I began to suspect something that, at the time, felt almost heretical: perhaps the predictability of a treatment depends on more than the quality of its plan. Perhaps there is a variable no software can calculate, one that doesn’t appear in a cephalometric analysis, can’t be measured with calipers, and can’t be planned in front of a screen, yet is present in every single treatment we undertake.

That variable sat in front of me every day. It had a face, a history, a personality, beliefs, fears, and its own way of interpreting what happened to it. In other words, it had a mind.

I had spent years trying to make the biomechanics of my treatments increasingly predictable. Now I was beginning to understand that I had looked at only part of the problem. I could plan with great precision what I wanted the teeth to do, but between that plan and the final result was still a person who, every day, would have to make decisions that could help achieve it, slow it down, or change it.

This realization didn’t immediately give me answers. It gave me something more important: a new area to explore. For years, when a treatment wasn’t progressing, my first question had been #emph[“What did I do wrong?”]; I still ask myself that question today, because it would be dangerous to stop, but it can’t be the only one, and above all, it can’t come before an even more important question:

What really happened?

Only after piecing together what happened can we determine how much responsibility lies with the treatment plan, how much with biology, and how much, if any, with the patient’s behavior.

I had spent years studying how teeth move. Now I finally understood that, to truly understand what happens during treatment, I would have to start studying the people who live with those treatments every day, too.

And that was a far more complex world than I had imagined.

