#capitolo(numero: "2", etichetta: [Chapter 2], titolo: [Understanding the Patient], epigrafe: [“When dealing with people, remember you are not dealing with creatures of logic, but creatures of emotion.”], fonte: [Dale Carnegie])

I was used to speaking to very different patients using almost the same words every time. I explained the treatment, outlined the goals, and gave precise instructions. If they understood, I considered my job done.

Over time, I have come to understand that understanding a piece of information does not mean giving it the same importance. If different people listen, decide, and react in different ways, why should they all be expected to cooperate in the same way?

Some patients want to know every detail, while others feel overwhelmed when they receive too much information. Some make decisions quickly, while others need time, reassurance, and confirmation before making a choice. Some people mainly want to know how long treatment will last; others ask to see photographs, simulations, and X\-rays; still others seem most interested in how the treatment will fit into their daily lives.

Effective communication, then, is not just about knowing what to say. It is also about understanding how to say it to the person in front of us.

Over time, psychologists, behavioral researchers, management experts, and trainers have developed numerous models to describe these differences. Some grew out of scientific research on personality; others were created as practical tools to improve leadership, teamwork, sales, or relationship management.

None of these models can fully capture the complexity of a human being. We are shaped by our histories, our upbringing, our experiences, the circumstances we find ourselves in, our emotions at the moment, and the people we are interacting with. The same person may behave very differently at work, at home, during a financial negotiation, or while sitting in a dental chair.

It would therefore be simplistic to divide everyone into rigid categories. Yet the ability to simplify is also the strength of some behavioral models. They are not meant to tell us who a person really is or to provide a psychological diagnosis, but they can help us recognize certain prevailing tendencies and more quickly choose the language, pace, and amount of information best suited to the person we are speaking with.

One of the best\-known and most widely used of these models is DISC.

Its origins are generally traced to the American psychologist William Moulton Marston, who published #emph[Emotions of Normal People]; in 1928, a book in which he analyzed how people respond to the environment they perceive around them.

People may tend to respond more actively or more reflectively, focus primarily on results or on relationships, and seek control, approval, stability, or precision to varying degrees. These tendencies give rise to four styles now commonly identified by the letters D, I, S, and C: Dominance, Influence, Steadiness, and Conscientiousness.

DISC has gradually come to be used in hiring, leadership, team building, sales, and conflict management, but it should not be treated as an absolute truth about human personality: it is, rather, a practical map of behavioral and communication styles.

Modern psychology, in fact, does not recognize a universal classification of four personality types. The model with the strongest scientific support is the #strong[Big Five];, which describes people along five broad dimensions: extraversion, agreeableness, conscientiousness, neuroticism or emotional instability, and openness to experience. The fundamental difference is that the Big Five does not place each person in a rigid category: we all have these traits to varying degrees, and their combination helps shape our individual profile.

The Big Five therefore has a stronger scientific basis for describing personality. DISC, by contrast, offers a more accessible language for observing how people interact, and that is precisely what interests us. Our goal is not to conduct a complete psychological assessment of a patient or give them a questionnaire before every orthodontic appointment. We need a simple lens that can help us recognize certain communication styles and adapt how we talk with them.

In fact, a person rarely fits just one of the four profiles perfectly. More often, they show a combination of traits, with one or two predominant styles, and their behavior may change depending on the situation. A normally sociable patient may become extremely analytical when facing surgery; a usually cautious person may become dominant when they think their child’s treatment is progressing too slowly; a very detail\-oriented patient may suddenly become emotional when told an extraction is being considered.

Our task, then, is not to stick a label on anyone, but to observe. What questions do they ask? How quickly do they want to get to the point? Do they mainly need data, reassurance, control, or a personal connection? How do they express doubt? How do they raise an objection? The answers to these questions can help us choose the most effective way to talk with them.

#sezione[The Dominance\-Style Patient]

The Dominance\-style patient is focused on results. They like to be in control, make decisions quickly, and tend to assess situations in terms of goals, timelines, costs, and consequences.

They often come into the office with a fairly clear idea of what they want. They may have seen other professionals, read up on the subject, or decided in advance what result they would consider acceptable. They are not necessarily aggressive, but they communicate directly and may have little patience for explanations they see as unnecessarily long.

During the appointment, they tend to get straight to the point and may ask questions such as “#emph[How long will it take?];” “#emph[What’s the fastest approach?];” “#emph[Can we avoid surgery?];” “#emph[What’s the best solution?];” “#emph[How much will it cost me in total?];” or “#emph[What happens if we don’t get the result?];”

These questions may sound blunt, but we should not immediately interpret them as an attack. Very often, they are the patient’s way of checking whether the clinician is knowledgeable, confident, and clear. A Dominance\-style patient does not just want an answer: they want to know whether the person in front of them can guide the treatment without hesitation.

With this patient, it helps to start with the conclusion and then explain the reasoning. Instead of opening with a lengthy description of the malocclusion, we can say:

#dialogo[#emph[“In your case, we have two realistic options. The first is simpler, but it involves a compromise in the result. The second requires a more involved treatment, but it would also allow us to correct the skeletal problem. Let me quickly explain the advantages and limitations of each.”];]

A Dominance\-style patient appreciates clear alternatives. That does not mean letting them choose a clinically inappropriate treatment, but involving them in the decision by making a clear distinction between what is possible and what is not. Whenever we can, it helps to connect our instructions to concrete consequences:

#dialogo[#emph[“Treatment will take approximately eighteen months, provided you wear the aligners as instructed. If you wear them less than prescribed, treatment will take longer, and we may need to replan certain stages.”];]

This kind of wording is generally more effective than a vague instruction such as #emph[“try to wear them as much as possible”]; because the first links a behavior to a consequence, while the second leaves much more room for interpretation.

When a Dominance\-style patient raises an objection, it is important not to turn it into a contest. If they ask, #emph[“Isn’t there a way to do it faster?”]; a defensive answer such as “#emph[No, because biology takes time, and you need to trust me”]; risks turning the conversation into a confrontation. Instead, we can respond:

#dialogo[#emph[“We can cut down on some scheduling delays, but we can’t speed up the biological process beyond a certain point without increasing the risk. In your case, this is the fastest safe approach. The factor we can do the most to control is how consistently you wear your aligners.”];]

That way, we maintain our authority without turning it into a power struggle. With this type of patient, then, it is best to avoid lengthy introductions, vague answers, absolute promises, and a paternalistic tone. Being direct does not mean being cold; it means respecting their need for clarity.

#sezione[The Influence\-Style Patient]

The Influence\-style patient is generally focused on relationships, emotions, and the overall experience. They communicate easily, tell stories about their life, share how they feel, and tend to form a personal connection with the clinician and the team quickly.

They are often the patient who comes in smiling, chats with the front desk staff, comments on the office, and tells us how they came to find the practice. During the appointment, they may jump quickly from one subject to another and get caught up in their enthusiasm.

Their questions often concern not just the treatment, but how it will fit into their life: “Will they be very noticeable?” “What do I do when I go out to dinner?” “Can I take pictures without them showing?” “Will I be able to speak normally?” “Do you know other people who have worn them without any problems?” or “What will my smile look like at the end?”

The Influence\-style patient may accept the plan quickly because they feel involved and trust the clinician, but that initial ease should not be mistaken for certainty that they have taken in all the instructions. A pleasant conversation can leave both people feeling that everything is perfectly clear, while some important information may have faded into the background.

With this patient, it helps to connect our instructions to their actual experience and the result they want, using simple language, examples they can easily picture, and clear goals. We can involve them, show them photographs, let them see their progress, and use the personal connection as a resource, without letting that connection take the place of clarity.

If we need to explain why wearing aligners consistently is important, for example, we might say:

#dialogo[#emph[“The most important thing you can do to achieve the smile we’ve planned is to wear your aligners consistently. If you wear them less than prescribed, your teeth may start to fall behind the planned sequence, and we may have to stop and replan part of your treatment.”];]

With an Influence\-style patient, it is especially useful to make sure their enthusiasm has turned into a concrete understanding. At the end of the explanation, we can ask them to summarize the key points or give them just a few very clear instructions, rather than overwhelming them with details they probably will not remember. Otherwise, we risk mistaking a good relationship for good communication. They are two different things.

#sezione[The Steadiness\-Style Patient]

The Steadiness\-style patient tends to seek security, continuity, and harmony. They generally do not like pressure, sudden changes, or situations in which they feel they have to make a decision too quickly.

They may be the patient who listens attentively, rarely interrupts, and seems extremely cooperative during the appointment. They are often kind, patient, and respectful of the clinician’s instructions, but that very willingness can lead us to overestimate how convinced or comfortable they really are with what we are proposing.

When they have a concern, they may not voice an objection right away. They may nod, thank us, and leave the office without expressing a worry that surfaces only later, perhaps in a call to the front desk or a conversation with a family member.

With this type of patient, it helps to slow the pace of the conversation, explain what will happen next, and reduce uncertainty. A statement such as:

#dialogo[#emph[“You don’t have to decide anything today. I’ll explain the options and the steps involved in treatment. Then you can take the time you need to think it over, and if any questions come up, we can talk through them together.”];]

can be much more effective than asking for an immediate decision.

During treatment, it is important to create space for patients to tell us about things that are not going well, too. Questions like “Everything going okay?” make it easy to give a positive answer. It may be more useful to ask, “What was the hardest thing to manage this week?” or “Was any part of the instructions I gave you especially difficult to follow?”

That way, we are not suggesting that the patient has done something wrong; we are making it normal to have encountered a difficulty.

With a Steadiness\-style patient, reassurance must not turn into minimizing concerns. If a particular condition is essential to treatment, we need to communicate it clearly, but without creating unnecessary pressure. Steadiness does not mean having no rules; it means making those rules understandable and predictable.

#sezione[The Conscientiousness\-Style Patient]

The Conscientiousness\-style patient tends to seek precision, logic, and consistency. They want to understand the reasons behind decisions and may ask very detailed questions before they feel sufficiently confident.

They are often the patient who arrives for the first appointment having read extensively, brings previous X\-rays or records, compares the information they receive, and wants to know not only what we will do, but why we chose that particular approach.

Their questions may be extremely specific: “How predictable is this movement?” “Why are you using this attachment?” “How likely is it that I’ll need a refinement?” “How can we know the tooth will move exactly as the simulation shows?”

It is natural to feel tested by these questions, but often that is not the patient’s intent. A Conscientiousness\-style patient uses information to reduce uncertainty. The vaguer our answers seem, the more questions they will ask.

With this patient, then, it helps to make our clinical reasoning clear and distinguish what we know from what we cannot predict with certainty:

#dialogo[#emph[“These movements are the most predictable, while these teeth have a greater chance of needing a refinement. That’s why we’ll monitor certain specific measurements during treatment.”];]

An answer like this may be more reassuring than an absolute promise, because it does not try to erase uncertainty artificially. Instead, it shows that we know where uncertainty may arise and how we intend to manage it. If the patient asks:

#dialogo[#emph[“How can you guarantee that the result will look exactly like the simulation?”];]

a sales\-oriented answer would be: #emph[“Don’t worry, that’s exactly how it will turn out.”]; A more accurate and probably more effective answer might be:

#dialogo[#emph[“The simulation shows the planned result, not a mathematical guarantee. Biology and how consistently you follow our instructions can affect how your teeth respond. Our job is to monitor progress and intervene when the actual tooth movement differs from what we planned.”];]

With a Conscientiousness\-style patient, being honest about limitations increases our credibility. If we do not know the answer to a very specific question, it is better to say so and check than to improvise. We should therefore avoid approximate answers, absolute promises, unverifiable data, and irritation at their questions. Precision in communication is especially important because this patient tends to remember what was said and compare it with what happens.

#sezione[A Map, Not a Label]

At this point, it might seem that all we need to do is observe a patient for a few minutes, assign them a letter, and automatically apply a communication style. But that is not how it works.

DISC should not become a new form of stereotyping. A patient who asks a lot of questions is not necessarily a Conscientiousness\-style patient: they may simply be frightened. Someone who wants to decide quickly is not always a Dominance\-style patient: they may be short on time or may already have had other consultations. A person who talks a lot is not necessarily an Influence\-style patient, and someone who stays quiet does not automatically fit the Steadiness profile.

The four styles should be used as working hypotheses, not diagnoses. We can begin a conversation with one approach and adjust it as we observe the patient’s response. If we give them a summary and they keep asking for details, they probably want a more thorough explanation; if we begin with a long explanation and notice impatience, we may need to get to the conclusions more quickly.

Effective communication, then, is not a fixed script but a continuous process of adaptation: DISC had taught me to explain the same concept in different ways, but it had also shown me its limitations. A person’s communication style does not let us predict how consistently they will follow instructions. A Dominance\-style patient may follow instructions rigorously or resist the rules; a Conscientiousness\-style patient may be impeccable or use every detail to shift responsibility away from themselves.

For this reason, DISC will remain with us in the pages that follow, but it will remain what it should be: a map for communication, not a way to classify patients. It will help us choose our words, the level of detail, and the pace of the conversation; our starting point will always be the behavior we observe.

We can learn to communicate better, but no personality model can tell us for certain what a patient will do after they leave the office with a box of aligners in hand and we are not there to watch them.

