#capitolo(numero: "3", etichetta: [Chapter 3], titolo: [Is the Patient Lying?], epigrafe: [“The confidence that people have in their beliefs is not a measure of the quality of the evidence but of the coherence of the story the mind has managed to construct.”], fonte: [Daniel Kahneman])

#dialogo[#emph[“If my patient tells me they always wear their aligners, why isn’t treatment progressing as planned?”];]

I asked myself that question many times during follow\-up visits. The patient assured me they had followed the instructions, but what I observed clinically told a different story. For a long time, my first reaction in these situations was to look for a problem with the treatment. As I have already described, only over the years did I begin to consider another possibility: that part of the explanation might lie in the patient’s behavior as well as in the biomechanics.

I still remember very clearly one of the cases that helped change my perspective.

Laura was about thirty. She was a lawyer, a mother, and, to all appearances, a very conscientious patient. We had begun treatment together with the goal of correcting a Class II malocclusion through maxillary distalization and the use of orthodontic elastics.

During treatment, the aligners were working well: the anterior crowding was resolving, and the fit was excellent. The Class II malocclusion, however, was not improving. At every follow\-up visit, I asked Laura if she was wearing her orthodontic elastics, and she answered: #emph[“Yes, absolutely. All the time.”];

For almost two years, we saw no significant correction of the Class II malocclusion. I kept looking for the explanation in the treatment: I reviewed the biomechanics, modified the treatment plan, and ended up doing four refinements. Meanwhile, the upper incisors kept moving forward. Then, at a follow\-up visit, I found that Laura had finally reached Class I. I asked her what had changed. She told me she was getting married in nine months and wanted to finish treatment before the wedding. That was why she had decided to wear her orthodontic elastics #emph[for real];. Only then did she explain that until that point she had worn them mostly at night and very little during the day.

For me, that admission was more important than the correction of the Class II malocclusion. I had spent a long time looking for a biomechanical explanation for a problem that depended, in large part, on Laura’s behavior. She knew my instructions and had always assured me she was following them. That was when I began to ask myself a simple but uncomfortable question:

#strong[Had Laura lied to me?];

At first glance, it seemed so. Yet calling it a lie did not help me understand what had happened. Why had a patient who knew the instructions given me, visit after visit, an answer so different from her actual habits?

She might have chosen not to tell me the truth. But there might also have been something I had not considered about the way she remembered and described her habits. From then on, I began to ask myself: #strong[why might what a patient reports differ from what they do?];

If we immediately assume the patient is lying, we will look for a way to prove them wrong. If instead we try to understand how they arrived at that answer, we have a better chance of finding out what is really happening and helping them change. In the pages that follow, we will explore some of the mechanisms that can create this gap between behavior and the account of it. They often operate at the same time: each may help explain part of what we observe.

#sezione[Reconstructive Memory]

We tend to imagine memory as a vast archive where events are stored in an orderly way, ready to be retrieved when needed. But research tells us it does not work that way. Remembering does not mean opening a drawer and pulling out a perfect copy of the past. It means reconstructing the past from fragments of experience, prior knowledge, expectations, and personal meanings.

If someone asked us how many hours we slept each night over the past month, we would be unlikely to answer precisely. We would probably remember a few particular nights and use those to estimate an average.

The same thing can happen when we ask a patient how many hours they wore their aligners in the preceding weeks. Putting them in and taking them out are actions repeated every day: it is hard to remember exactly how long each interval lasted. The patient may therefore give us an inaccurate estimate without any intention of deceiving us.

Frederic Bartlett described remembering as a reconstructive process. Later, Elizabeth Loftus’s studies showed that information received after an event can influence how it is remembered. Daniel Schacter described several memory distortions as aspects of how memory normally works.

When we ask a patient to tell us how they behaved in the preceding weeks, then, we are gathering a reconstruction, not an exact account of every day. That does not automatically make their answer false. It makes it human.

#sezione[Cognitive Dissonance]

Reconstructive memory helps us understand why a patient might remember their behavior inaccurately. But there are situations in which that explanation alone seems insufficient. The patient is sitting across from us. Some tooth movements have not occurred as planned. We ask how things are going with the aligners, and the answer comes without hesitation:

#dialogo[#emph[“I wore them all the time.”];]

At moments like these, it is natural to wonder whether the patient is trying to convince us or, at least in part, themselves. That question brings us to #strong[cognitive dissonance];, described by Leon Festinger in 1957. We tend to seek consistency between how we see ourselves and what we do. When the two conflict, we may feel tension and look for a way to reduce it.

Imagine a patient who considers themselves conscientious, reliable, and cooperative. During treatment, however, they forget to wear their aligners several times, spend too long eating, leave them out during dinner with friends, or decide not to wear them for an entire evening. They then have to reconcile two thoughts: #emph[I am a responsible person]; and #emph[on some occasions I did not follow the instructions];. They may acknowledge those occasions and decide to change their behavior. Or they may downplay their importance in their own mind:

#dialogo[#emph[“After all, I wore them almost all the time.” Or “It was just one evening.”];]

The exceptions thus become part of an account that allows the patient to continue seeing themselves as cooperative. They may not do this consciously: when they speak to us, they might sincerely believe they followed the instructions much more closely than they actually did.

#sezione[Social Desirability Bias]

There is another factor to consider. The patient is not merely trying to remember what they did: they are talking to us, and they know what answer we expect. #strong[Social desirability bias]; is the tendency to present ourselves favorably to others, especially when a question concerns behavior for which we might feel judged.

The patient knows the instructions they were given. They know how many hours they should wear their aligners and that orthodontic elastics are part of the treatment. Admitting they have not followed those instructions can be uncomfortable. So when we ask how things are going, the desire to seem like a good patient can influence their answer. They do not need to make up a story. A small adjustment may be enough: #emph[“pretty often”]; becomes #emph[“almost all the time,”]; and #emph[“almost all the time”]; becomes #emph[“all the time.”];

This is a well\-known phenomenon in studies that rely on people’s reports of their own behavior. For us, it has a practical consequence: a question like #emph[“You’re wearing your aligners, right?”]; already suggests the expected answer and can make it harder for the patient to tell us what is really happening.

#sezione[Self\-Serving Bias]

Another mechanism concerns how we explain outcomes to ourselves. #strong[Self\-serving bias]; is the tendency to attribute successes to our own actions and failures to external causes.

When treatment is going well, the patient may think: #emph[“I’ve done a good job following the instructions.”]; When a planned tooth movement does not occur, they may instead think: #emph[“These aligners aren’t working.”];

In this case, what changes is not necessarily our memory of what we did, but how we interpret what happened. Attributing a difficulty to an external cause can protect us from the idea that we contributed to the problem. Of course, sometimes the cause really does lie in the treatment: a movement may have been planned poorly, an attachment may be inadequate, or the biological response may differ from what was expected. This mechanism does not, therefore, give us license to automatically blame the patient.

It does, however, help me look again at what happened in cases like Laura’s. I tended to look for an error in my treatment plan; she may have looked for one in the aligners. So I modified the treatment, while she may have remained convinced that she was following the instructions as asked.

#sezione[Confirmation Bias]

In 1960, Peter Wason described another phenomenon that would become central to the study of human reasoning: #strong[confirmation bias];, the tendency to seek out and interpret information that fits what we already believe.

Patients, too, may view their treatment through this filter. Someone convinced they have followed the treatment plan extremely closely will tend to give more weight to the days they wore their aligners exactly as instructed, the sacrifice they made during a dinner, or the times they put them back in immediately after lunch. The weekend they left them on the nightstand, the long Sunday lunch, or the vacation when their schedule became less strict will figure much less prominently.

The opposite can also happen. A patient convinced that treatment is not working will tend to focus on the tooth that is still rotated or the small imperfection that remains, while all the tooth movements already completed gradually fade into the background.

The initial belief thus becomes the filter through which we interpret what happens. And of course, this applies to us as clinicians, too. If we are convinced the problem is biomechanical, we will look mainly for evidence that supports that explanation. If we are convinced the patient is not following the treatment plan, we run the opposite risk.

Understanding confirmation bias, then, is not about proving the patient wrong. It is also about reminding ourselves how easily we could be wrong.

#sezione[Unrealistic Optimism and Temporal Discounting]

Then there is a question every orthodontist has asked at least once: why does a patient keep acting against their own interests despite knowing the rules of treatment perfectly well? #strong[Unrealistic optimism]; can help us understand part of the answer.

In the 1980s, Neil Weinstein observed that we tend to consider some negative events less likely to happen to us than to others and, conversely, to see positive events as relatively more likely. In other words, we know the risk, but it tends to feel more remote when it concerns us.

The patient knows that wearing their aligners less than required can slow treatment, but thinks a few hours less will not really make a difference in their case. They know that not wearing their orthodontic elastics can prevent certain tooth movements, but imagine they will still be able to catch up. They know retention is important, but consider it unlikely that their own teeth will shift significantly. So it is not necessarily that the patient has failed to understand the information: they may have understood it perfectly. What changes is the weight the patient gives to the possible consequence.

Another mechanism can come into play here, one well described in studies of #strong[temporal discounting];, or the devaluation of future outcomes: we tend to give greater weight to immediate costs and benefits than to consequences farther in the future. For the patient, this means continually weighing a small discomfort now against a future benefit. Taking out their aligners for an evening, not putting them back in immediately after dinner, or skipping their orthodontic elastics brings a small but immediate benefit; a possible delay in treatment, by contrast, seems distant, uncertain, and hard to perceive at the moment the decision is made.

It is the interaction between these two mechanisms that gives a future consequence so little weight: not only is it far away, but the patient may also be convinced that it probably will not happen to them.

This point will be especially important when we begin developing the protocols, because it will force us to distinguish between informing the patient of a consequence and making that consequence truly matter when they have to make a decision.

#sezione[So, Is the Patient Lying?]

At this point, we can return to the question we started with: #strong[is the patient lying?]; Sometimes, yes. It would be naive to build an entire chapter around cognitive mechanisms to avoid that possibility. Some people know perfectly well that they have not followed the instructions and deliberately choose to tell us otherwise, perhaps to avoid a reprimand, protect their image, or avoid taking responsibility. But it would be just as naive to stop there.

Between objective reality and the account we give of it lies a much more complex space, where memory reconstructs what happened; dissonance helps us preserve a consistent image of ourselves; social desirability bias changes what we are willing to admit to others; self\-serving bias shapes how we assign responsibility; confirmation bias favors information that best fits what we already believe; and unrealistic optimism and temporal discounting can make the future consequences of our decisions seem less relevant. These mechanisms do not automatically turn a false answer into a true one, but they help us understand why the gap between actual and reported behavior can be much more complex than a simple choice between honesty and lying.

And it is precisely this distinction that changes how we approach the patient. If our goal is to establish who is right, every inconsistency becomes the start of a confrontation: we show the patient what we observe, the patient defends what they remember, we press the point, and they dig in. The harder we try to prove that they are not telling the truth, the more we risk pushing them to defend the account they have just given. Our goal, though, is not to get a confession, but to get them to behave differently from that point forward.

That is probably the most important implication of everything we have seen in this chapter. When a patient says they have worn their aligners as instructed but the clinical picture suggests otherwise, we do not necessarily need to decide whether they are lying, remembering inaccurately, or unconsciously protecting their self\-image. Instead, we need to figure out how to bring the conversation back to what will happen #strong[from today on];.

Had Laura told me the truth in the end? Had she deliberately lied to me for months, or was she genuinely convinced that the way she wore her orthodontic elastics was close enough to what I had asked of her? I do not know, and years later I realized that perhaps it is not even the most important question. What I do know is that when the wedding turned a distant, abstract consequence into a concrete, near\-term goal that mattered to her personally, her behavior changed. The orthodontic elastics were the same, the biomechanics were the same, and I was the same orthodontist: #strong[what had changed was what following the treatment plan meant to her.];

And that is where understanding the patient’s mind stops being merely interesting and starts becoming clinically useful. When adherence breaks down, the consequences extend beyond the clinical result: they mean more time, more appointments, more refinements, and more resources spent to achieve the same result. In the next chapter, then, we will look at poor adherence from another, much more concrete perspective: #strong[what is its real cost?];

