#capitolo(numero: "4", etichetta: [Chapter 4], titolo: [The Hidden Cost of Poor Patient Adherence], epigrafe: [“Beware of little expenses; a small leak will sink a great ship.”], fonte: [Benjamin Franklin])

Some patients I remember because of the complexity of their malocclusion, others because the result was particularly difficult to achieve, and still others because, when I think back on their treatment, the first thing that comes to mind is not their teeth but the time it took. Not necessarily because the treatment was long in absolute terms: some complex cases take time, and that is part of orthodontics. I mean the very different feeling you get when you reach the end, look back, and realize that a considerable amount of that time would not have been necessary.

A new scan because the aligners had stopped fitting properly, a new treatment plan, another series of aligners, an unplanned appointment to deliver them, and a few extra checkups. Then perhaps another scan, because the new sequence had not produced what we expected, either. Meanwhile, more months had passed, and the treatment, which on paper should have been nearing completion, continued to take up space on the practice schedule.

On their own, these steps seem almost harmless. A scan takes only a few minutes, an extra checkup can be squeezed in between two appointments, preparing a new treatment plan is part of our job, and when we use packages that allow us to request additional series of aligners for a set period, even producing new trays can seem to have no real cost. Only when we start adding it all up does the picture change: how many hours of chair time did we spend on that patient beyond what we had planned? How many times did we reopen the case, revise the prescription, review a new digital treatment setup, or add an appointment that should not have been needed? And above all, how much of that extra work was truly unavoidable, and how much had become necessary because the patient had not followed the treatment plan?

Until then, I had viewed the problem mainly through individual clinical episodes. I was seeing patients whose aligners lost tracking, treatments that dragged on, and refinements that piled up, but I had not yet tried to look at the phenomenon as a whole. In the previous chapter, we saw why what a person remembers and reports about their behavior may not match exactly what actually happened; at this point, though, the question changes. If we stop relying solely on what patients tell us and start measuring what they actually do, #strong[how large is that gap?];

#sezione[How Closely Do Our Patients Really Follow the Treatment Plan?]

In orthodontics, we are used to the word #emph[compliance];, although in recent years the literature has tended to favor #emph[adherence];, a less paternalistic term describing the extent to which a patient’s behavior matches the instructions they have received.

The problem is that there is still no universally accepted definition of orthodontic adherence. One of the most comprehensive reviews on the subject, published in 2024 by Van der Bie and colleagues, identified 3,284 papers and included 60 in the final analysis. The authors showed how fragmented the literature is and how difficult it is to compare studies that measure different behaviors using different tools. In practice, though, three broad areas emerge through which we can assess adherence: wearing appliances as instructed, keeping appointments, and maintaining adequate oral hygiene during treatment.

This means it probably does not make much sense to divide patients simply into those who follow instructions and those who do not. A patient may wear their aligners very consistently but forget their orthodontic elastics, may be meticulous about oral hygiene but miss appointments, or may start treatment enthusiastically and gradually become less consistent after many months. Adherence, then, is not an unchanging personal trait but a set of behaviors that can change over the course of treatment.

For a very long time, the simplest way to find out how much a patient wore a removable appliance was to ask them: #emph[“How many hours do you wear it?”];, #emph[“Do you wear it every night?”];, #emph[“Are you wearing your elastics?”]; Today, we can compare those answers with data from systems that objectively record wear time, and when we do, a gap emerges that deserves our attention.

In 2017, Al\-Moghrabi and colleagues published a systematic review and meta\-analysis of compliance with removable orthodontic appliances, comparing the wear time prescribed by the orthodontist, the time reported by the patient, and the time recorded objectively. The results showed a fairly clear pattern: actual adherence fell short of what was prescribed, and patients tended to overestimate their wear time. In the six studies that compared prescribed wear time with objectively recorded wear time, patients wore the orthodontic appliance an average of #strong[5.81 fewer hours per day]; than prescribed. Even more relevant to our discussion was the gap between what patients reported and what the devices recorded: reported wear time was #strong[5.02 hours per day higher];.

Five hours.

A difference that, repeated every day for weeks or months, completely changes the conditions under which orthodontic tooth movement is supposed to take place. Van der Bie’s 2024 review confirms how difficult it is to assess adherence based solely on what patients report and emphasizes that objective measurements are more reliable when available.

The situation becomes even more complicated during adolescence. A review published in 2025 on adolescents’ adherence to removable orthodontic appliances found considerable variation in adherence and identified numerous factors that can affect it, including motivation, forgetfulness, discomfort, family support, and social pressures. In one of the included studies, about 40% of adolescents wore their vacuum\-formed retainer for fewer than 18 hours a day during a phase when they were supposed to wear it for 22 hours. The point is not to regard adolescents as less reliable patients, but to recognize how difficult it is to ask someone to maintain the same behavior every day, for months or even years. On paper, #emph[“wear it every night”]; seems like a very simple instruction; in real life, it is a much more demanding request than those four words suggest.

With aligners, too, the ability to measure wear time objectively lets us compare what we prescribe with what patients actually do. In 2026, Sabbagh and colleagues published a randomized controlled study in which wear time was recorded by microsensors built into the aligners. Forty\-three adult patients, who had been instructed to wear their aligners for twenty hours a day, were divided into two groups: patients in the first group did not know the data collected would be used to assess their compliance, while those in the second group were explicitly informed of this.

Of the participants, #strong[67.5% wore their aligners for between ten and twenty hours a day];, below the prescribed twenty hours. But perhaps even more interesting was the effect of monitoring: knowing that their wear time was being tracked increased patients’ average aligner wear time by #strong[4.41 hours per day];. The appliance, the instructions, and the treatment were the same in both groups; the only difference was whether patients knew their behavior would be measured.

This finding introduces a principle we will return to in the protocols later: fully understanding an instruction does not necessarily mean following it. The context in which we ask patients to do something, how we check whether they do it, and how we make the consequences visible can all affect what they actually do.

#sottosezione[When Adherence Changes the Treatment]

At this point, it would be easy to conclude that if a patient follows the treatment plan less closely, treatment inevitably takes longer. The relationship, though, is not so simple, because the length of orthodontic treatment depends on the complexity of the malocclusion, the individual’s biological response, the planned tooth movements, treatment choices, treatment planning, and many other variables unrelated to patient behavior. Van der Bie’s review also emphasizes that the relationship between the degree of adherence and treatment outcomes is not yet fully understood.

There is, however, evidence that certain patient behaviors are associated with treatment duration. Beckwith and colleagues analyzed 140 treatments completed at five orthodontic practices, examining thirty\-one variables. Six factors together explained more than half of the observed variation in treatment duration, and three of them were related to patient behavior: missed appointments, brackets or bands that had to be replaced, and oral hygiene problems. A few years later, Skidmore and colleagues analyzed 366 patients treated with fixed appliances and again found an association between longer treatment and behaviors such as not wearing orthodontic elastics enough, inadequate oral hygiene, and broken brackets.

These studies were conducted predominantly on fixed appliances, and it would be wrong to apply their findings automatically to aligner treatment. The principle that emerges remains important, though: treatment duration depends not only on what we plan and do, but also on what patients do during the weeks between appointments. #strong[In aligner treatment, this matters particularly because daily wear time is one of the conditions on which we build the entire treatment plan.]; And this is where we encounter something extremely familiar to anyone who works with aligners every day: #strong[refinements.];

#sezione[Not All Refinements Are the Same]

Before linking refinements to adherence, we need to make a distinction: not every refinement results from poor adherence, and needing a new series of aligners does not necessarily mean treatment has failed. Some tooth movements are less predictable, patients’ biological responses differ, and biomechanical limitations, treatment\-planning problems, and many other clinical factors can make a refinement entirely necessary even for a patient who follows the plan impeccably. We are interested in a much more specific group: #strong[avoidable refinements];, particularly those made necessary because the patient’s actual behavior differed from what we had based the treatment plan on.

This distinction is essential, especially given how common refinements are regardless of their cause. Kravitz and colleagues analyzed 500 patients treated with Invisalign at two orthodontic practices and found an average of #strong[2.5 refinement scans per patient];. Only 6% completed treatment without an additional scan, while the actual average treatment duration was 22.8 months, 5.1 months longer than initially projected. In addition, 17.2% of patients had to switch to fixed appliances to complete treatment.

These numbers #strong[do not tell us how many of those refinements resulted from poor adherence];, because the study was not designed to determine that. They do, however, show how wide the gap can be between the treatment we envision at the outset and what is actually needed to complete it. In 2025, an international consensus on the indications and limitations of aligner treatment called attention to this very problem: more than 10% of patients in Kravitz’s study required five or more refinements, increasing the time and resources needed to complete treatment.

Another study helps us understand why the number of refinements deserves attention. Abu Arqub and colleagues analyzed 355 patients treated with aligners, examining the relationship between initial complexity, number of refinements, treatment duration, and outcome. The greatest improvement occurred with the #strong[first refinement];. Among patients who had not yet reached the improvement threshold set by the authors, the likelihood of reaching it decreased progressively as the number of refinements increased; in the sample analyzed, after more than three refinements, no additional patients were observed reaching that improvement threshold for the first time.

It is important not to turn this finding into a claim the study does not make. #strong[It does not mean that treatment becomes pointless after three refinements, and it does not mean a fourth refinement should be avoided.]; A complex case may require further stages that are entirely justified. The finding does, however, suggest something extremely interesting: more refinements do not guarantee a proportional clinical benefit, and this is precisely where the clinical issue begins to intersect with the economic one.

#sottosezione[The Hidden Cost of Refinements]

For me, this changes how I think about a phrase we can say very easily in day\-to\-day practice: #emph[“Let’s do another scan.”]; Clinically, it may seem like a relatively small decision; from an operational and financial standpoint, though, it is anything but.

Many aligner systems offer packages that, after the initial purchase of treatment, let us request additional series for a set period without paying a new production fee each time. This model is extremely convenient, especially for complex treatments, but that very feature can unconsciously change how we perceive the cost of a refinement. If no new invoice arrives from the company after another scan, it is easy to feel that the new series is essentially free. Patients may see it the same way: if they need more aligners, more aligners will simply arrive.

That is where the misconception begins.

The price of the new series is only part of what is involved when we decide to do a refinement. We need to understand why the aligners lost tracking, perform a new scan, update the records when necessary, reconsider the prescription, review and approve the new digital treatment setup, arrange delivery of the aligners, and schedule additional checkups. Much of this cost does not appear on any invoice, because it takes the form of the most valuable and limited resource we have in our practices: #strong[time];.

When we talk about financial cost, though, we need to return to the earlier distinction. A clinically necessary refinement is part of the work required to complete treatment; an avoidable refinement, by contrast, requires resources we could have saved. The time spent may not appear as a separate line item in the practice’s accounts, and we will not receive an invoice at the end of the month to remind us of it, but that does not mean it has no cost.

An hour spent getting a treatment back on track could be an hour in which we might have conducted an initial consultation, started a new case, or performed a scheduled procedure. When the same thing happens with dozens of patients, those hours begin to add up. If we assumed, for example, that an avoidable refinement required even just one additional hour of clinical and administrative resources in total, #strong[fifty refinements would mean fifty hours of work];; if each required two hours, that would become one hundred.

It would be wrong, though, to assign those hours a universal monetary value. A practice that generates 300 euros per hour will not have the same opportunity cost as one that generates 1,000 euros per hour, just as a scan handled entirely by staff will have a different impact from a procedure that directly involves the orthodontist. The principle, though, does not change: #strong[every avoidable refinement consumes some of the practice’s capacity that can no longer be used for something else.];

When I searched the literature for a figure that would establish the average cost of an avoidable refinement, I could not find one, and a universal figure probably could not exist, because it depends on how the practice is organized, staffing costs, the aligner system used, the time spent on treatment planning, and the value of clinical time. The literature does, however, allow us to trace the chain that leads to the problem: actual adherence may be lower than both prescribed and reported adherence; some patient behaviors are associated with longer treatments; and refinements are extremely common in aligner treatment, while their benefit does not necessarily grow in proportion to their number. What no article can tell us is #strong[how much all this costs in our own practices: we have to calculate that ourselves.];

The point, then, is not to determine which pricing model is best, but to understand a much more important distinction: #strong[the price of a new series of aligners and the cost of managing that new phase of treatment are not the same thing.]; The first may already be included in the package purchased; the second remains every time the practice has to step in again.

#sezione[The Real Price of Poor Adherence]

At this point, poor adherence takes on a different meaning. It is no longer simply a matter of a patient not following our instructions, but a variable capable of changing the conditions on which we built the treatment plan. If patients wear their aligners less than necessary, do not wear their orthodontic elastics as instructed, or repeatedly miss appointments, the plan we designed remains the same, but the conditions under which it is supposed to work do not.

When this leads to a loss of tracking, incomplete tooth movements, or the need to redesign the treatment, part of the treatment has to be made up. The number of appointments, the time spent with the patient, the overall treatment duration, and the resources needed to achieve a result we had planned to achieve sooner and under different conditions all increase. But perhaps the most important point to emerge from this chapter is not about cost: it is about #strong[predictability];.

If we know that actual adherence may fall short of what we prescribe, that what patients report may overstate what they actually do, and that even knowing they are being monitored can change aligner wear time by several hours a day, it becomes difficult to keep treating adherence as something we can simply prescribe and then take for granted. This does not mean relieving patients of responsibility, but recognizing that adherence is a #strong[clinical variable]; and, like every variable that can affect the outcome, it must be considered, observed, and managed.

That shift in perspective changed the question I ask myself when a patient is not following the treatment plan. No longer just #emph[“Why aren’t they doing what I told them?”]; but #emph[“If I know this behavior can occur, what can I do to spot it sooner, make it less likely, and intervene when it does?”];

I cannot know for certain how many hours a patient will wear their aligners once they leave my office, nor can I be there when they have to decide whether to put on their orthodontic elastics, switch to the next aligner, or follow a particular instruction. But I can decide how to prepare them for those choices, how to check what is happening during treatment, and, above all, how to intervene when I recognize one of the behaviors I have seen recur again and again over the years.

And this is where the problem of adherence finally meets the central theme of this book: recognizing recurring behaviors allows us to prepare and replace improvisation with a method. #strong[That idea gave rise to the protocols we will look at in the coming chapters.];

