#capitolo(numero: "5", etichetta: [Chapter 5], titolo: [Introducing the Protocols], epigrafe: [“There is always a well\-known solution to every human problem—neat, plausible, and wrong.”], fonte: [H. L. Mencken])

Sooner or later, we will see another patient who forgets their orthodontic elastics, wears their aligners less than they need to, or insists they followed every instruction while the course of treatment tells a different story.

The question is no longer whether it will happen, but whether we will improvise again when it does. Every recurring behavior gives us a chance to prepare a response that the whole practice can recognize, assess, and follow: a protocol.

For many years, though, I kept improvising. When something was not working, I searched for the right words, tried to explain it better, added information, showed photographs, stressed the consequences, or changed my approach completely depending on the person in front of me. Sometimes it worked very well, sometimes decidedly less so; with some patients I could bring about an immediate change, with others I ended up in endless conversations, and with still others I left the appointment feeling that I had explained the problem perfectly without managing to change a thing. At the next appointment, if the behavior happened again, I essentially started from scratch.

Over time, I realized there was something paradoxical about working this way. In orthodontics, we use protocols for practically everything: we standardize record collection, diagnosis, planning, clinical procedures, follow\-up visits, and many of our biomechanical decisions because we know that when a situation tends to recur, deciding in advance how to handle it reduces variability and increases predictability. Yet when the variable that deviates from the plan is the patient’s behavior, we still very often react case by case, even though these situations, too, recur with surprising regularity.

The names, ages, malocclusions, and excuses change, but the patterns underlying many adherence problems are far fewer than we might think. That was probably the most important discovery: #strong[problematic behaviors are numerous, but they are not endless and, above all, they are not entirely unpredictable];. If we learn to recognize the patterns that tend to recur, our response, too, can stop being improvised and start being planned.

That need gave rise to the protocols.

#sezione[Before the Protocols: Planning for Adherence]

In Chapter 2, we saw that communicating effectively means more than knowing what to say. It also means adapting our language, pace, and amount of information to the person in front of us. This can help us make an explanation clearer, more credible, and more meaningful. Even perfectly tailored communication has a limit, though: it can help a patient understand, but it cannot guarantee what they will do.

A patient may understand perfectly well how important it is to wear their aligners, wear their orthodontic elastics, or follow the retention plan and still do something different a few hours later. They may even leave the office sincerely convinced that, from then on, they will do exactly what we asked, only to slip gradually back into their old habits when everyday life takes over. #strong[Informing someone and changing their behavior are not the same thing.];

Psychologists have long studied this gap between intention and behavior. Among the models most relevant to our discussion are the #emph[implementation intentions]; studied by Peter Gollwitzer, in which a general intention becomes a much more concrete plan linking a particular situation to a specific action: not simply #emph[“I need to remember to do this,”]; but #emph[“When this situation comes up, I’ll take this action.”]; It is a particularly useful distinction in orthodontics, because many adherence problems arise not from a lack of intention to follow instructions, but from the difficulty of turning that intention into a sufficiently consistent daily routine.

That, though, is only part of the problem. Making a behavior easier to carry out can help when patients want to follow the plan but struggle to turn that intention into a habit; in other situations, we must first make the requested behavior matter enough to patients that they genuinely decide to change it. This brings us to another important element in how I developed my protocols: #strong[persuasion];.

Oddly enough, this was not a new subject for me at all. Long before I became an orthodontist, I was fascinated by magic, sleight of hand, mentalism, and hypnosis. I wanted to understand why two people could watch the same scene and perceive different things, how attention could be directed toward certain details, and how much the context in which information is presented could change its interpretation. Over the years, that curiosity also led me to study communication and persuasion more deeply, at first out of personal interest and, only much later, to wonder whether some of those principles might have practical applications in my relationships with patients, too.

The word #emph[persuasion]; can make us uneasy when used in connection with medicine, as though it necessarily implied a desire to convince someone to do something against their own interests. But I believe the problem is not persuasion itself; it is the purpose for which it is used. Every communication changes the listener’s perception to some degree: choosing one word instead of another, presenting a consequence in terms of what can be gained or lost, showing a photograph, using a number, or asking a question already creates a frame through which the other person will interpret what we are saying.

Persuasion becomes ethically problematic when it is used to conceal information, distort facts, exploit a vulnerability, or steer someone toward a choice that benefits us but not them. It can mean something entirely different when it is used to help a patient maintain a behavior they themselves have agreed is necessary to achieve the treatment goal they chose. If someone freely decides to begin aligner treatment and knows that the outcome will also depend on their own behavior, helping them wear their aligners for the required amount of time, wear their orthodontic elastics, or follow their retention plan correctly does not take away their freedom. It means recognizing the enormous gap between knowing what we should do and actually managing to do it every day—a gap filled with habits, lapses of memory, immediate rewards, resistance, and rationalizations.

That is precisely the gap the protocols must address.

#sezione[The Three Motivators]

When I began thinking this way, I realized I did not need to come up with a different way to convince each patient to follow instructions every time. I first had to understand #strong[what really mattered to the person in front of me];, because not everyone wants the same things with the same intensity and, above all, not everyone gives the consequences of a behavior the same weight.

Over time, I began to recognize three major motivators that now recur throughout my protocols: #strong[time, the outcome, and the investment];. These are not three persuasive techniques, but three different ways a consequence can become genuinely meaningful to a patient.

The first motivator is #strong[time];, a limited resource that is easy to understand and easy to connect directly to the consequences of our actions. In orthodontics, it has another distinctive feature: patients often perceive the length of treatment as something that depends mainly on the clinician, when many treatments have a behavioral component that can change it substantially. For some people, finishing six months earlier or six months later makes an enormous difference: there may be a wedding, a trip, a move, college, or simply the desire to be done with aligners at last. In these cases, making the connection between what patients do today and what might happen in the months ahead clear can mean far more than another explanation of the biomechanics.

The second motivator is #strong[the outcome];. Some patients place such a high value on the final result that they will accept a longer course of treatment without much difficulty, as long as it allows them to achieve exactly the goal they want. In these cases, repeatedly talking about additional months may have very little effect. It becomes much more important to connect the requested behavior to the quality of the result they hope to achieve, helping them understand that a particular tooth movement might not be fully achieved or that insufficient adherence might force us to accept a compromise we had not originally planned.

The third motivator is the #strong[investment];, by which I do not mean only money. Orthodontic treatment certainly involves a financial investment, but it also involves months or years of time, appointments, family scheduling, daily attention, sacrifices, and trust. Everything the patient has already devoted to treatment has value, and that investment can become particularly meaningful when a behavior threatens to undermine what has already been accomplished or force us to repeat part of the process.

Time, the outcome, and the investment will play a role in practically every protocol we encounter later, but we will not necessarily use them all at once, and no single motivator is universally better than the others. #strong[The clinical context and the person in front of us]; will suggest which consequence may matter most at that moment. The general principle, then, is this: #strong[communication can influence behavior only when it connects what we ask patients to do with a consequence they consider genuinely important];.

#sezione[The Basic Structure of a Protocol]

When I began putting these principles together, I noticed that, beyond any particular technique, the conversations that worked best tended to follow a fairly consistent structure. It was not a matter of finding especially clever words each time, but of moving through a series of steps in an order that made confrontation less likely and concrete change more likely.

The first step is to #strong[reduce defensiveness]; by presenting the problem through clinical observations, specific questions, and verifiable evidence rather than judgments about the person. The second is to allow patients, when possible, to #strong[save face without continuing the problematic behavior];: we do not necessarily need them to admit they were wrong or that they lied, but we do need them to recognize that something must change. The third is to make #strong[the consequences concrete];, connecting the behavior to the effects it can actually have and choosing, based on the person, which of the three motivators—time, the outcome, or the investment—matters most. The fourth step is to #strong[reduce ambiguity about future behavior];, turning a vague #emph[“you need to follow the instructions more consistently”]; into something specific enough to observe and verify. Finally, we must #strong[make the behavior easier and check whether it works];, reducing the obstacles that can prevent patients from turning intention into action and arranging feedback soon enough to tell whether the change we made is actually working.

This structure should not become a rigid framework. The purpose of a protocol is not to get the same response from every patient, which is impossible, but to make #strong[our response]; more predictable when we face problems we have encountered before. In the pages ahead, we will look at a series of psychological and communication principles we can use to build scripts suited to the clinical situation and the person in front of us. #strong[We will then see them put into practice in the protocols in later chapters.];

#sezione[1\. Reduce Defensiveness]

In difficult conversations, I used to start with what seemed most logical to me: the problem. If a patient was not wearing their orthodontic elastics, I talked about the elastics; if they were not wearing their aligners enough, I talked about the hours; if they were not wearing their retainer, I explained the risk of relapse. From my point of view, this was a perfectly rational approach, but over time I realized that the first words of a conversation could profoundly affect how the patient interpreted everything I said afterward.

If we open an appointment by saying #emph[“You’re not doing what I asked you to do,”]; we have already assigned the roles in the conversation without saying so: we are the accuser, and the patient is the one who has to defend themselves. We may be entirely right from a clinical standpoint, but from that point on, some of the conversation’s energy will go toward assigning responsibility, justifying what happened, or protecting the patient’s self\-image.

If instead we begin by saying #emph[“Let’s look together at how your teeth are responding compared with what we planned”]; or #emph[“One tooth movement isn’t progressing as we expected, and I’d like to figure out what we can do to get it back on track,”]; we have not changed the clinical problem or concealed what we think, but we have created a different context for addressing it.

The request does not begin when we say it aloud. It begins with how we set up the conversation in which we make it. The principle of #strong[framing];, studied by Tversky and Kahneman, describes how the way information or a problem is presented can influence how it is perceived and the decisions that follow, even when the underlying facts remain the same. That is why, when I need to address insufficient adherence, I now try to start with the treatment rather than with blame. This does not mean avoiding a difficult conversation. It means creating conditions that give the conversation a better chance of producing change rather than a defensive response.

The same principle applies when a patient raises an objection. One of the most natural mistakes in clinical communication is to try immediately to correct what we have been told. If patients say something is uncomfortable, we are tempted to explain that it should not be all that uncomfortable; if they say they cannot do something, we start showing them that they actually could; if they tell us they have always worn their aligners, we try to explain that what we are seeing proves otherwise.

The problem is that the moment we try to prove that another person’s perception is wrong, we almost automatically force them to defend it. If a patient says #emph[“I can’t wear my elastics because they’re too uncomfortable”]; and we immediately reply #emph[“That can’t be right. All my other patients wear them,”]; the conversation quickly stops being about the elastics and starts being about whether their experience is legitimate. The patient has to convince us that the discomfort is real, while we try to convince them that it should be tolerable. In the end, we may have argued for ten minutes without getting any closer to the behavior we want to encourage.

If framing concerns the lens through which we present a problem, #strong[reframing]; means trying to change that lens, giving the same fact a different meaning without having to deny it. If part of treatment is uncomfortable, I can acknowledge that it is and then connect that discomfort to the role it plays in reaching the goal. If a behavior takes effort, I can acknowledge that too and help the patient see that effort in a different light.

#strong[An objection that has been acknowledged needs less defending];, and only then can we begin to work on the meaning we give it. This does not mean agreeing with the patient about everything. It means choosing carefully what is worth debating, because our goal is not to win an argument but to create conditions that make the necessary behavior more likely.

#sezione[2\. Save Face Without Continuing the Behavior]

In the chapter on the patient’s mind, we saw how difficult it can be to acknowledge behavior that conflicts with our self\-image. This principle becomes particularly important when we need to address adherence.

Telling someone #emph[“you’re an uncooperative patient”]; turns a behavior into a personal trait. The patient now has to do more than discuss how many hours they wore their aligners: they have to defend their identity as a responsible person. From that point on, anything we say may be perceived as a personal judgment.

That is why I gradually learned to separate the person from the behavior. A patient may be exceptionally punctual for appointments but forget their orthodontic elastics, maintain impeccable oral hygiene but wear their aligners incorrectly, or follow instructions perfectly for a year and then go through a period when keeping up their routine becomes harder. Labeling them broadly as #emph[“nonadherent”]; does not help us understand which behavior we need to change and, more importantly, may increase their need to defend themselves.

When possible, I prefer to do the opposite and start with something the patient is already doing well. If they wear their aligners correctly but forget their orthodontic elastics, I can acknowledge the part of the treatment plan they are following and use that ability as a starting point for the other part. This is not flattery, and we should not credit patients with qualities they do not have. It means recognizing a real behavior and building on it to address what is missing. The implicit message is not #emph[“you’re incapable of following a treatment plan,”]; but #emph[“you’ve already shown you can manage one demanding part of treatment; we need to bring that same consistency to this part, too.”];  #strong[Protecting the person does not mean protecting the behavior we need to change.];

The same approach becomes even more important when what a patient tells us does not match what we observe clinically. We ask, #emph[“Have you been wearing them?”]; and the patient answers, #emph[“Yes.”]; We try to be more specific: #emph[“How many hours?”];  #emph[“All the time, practically all day.”]; At that point, faced with something that does not seem clinically possible, we might be tempted to respond, #emph[“That can’t be right, because otherwise this tooth would have moved.”]; The problem is that, from that moment on, the goal of the conversation changes: instead of looking for a solution to the clinical problem, we try to prove that the patient did not follow instructions, and the patient tries to prove otherwise.

But we do not necessarily need a confession to change the behavior. If what we observe is enough to tell us that something needs to change, we can use that information to decide what to do without forcing the patient to admit they made a mistake or gave us inaccurate information. #strong[The protocol should give us a way out of the choice between proving the patient wrong and accepting their version of events:]; we can recognize that the behavior needs to change without first settling who is right about the past.

This does not mean ignoring what happened or accepting any explanation. It simply means distinguishing the information we need to make a clinical decision from the information we seek only to assign responsibility. Once I have gathered the information I need, I prefer to shift the conversation from the past to the future. We do not always need to establish with absolute precision how many hours a patient wore their aligners three weeks ago, but we certainly need to establish how they should wear them starting tomorrow and how we will check whether the change is working.

#strong[The past helps us make a decision, not win an argument.];

#sezione[3\. Make the Consequences Concrete]

Once we have reduced the patient’s need to defend themselves, we need to make it clear why that behavior must change. This is where the three motivators truly come into play: #strong[time, the outcome, and the investment];.

One of the things that changed how I talk with patients most was realizing that a reason a patient gives can mean far more than one I give them. For years, I tried to explain why following the treatment plan mattered, when it often would have been more useful to pause first and understand what really mattered to the person in front of me. The difference may seem small, but it changes the balance of the conversation: in the first case, I tell the patient why they should want something; in the second, I try to understand what they already want and connect the necessary behavior to that goal.

This principle is close to what the literature on social influence describes in terms of #strong[commitment and consistency];: people tend to prefer behaviors consistent with positions, goals, or commitments they see as their own. This does not mean trapping patients with their own words, but using what they themselves consider important as a reference point for what they do next. If a patient tells me their priority is to finish before their wedding, I do not need to convince them that time matters; they have just told me so. If they explain that they do not care how long treatment takes as long as the result is perfect, emphasizing the duration means using the wrong motivator. If, instead, they feel particularly strongly about #strong[the investment they have already made in treatment];, connecting adherence to the possibility of protecting what they have achieved will probably be more meaningful. #strong[Before trying to change a behavior, then, we need to understand which consequence truly matters to the person in front of us.];

At that point, we need to make it concrete. As orthodontists, we inevitably tend to describe problems using the categories we learned during our training: we see tracking, torque, anchorage, relapse, stability, and movements that have not been expressed, because these are the concepts through which we interpret treatment. Patients, though, do not experience treatment through the same categories. Saying #emph[“this canine isn’t expressing the planned movement correctly”]; may be clinically impeccable, but it does not necessarily create a meaningful picture in the mind of the person listening. Explaining that, if the movement continues to lag behind, we may have to pause the aligner sequence, take a new scan, and add several months to treatment makes the same problem much more concrete. #strong[Time, the outcome, and the investment do not change the clinical reality, but they allow us to translate it into something patients can assess in terms that matter to them.];

This step is particularly important because many orthodontic consequences are far off. A patient who decides not to put their aligners back in after dinner tonight does not immediately see a loss of tracking; one who forgets their orthodontic elastics does not notice that night the movement that will not occur. The benefit of following the treatment plan is often delayed, while the benefit of not doing so is immediate: they can eat more comfortably, they do not have to look for their orthodontic elastics, and they can go to bed without thinking about treatment. Making a consequence concrete therefore means narrowing that gap, mentally connecting today’s behavior to something the patient wants to achieve or avoid tomorrow.

How we present that consequence can also change what it means to the patient. One of the central principles of #emph[prospect theory]; is that people do not weigh gains and losses in a perfectly symmetrical way and that, in many decision\-making contexts, a loss can carry particular psychological weight. We can explain to patients that wearing their orthodontic elastics correctly will allow them to keep moving toward the planned outcome, or help them understand that inconsistent wear risks undermining a correction they have already achieved; we can show them the benefit of finishing on schedule, or make the possibility of losing some of the time they have already invested more visible.

We must use this principle responsibly. Making a consequence more apparent does not necessarily mean giving every possible scenario equal weight when we describe it to a patient: we can choose to emphasize the one we think will best help them understand the importance of the requested behavior and, in some cases, present a possible consequence more starkly precisely to reduce the risk that they will underestimate it. If a loss of tracking could require a new scan, or inconsistent adherence could lengthen treatment, we can use those possibilities to make the message more concrete, even though we cannot know for certain that they will occur. #strong[A consequence does not have to be certain for us to communicate it, but it must remain plausible enough to be clinically defensible.];

Making a consequence concrete also means choosing carefully how to represent it. For a long time, I was convinced that a more detailed explanation was necessarily a better one. Over the years, though, I have learned that patients rarely remember everything we say during an appointment, especially when the information is extensive, technical, or far removed from their everyday experience. They are much more likely to remember an image, a comparison, a phrase, or a number that helped turn an abstract concept into something immediately understandable. That is why photographs, scans, metaphors, comparisons, and even simple numerical references can be extremely useful tools.

Not every number we use in a conversation has to represent a scientific measurement. Sometimes a number simply serves a communication purpose: it makes a proportion more intuitive, gives a consequence a sense of scale, or helps the patient remember the message. #strong[What matters is distinguishing a simplification used to communicate from clinical data presented as scientifically measured.]; If I show a scan or cite an actual figure, that information must, of course, be accurate; if I use a number, a metaphor, or a simplification to make a concept understandable, that is a communication tool.

Our goal is not to convey the full complexity of our clinical reasoning to the patient, but to make sure the important idea stays with them after the appointment and comes back to mind a few hours later, when they have to decide what to do.

#sezione[4\. Reduce Ambiguity About Future Behavior]

Once we understand why something needs to change, we need to establish #strong[what, specifically, will change];. Expressions such as #emph[“almost always,”];  #emph[“as much as I can,”];  #emph[“only sometimes,”];  #emph[“practically all day,”]; or #emph[“I can’t”]; are perfectly natural in everyday language, but they do a poor job of describing clinically relevant behavior.

To us, #emph[“wearing the aligners”]; may mean wearing them twenty\-two hours a day; to the patient, it may mean wearing them for most of the day, carefully putting them back in before the appointment, or wearing them whenever they could. Clinician and patient can therefore use the same words while assigning them very different meanings.

For a long time, my response was to challenge those statements. If what I observed clinically suggested something different, I tried to convince the patient that their #emph[“always”]; was not really always. Today I prefer to do something much simpler: I try to #strong[get the patient to be specific];. I do not debate the meaning of #emph[“always”];; I walk through a typical day. I do not try to determine whether #emph[“I can’t”]; is true or false; I try to understand exactly when the difficulty arises.

If a patient tells me they always wear their aligners, I can ask what time they eat breakfast, how long the aligners stay out, what happens at lunch, whether they drink coffee during the afternoon, how they handle dinner, and what time they put the aligners back in before bed. If, instead, they tell me they cannot wear their orthodontic elastics, I can try to find out when the problem occurs: at work, while they are talking, in the evening, or when they go out. I can ask whether they forget them, do not have them with them, or find the discomfort too much after a few hours. I am not interrogating them to catch them in a contradiction. I am turning a broad, subjective assessment into a sequence of behaviors we can observe and, if necessary, change. #strong[When the problem becomes specific, it often becomes more manageable, too.];

The same principle should apply when we define what needs to happen from that point forward. The more important an instruction is to the outcome, the less it should depend on personal interpretation. #emph[“Try to wear your elastics as much as possible”]; leaves enormous room for ambiguity. Specifying when they need to be put on, when they can be taken off, and what to do if the patient forgets them turns a general recommendation into an observable behavior. This is where the #emph[implementation intentions]; we discussed at the beginning of the chapter come in handy. Telling a patient #emph[“you need to remember your elastics”]; leaves them with the task of remembering every time. Working with them to create a rule such as #emph[“when I finish brushing my teeth at night, I put on my elastics”]; instead links the behavior to a recognizable situation. We have not changed the instruction, but we have made it easier to turn into a routine.

Reducing ambiguity also means paying attention to how we phrase our own instructions. In a therapeutic relationship, there is inevitably a difference in expertise: patients come to us precisely because they expect us to know what is necessary and what is optional. Yet, in our understandable effort not to sound authoritarian, we sometimes make something seem negotiable when, clinically, it is not.

Phrases like #emph[“maybe give it a try,”];  #emph[“it would be better,”];  #emph[“try to do it,”]; or #emph[“let’s see if you can”]; are perfectly appropriate when we are genuinely suggesting something optional, but they become problematic when they concern a behavior we consider necessary for treatment to work. Being authoritative does not mean being harsh or using our role as clinicians to demand blind obedience. It means taking responsibility for stating clearly when a particular condition is necessary to achieve the goal we have agreed on.

This becomes especially important when patients understandably look for an easier solution. If they ask whether they can wear their aligners for fewer hours, stop wearing orthodontic elastics, or cut back on an instruction we consider necessary, the desire to maintain a pleasant relationship can lead us to make something seem negotiable when, clinically, it is not. The approach I have found most useful is to distinguish #strong[what]; from #strong[how];. The #emph[what]; is a matter of clinical instruction: if a particular behavior is necessary for treatment, there is no point pretending otherwise. The #emph[how];, on the other hand, can often be worked out with the patient, giving them room to decide how to organize their day, build a routine, and fit that behavior into their daily life.

Patients do not get to decide whether a clinically necessary part of treatment is necessary, but they can help decide how to make it work with their daily schedule. I consider this balance essential: #strong[being authoritative about the clinical need while giving patients autonomy over the behavioral strategy];.

#sezione[5\. Make the Behavior Easier and Check Its Effect]

Defining exactly what patients need to do does not yet mean they will be able to do it. The problem is not always a lack of motivation: a behavior may be hard to fit into a routine, require too many decisions, depend on remembering at the wrong moment, or need to happen somewhere the patient does not have what they need.

In these cases, increasing the pressure in our conversations does relatively little, while changing the environment in which the behavior needs to happen may be much more useful. This is one of the ideas behind #strong[choice architecture];: every decision takes place in a context, and how that context is organized can make certain actions easier or harder.

The principle of #emph[nudging]; fits within this approach: small interventions that make a useful behavior easier without ruling out alternatives. A reminder, a sooner follow\-up check, a supply of orthodontic elastics kept in the right place, or a routine tied to an existing habit can narrow the gap between a stated intention and everyday behavior.

A patient who forgets their orthodontic elastics before bed, for example, could link putting them on to something they already do every night, such as brushing their teeth or setting their phone on the nightstand. Someone who tends to leave their aligners at home could organize their routine so they always have what they need with them. In these cases, we have not necessarily increased motivation; we have reduced the friction that kept intention from turning into action. #strong[Our goal, then, is not just to persuade patients to do something, but to make that behavior easy enough to fit into their daily lives.];

At this point, though, one element is still missing: #strong[feedback];. One of the problems with orthodontic treatment is that many consequences emerge slowly. Patients can wear their aligners less than they need to for several days without seeing any obvious difference, and they can forget their orthodontic elastics for weeks without immediately noticing what is happening. By the time the problem becomes clinically visible, the behavior that caused it may be well in the past, making the connection between cause and effect much less intuitive.

That is why showing an earlier photograph, comparing two scans, pointing out a spot where the aligner no longer fits properly, or showing that a movement has fallen behind can be much more effective than repeating the same recommendation yet again. Feedback makes visible something that would otherwise remain abstract and helps patients connect what they do every day with what is happening during treatment.

Feedback, though, must not become evidence in a trial. We do not show the scan to say #emph[“See? I was right, wasn’t I?”];, because at that point we would simply have turned a clinical tool into a weapon in the discussion. We use it to make the connection between what is happening and what needs to change visible.

But feedback is not just for the patient. #strong[It also helps us check whether the assumption behind our intervention was correct.]; If we have identified a problematic behavior, tried to change it, and, after an appropriate interval, treatment still is not responding as we expected, we cannot simply conclude that the patient is still not following instructions. We need to be willing to reconsider our assumption and ask whether there is a clinical, biological, or biomechanical problem we failed to recognize.

Feedback therefore closes the loop on the protocol and, at the same time, can start it again. It shifts the conversation away from personal trust and toward observable evidence: we do not have to decide whether to believe the patient. We can look together at what is happening, decide what to change, and check soon enough whether the new strategy is working. #strong[A protocol does not end when we have told the patient what to do, but when we have verified that what we decided together is producing the effect we wanted.];

#sezione[Shared Responsibility, Distinct Roles]

Orthodontic treatment is inevitably a collaboration in which clinician and patient take part in the same process with different responsibilities. We are responsible for diagnosis, treatment planning, clinical decisions, follow\-up visits, and any changes needed during treatment. The patient has different responsibilities, including following instructions, wearing aligners and orthodontic elastics correctly, keeping appointments, and telling us when something is not working.

Shared responsibility does not mean that responsibilities are indistinguishable. Simply saying #emph[“the outcome depends on both of us”]; is accurate only if we can then clarify which part depends on us and which depends on the patient. This helps us avoid two opposite extremes: automatically blaming the patient for every problem or taking responsibility ourselves for behaviors over which we have no direct control.

That is also one reason protocols should begin #strong[before a problem arises];. Defining roles when everything is going well is much easier than trying to establish them once the aligners have already stopped tracking and clinician and patient are trying to figure out who is responsible.

The same goes for consequences. If a particular behavior could require a new scan, change the timeline, or affect how we need to proceed, it is better for the patient to know that before the situation arises. #strong[A consequence explained after a problem occurs can feel like a punishment; the same consequence explained in advance becomes a condition of treatment.];

#sezione[A Protocol Must Belong to the Practice]

Finally, there is one point I consider essential: a protocol cannot exist only in the orthodontist’s head, because over the course of treatment, the patient speaks with the front desk staff, assistants, hygienists, other clinicians, and everyone else involved in their care. If each of them communicates a different rule, it quickly stops being a rule.

Not everyone needs to use exactly the same words. Repeating a script too rigidly could seem artificial and keep each team member from communicating naturally. But it is essential that #strong[the meaning of the answer remain the same];.

The front desk staff needs to know which appointments can be rescheduled, which scheduling consequences need to be communicated, and which decisions are solely the clinician’s to make. Assistants need to understand what the instructions mean and not inadvertently turn a necessary condition into a mere suggestion. The clinician, for their part, cannot contradict a position in front of the patient that the team has correctly taken based on the shared protocol.

The patient may speak with different people, but should still sense #strong[one consistent clinical direction];. If the clinician presents an instruction as essential and an assistant presents it as optional, we cannot be surprised when the patient chooses the interpretation that requires less effort. Likewise, if a rule or consequence changes depending on whom the patient speaks with, that rule inevitably becomes negotiable.

Team consistency, then, does not mean everyone repeating the same phrases. It means agreeing on the same conditions, the same limits, and the same direction. #strong[A protocol truly becomes a protocol only when it stops being the clinician’s personal response and becomes the consistent way the entire practice handles a familiar situation.];

#sezione[From Principles to Strategies]

None of the principles described in this chapter is a magic formula, and none is enough on its own to build a protocol. We will not need to use framing, loss aversion, nudging, feedback, and every other tool at the same time in every conversation. A conversation built that way would feel artificial and would probably be ineffective, too.

Each strategy should draw only on the elements that are useful for the problem in front of us. In some cases, our main task will be to reduce defensiveness; in others, it will be to make a consequence more concrete, reduce ambiguity, make a behavior easier, or state a clinical requirement more clearly. Cognitive psychology and behavioral economics do not give us a script to recite. They give us tools that can help us understand why some responses make change easier while others are more likely to lead to conflict.

The way we communicate must also adapt to the person in front of us. A direct patient may need a brief explanation focused on the decision at hand, while a more analytical patient will want more detailed information and a fuller account of each step. Someone who is less confident may need a more gradual approach and more reassurance. What should not change is the logic of the protocol: #strong[reduce defensiveness, protect the person without protecting the behavior, make consequences concrete, define the future behavior precisely, make it easier to carry out, and check its effect];.

The protocols presented in the following pages grew out of situations I encountered repeatedly in clinical practice, responses that did not work at first, and changes made over time. They should not be treated as universal formulas that always produce the same reaction. Human behavior inevitably varies, and no sequence of communication can guarantee that a patient will follow instructions. Their value lies not in eliminating that variability, but in helping us approach similar situations with a recognizable method—one we can observe, discuss, and improve. #strong[An improvised response disappears with the conversation in which it was used; a repeatable method, by contrast, can help the entire practice learn.];

That is why each protocol in the chapters that follow will have a recognizable structure. We will start with the clinical situation and the signs that help us identify it, pinpoint the behavior that needs to change and the common mistakes that can increase patient resistance, then define the steps to take, the questions to ask, the points to check, and the consequences to explain in advance. The clinical dialogue will show how to put that structure into concrete words, while the #strong[“Why This Protocol Works”]; box will let us return, when useful, to the psychological principles we have just discussed.

This distinction between procedure and explanation matters. Colleagues need to know what to say and do, but they should also understand why a particular sequence may be more effective than an accusation, a vague reminder, or simply repeating instructions. Only by understanding the mechanism can we change the words without changing the protocol’s function.

The goal, then, is not to put together a collection of #emph[“clever lines”]; or to turn the orthodontist into an expert in persuasion. I want to arrive at something much simpler and more useful: when faced with a patient who is not following instructions, I want our first thought to gradually stop being #emph[“How do I explain it to them one more time?”]; and become #emph[“What is keeping this person from doing what they already know they need to do? What consequence actually matters to them? And what protocol can I use to make that behavior more likely to change?”];

For many years, we have planned tooth movement with extreme precision while leaving the conversations needed to protect that plan to improvisation. We repeated instructions, motivated patients, and tried to find a new way to put it each time. When the problem came up again, we started over.

Protocols change that perspective. They do not try to make patient behavior predictable, and they certainly cannot control it. But they allow us to make #strong[our response]; more predictable. If certain behaviors follow recognizable patterns, we can learn to recognize them. If we can recognize them, we can prepare to manage them. And if we can prepare to manage them, our response can finally stop being an improvisation and become a method.

#strong[This is where the most practical part of this book begins.];

