#leva[Main motivators: time and control.]

With a Dominance\-style patient, I would avoid a direct confrontation, because openly questioning what they are telling us could quickly turn the conversation into a contest. It is more effective to present the two possibilities clearly and let what the patient does next determine which path we can take.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“If you’re already wearing them for the right number of hours, we need to slow down because your teeth need more time. If, instead, you think you could be more consistent, we can keep the weekly changes and see right away whether tracking improves. We’ll have our answer at your next visit.”];]
  ]),
  (lettera: "I", nome: [Influence], corpo: [
#leva[Main motivators: the outcome and motivation.]

With an Influence\-style patient, I would avoid an overly long or technical explanation and focus more on the possibility of getting things back on track quickly and keeping the original pace if they make a real change in their behavior.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“We can still try to keep the weekly changes. If you know you wore them less on some days, let’s get back to the prescribed wear time starting today and see how your teeth respond. If tracking is back where we want it at your next visit, we’ll continue this way.”];]
  ]),
  (lettera: "S", nome: [Steadiness], corpo: [
#leva[Main motivators: reassurance and a clear path forward.]

With a Steadiness\-style patient, it is important to keep them from interpreting the situation as a sign that treatment is failing. I would explain that we have two possible paths and that we can monitor either one over time.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“This doesn’t mean the treatment isn’t working. We just need to find out whether your teeth need more time or whether we can make your wear time more consistent. If you think you can be more consistent, we’ll keep the weekly changes for now and look at how your teeth are responding together at your next visit.”];]
  ]),
  (lettera: "C", nome: [Conscientiousness], corpo: [
#leva[Main motivators: data and explanation.]

With a Conscientiousness\-style patient, I would make greater use of what we can observe clinically, showing them how the aligners are tracking and explaining the two possibilities precisely, along with how the next visit will help us distinguish between them.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“Clinically, we can see that your teeth have fallen behind the planned positions. If you’re already wearing the aligners for the right number of hours, we need to consider a slower biological response and give each aligner more time. If, instead, you think you’ve missed some hours of wear, we can keep the weekly changes for now, return to the prescribed wear time, and check at your next visit whether the gap has narrowed.”];]
  ]),
))

