#capitolo(numero: "7", etichetta: [Chapter 7], titolo: [The Patient Who Wears Their Aligners but Not Elastics], epigrafe: [“A chain is only as strong as its weakest link.”], fonte: [English proverb])

When I began using intermaxillary elastics with aligners many years ago, relatively few clinicians were systematically incorporating them into their treatment mechanics, especially to correct Class II malocclusions through distalization or other #strong[sagittal mechanics];. To me, they were a fundamental part of treatment, and, as often happened whenever I introduced something new into my practice, I watched every case closely, trying to understand which mechanics worked best and which movements were more predictable.

Some treatments proceeded exactly as I had expected, while others seemed to behave completely differently. I would start a distalization, wait a few months, and then find an apparently contradictory situation at the follow\-up visit: the aligners were tracking the teeth perfectly, tracking was excellent, crowding was resolving, rotations were improving, and everything that depended directly on the aligners seemed to be moving in the right direction. #strong[The Class II malocclusion, however, was not improving.];

Over time, I began to recognize a pattern that recurred fairly regularly: #strong[a patient could wear their aligners correctly while being far less consistent with their orthodontic elastics.]; I realized that I had made a simple conceptual mistake: I tended to think of adherence as a general characteristic of the patient, when wearing aligners correctly and wearing orthodontic elastics correctly are two entirely different behaviors. Today, that distinction seems almost obvious to me, but for many years it was anything but.

#sezione[When the Aligners Work but the Elastics Don’t]

In my practice, I use intermaxillary elastics primarily for sagittal and transverse correction. They can be very useful for crossbites, although I generally introduce them at a later stage of treatment, so adherence presents somewhat different challenges there. The situation in which their use becomes particularly important, and in which I have learned the most from observing patient behavior, is sagittal correction.

When I perform a distalization, I prescribe orthodontic elastics from the start and expect sagittal correction to progress alongside the movements planned for the aligners. If, after a few months, I see spaces opening between the posterior teeth and the aligners continuing to track well, but the Class II molar relationship is not improving and unwanted effects are beginning to appear in the anterior teeth, I strongly suspect that the patient is not wearing their elastics consistently enough. This is essentially what we saw happen in Laura’s case, described in the first part of the book.

When I use interarch mechanics instead, I introduce orthodontic elastics after aligning, leveling, and coordinating the arches transversely. Here, too, the sign is fairly clear: the aligners continue to fit well and everything proceeds as planned, but the sagittal correction does not become apparent as I had expected.

This is a very different situation from that of a patient who does not wear their aligners correctly. In the previous chapter, a generalized loss of tracking was one of the main warning signs; here, tracking can be perfect, and the patient can look in the mirror every day and see their teeth getting progressively straighter, becoming convinced that treatment is working exactly as it should. Paradoxically, that is one reason it can be so difficult to help them understand the importance of orthodontic elastics.

Once the aligners are in, they can stay in the mouth for many hours without requiring any further decisions. The patient takes them out to eat, generally has them right in front of them, and, when the meal is over, simply has to remember to put them back in.

Orthodontic elastics, by contrast, require a series of small additional actions repeated several times throughout the day. They must be removed and replaced regularly, the patient has to remember to always have a pack on hand, and, especially at first, putting them on can be harder and more uncomfortable. Every meal, outing, workday, school day, sporting event, or trip can become a point at which that routine breaks down.

The literature specifically on orthodontic elastics confirms how important these small obstacles can be. Veeroo and colleagues interviewed patients who had been prescribed full\-time wear of intraoral elastics and identified forgetfulness, meals, pain or discomfort, social situations, sports, lost or broken elastics, and a limited perception of the benefits among the main barriers to adherence.

#strong[It is not enough to know whether a patient is following instructions. We need to understand which part of treatment they are following and, above all, where the behavior we prescribed breaks down.];

The first way to find out is to avoid the seemingly most natural question, which is probably also the one that gives us the least information. If I ask #emph[“Are you wearing your elastics?”];, the answer is almost always predictable: #emph[“Yes, Doctor. Always.”];

But if I try to reconstruct a normal day, that #emph[always]; often becomes less absolute. So I start asking: #emph[“Do you take them off when you have breakfast? And what do you do as soon as you finish? Do you put them back on immediately after lunch? Do you wear them at work or school? When you go out in the evening? On weekends? Do you always have a pack with you?”];

At that point, it may emerge that #emph[“always”]; actually means overnight and for much of the day, but not when the patient is at work; or during the week, but much less on weekends; or that several hours pass after lunch before they remember to put them back on. This does not necessarily mean they were deliberately lying to me before. As we saw in the first part of the book, our perception of our own behavior can differ from what we actually do throughout the day, and we know that patients tend to report more hours of wear than objective measurements show. That is why I have no interest in asking ten times, #emph[“Are you sure you’re wearing them?”]; I need to understand not just whether the patient wears them, but #strong[when they wear them, when they take them off, and, above all, when they do not put them back on];.

There are also a few small signs that I have learned to watch for over the years and that can help me get a clearer picture of what the patient actually does. One line I have heard so many times that it has become almost a classic comes from the patient who arrives for a follow\-up visit without their elastics and, when I ask why they did not put them on that day, replies: #emph[“I didn’t put them on because I was coming here anyway.”]; At that point, I simply answer: #emph[“What does that have to do with it? If you’re supposed to wear them all the time, you need to have them on when you come to see me, too.”];

It is not the hour spent without elastics before the visit that particularly worries me, but the reasoning behind that decision. If the patient thinks it is perfectly natural to stop wearing them on the very day they come to the orthodontist, they probably have not yet taken the central point of the prescription to heart. Orthodontic elastics are not something to wear when you remember or when they are not too much trouble: if they are prescribed for continuous wear, they need to become as much a part of treatment as the aligners.

Another small indicator I find extremely useful in practice is watching #strong[how]; the patient puts on their elastics. When I have doubts, I hand them the elastics and simply say, #emph[“Show me how you put them on.”]; A patient who has genuinely been wearing elastics for weeks or months tends to do it automatically. They know where the hooks are, know how to position their fingers, and can generally put the elastics on quickly, often without even using a mirror. When, instead, someone who is supposed to have been wearing them for months asks me for a mirror, spends a long time looking for the hooks, needs the tool, or takes several minutes to get them on, that raises another small red flag for me.

The same principle applies to another behavioral indicator I have used for many years: #strong[how many orthodontic elastics the patient actually wears];. When I prescribe them, I normally give the patient two packs, about two hundred elastics, and ask them to replace them at least three times a day. I do not tell them that this supply will not last until the next visit, which I generally schedule three or four months later, because I want to see what happens without prompting them.

If the patient really follows the prescription, they should run out well before the appointment and will have to contact the office to say something like, #emph[“Doctor, I’m running out of elastics. Can I stop by to pick up some more?”]; That call cannot, of course, tell me how many hours a day they wore them, but it is a behavior consistent with what I prescribed and, above all, it happens spontaneously, without my having told them that I would expect them to ask for more.

The opposite situation is even more interesting. After three or four months, the patient returns for a follow\-up visit. I ask how their supply of elastics is holding up, and they might answer: #emph[“Great, doctor. I still have an almost full pack.”]; At that point, I do not need to start a discussion about how many hours they actually wore them. If I gave them about two hundred elastics and asked them to replace them at least three times a day, that supply simply is not consistent with several months of wear as prescribed.

Interestingly, Veeroo and colleagues also used the number of elastics consumed as an indicator of adherence, asking participants to save and return the ones they had worn. Mine is not intended as a scientific measure of adherence, but the principle is similar: #strong[sometimes what a patient uses up tells us more about their behavior than what they remember doing.];

#strong[Of course, none of these signs, taken alone, proves that a patient is not following instructions.]; Difficulty putting on orthodontic elastics can depend on manual dexterity, eyesight, age, or the complexity of the configuration; the number of elastics left can be affected if the patient has lost some, received more, or used a different pack. What matters to me is the #strong[convergence of clues];: sagittal correction that is not progressing as expected, an inconsistent account of the patient’s day, little familiarity with putting on elastics, and a rate of use that does not match the prescription. #strong[When several clues begin to tell the same story, the picture becomes much more meaningful.];

#sezione[Orthodontic Elastics Work If Worn as Prescribed]

Once I understand #strong[where]; adherence tends to break down, there is a second step I consider just as important: making sure the patient has truly understood what the prescription I gave them means. If I prescribe orthodontic elastics for all\-day wear, the patient cannot decide on their own to wear them only at night or only when it is more convenient. Wearing them only at night does not mean following the same treatment a little less faithfully; it means #strong[following a different prescription from the one I planned];.

For years, I have used a very simple example to make this idea easy to grasp: #emph[“Imagine you’re in a car going uphill with no brakes. When you put on your elastics, it’s as if you’re pressing the gas pedal: the car climbs and climbs and climbs, and little by little we get the correction we want. But when you take off the elastics, the car doesn’t stay where it is: it starts rolling backward, and you lose some of the ground you gained. That’s why even one day without elastics can undo what we achieved over the previous three or four days. But we shouldn’t think only in terms of whole days, because the same problem can occur for several hours every day. Think, for example, about what happens if you wear them properly from the afternoon through the evening and all night, but then you go to school in the morning and don’t put them on again until five in the afternoon. Or you put them on in the morning, take them off during a break to eat something, and then, because you don’t have a fresh pack with you, go without elastics for the rest of the day. During those hours, we aren’t simply pausing treatment: we risk losing some of the effect we achieved in the preceding hours. The same goes for the weekend. If you wear them properly Monday through Friday, but on Saturday and Sunday you go out, play sports, see friends, and decide not to wear them, we risk losing much of what we gained during the week in those two days. So the issue isn’t just how many hours of wear you can add up by the end of the day, but how continuously we can keep the elastics working. That’s why I insist on this so much: I don’t want you to wear elastics only when it’s convenient, because they need to support the correction we’re trying to achieve without interruption.”];

The numerical comparison I use with patients—that one day without orthodontic elastics can undo what was achieved over the previous three or four days— #strong[does not come from an experimental measurement and should not be interpreted as one];. There are no data that would allow us to determine with that degree of precision how much correction is lost after a day, or after a given number of hours, without elastics. Yet I have deliberately used this line for years because it makes the need for continuous force easy to grasp and, above all, because #strong[I want the possibility of interrupting elastic wear to worry the patient];.

In a sense, it is another of the #strong[white lies]; we discussed in the first part of the book: I am not inventing a risk that does not exist, because wearing elastics intermittently really can disrupt the continuity of the mechanics I planned, but I deliberately turn that risk into a simpler, more definite, more memorable message than the literature would allow me to quantify. #strong[I do it with a specific goal: to increase the likelihood that the patient will follow the prescription.]; Saying that a day without elastics could cost the patient several days of correction makes the consequences of that behavior much more concrete than a general recommendation to wear them more consistently.

There is another factor to consider as well: the force exerted by orthodontic elastics does not remain constant over time. In an #emph[in vivo]; study of patients wearing Class II orthodontic elastics, Qodcieh and colleagues observed a progressive decline in force, particularly noticeable within the first few hours of wear. #strong[That is another reason I ask my patients to replace their elastics at least three times a day.];

#sezione[Before Trying to Motivate Them, I Find Out Why They Aren’t Wearing Them]

When I realize a patient is not wearing their elastics as prescribed, I do not automatically assume the problem is a lack of motivation. First, I try to understand what is actually happening. There is a big difference between someone who has not understood why they matter; someone who wears them only at night because they have decided on their own that eight hours is enough; someone who finds them too uncomfortable to wear at work; and someone who sincerely wants to wear them but finishes lunch each day, returns to their desk, and forgets about them until evening.

In that last case, continuing to explain how important orthodontic elastics are does little good, because the problem is not persuading the patient further but #strong[making the behavior easier];. I might suggest keeping a pack of elastics on their desk, one in their bag, and perhaps one in the car or another place where they regularly spend part of the day. Or I can use the principle of #strong[implementation intentions];, described in Chapter 5, by linking putting the elastics back on to something the patient always does and turning a vague recommendation like #emph[“Remember to put them back on”]; into a much more concrete rule: #emph[“When you finish eating, brush your teeth and put your aligners and elastics back on.”];

Digital reminders can also help when the problem is simply remembering to repeat a behavior. In a study of 42 Class II patients, Leone and colleagues sent motivational and reminder messages to the experimental group twice a week for three months and observed 3.7 times as much Class II correction as in the control group. More recently, Şenocak and Camcı studied 124 adolescents, 68 of whom were assessed specifically for Class II elastic wear, comparing text reminders, video reminders, and no reminders. #strong[When the problem is remembering a repetitive behavior, increasing motivation is not always enough; sometimes we need to build a better routine.];

The situation is entirely different when a patient does not forget their elastics but #strong[consciously chooses not to wear them at certain times of day];. They wear them at home and overnight but take them off at work, at school, or when they go out because the elastics are visible, interfere with their speech, or make them feel self\-conscious. In this case, reminders and spare packs do little good, because the problem is not remembering to put them back on: the patient has decided that, in certain situations, the social cost or discomfort of wearing elastics matters more than the prescription we gave them.

And this is exactly where our approach needs to change. Making the behavior easier is not enough: we need to make #strong[the consequences of not following the prescription more meaningful];. After all, the problem is not just that treatment might take longer. If the patient wears elastics only while at home, correction may proceed more slowly, remain incomplete, and affect the final result as well. At that point, I need to make clear to the patient that they have a choice: follow the prescription we planned, consider whether a clinically appropriate alternative exists, or accept that treatment may become longer and less predictable, with a result that could differ from what we originally planned.

#sezione[The Patient Sees Straight Teeth, Not the Class II]

When I find that the problem is primarily motivation, the approach I use most often is, once again, #strong[time as a motivator];. We might think that the outcome as a motivator would be more effective with elastics, because what we risk losing is precisely the sagittal correction. But in practice, there is a very simple problem: #strong[the patient often does not see what we see];.

We see a Class II relationship, assess the molar and canine relationships, and know exactly how much correction remains. The patient, meanwhile, looks in the mirror and sees, above all, their incisors getting straighter, the crowding diminishing, and their smile gradually improving. From their perspective, treatment is working, so it is hard to understand why we keep insisting so much on two little elastics.

That is why I explain: #emph[“Your teeth are getting straighter, and you can see that too, because that part of treatment depends mainly on the aligners. The elastics, though, are doing a different job: they correct the relationship between the upper and lower arches. If that part falls behind, we risk reaching a point where the teeth are perfectly aligned but the bite still needs correcting. Then we will have to keep working on the part that fell behind, and]; #strong[#emph[treatment will take longer than we planned]];#emph[.”];

Using #strong[time as a motivator]; makes a consequence tangible that patients have trouble seeing in the mirror: they may not recognize a residual Class II relationship, but they understand perfectly well what it means to #strong[have to continue treatment longer];.

At this point, a perfectly understandable question often comes up: #emph[“But how long will I have to wear them?”]; If I am emphasizing that elastics must be worn consistently and that insufficient adherence can prolong treatment, it is natural for the patient to want to know how long they will have to keep wearing them.

This is where, in my practice, #strong[DentalMonitoring];, which we will discuss in a later chapter, offers an enormous advantage. I explain to the patient that they should not decide on their own when to reduce or stop wearing elastics, and that they do not need to wait for their next office visit, because I can use their periodic smartphone scans to track the correction remotely. I tell them: #emph[“Don’t worry about figuring out when it’s time to wear them less or stop wearing them. Keep wearing them exactly as I prescribed because, even if I won’t see you in the office again for three, four, or perhaps six months, I’ll track your progress through your smartphone scans. When I see that we’ve achieved the correction we want, I’ll tell you how to change your elastic wear. You don’t have to decide when the elastics have finished their job.”];

This does more than reassure the patient: it also #strong[removes any ambiguity from the prescription];. Until I tell them otherwise, they must continue wearing the elastics exactly as prescribed. This also reduces the risk that they will start cutting back on their hours on their own simply because they think they have worn them enough.

All these elements help me understand where adherence breaks down and choose the best strategy for restoring it. #strong[As in the previous protocol, my goal is not to get a confession or prove to the patient that they are not wearing the elastics as they claim, but to create the conditions for their behavior to change from that point on.];

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

When the aligners are working properly but sagittal correction is not progressing as expected, I can frame the conversation this way:

#script[
#emph[“The aligners are working well, and your teeth are gradually getting straighter. The elastics have a different job, though, and right now that part of treatment isn’t progressing as I expect. Before changing anything, I want us to look together at how you wear them on a typical day. I’m not just interested in whether you put them on, but especially in when you leave them off: what happens after lunch? Do you put them back on right away? Do you wear them at work or school? What about when you go out in the evening? On the weekend?”];

#emph[“If the problem is that you simply forget them at certain times, we need to make remembering easier: keep a pack at work or school, carry one with you at all times, and make a firm rule. When you finish eating, brush your teeth and put your aligners and elastics back on. But if you choose not to wear them in certain situations because they’re visible, uncomfortable, or interfere with your speech, we need to approach this differently, because wearing them only when it’s convenient means following a different prescription from the one we planned.”];

#emph[“Your teeth are getting straighter, so when you look in the mirror, you may feel that everything is going perfectly. But the elastics correct the relationship between the upper and lower arches. If that part falls behind, we risk reaching a point where your teeth are perfectly aligned but your bite still needs correcting, and treatment could take longer than we planned. That’s why, starting today, I want elastics to be just as much a part of your treatment as the aligners.”];
]

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[Reconstructing Behavior]

Simply asking #emph[“Are you wearing your elastics?”]; tends to elicit a broad, predictable answer, whereas going through the different parts of the day helps identify when the patient actually stops following the prescription. That shifts the conversation away from whether the patient is being honest and focuses it on the behavior we need to change.

#perche-voce[Behavioral Indicators]

The progress of sagittal correction, whether the patient is wearing their orthodontic elastics at the appointment, how naturally they put them on, and how many packs they have used can, taken together, provide much more useful information than a verbal statement alone. These indicators are not meant to prove that the patient is lying, but to help us reconstruct what is actually happening.

#perche-voce[Reducing Friction]

If the problem is practical, simply increasing motivation does little good. Keeping orthodontic elastics in several strategic places and always having a pack available reduces the risk that the patient will stop wearing them because they forgot their pack or do not have one on hand.

#perche-voce[Implementation Intentions]

A rule such as #emph[“When you finish eating, brush your teeth and put your aligners and elastics back on”]; uses the principle of #strong[implementation intentions];, described in Chapter 5, by linking the behavior to a specific event that happens every day and making it easier to turn into a routine.

#perche-voce[Making the Consequence Meaningful]

When the problem is not forgetting the orthodontic elastics but consciously choosing not to wear them at certain times, making the behavior easier is not enough. We need to make the consequence of that choice more meaningful, turning something the patient has difficulty seeing into something that has a concrete meaning for them.

#perche-voce[Time as a Motivator]

Patients can easily see that their incisors are getting straighter, while they may not notice at all that sagittal correction has fallen behind. That is why #strong[time as a motivator]; is often more effective than the outcome as a motivator: explaining that they could reach a point where their teeth are aligned but part of treatment remains to be completed makes it immediately clear why adherence needs to improve now rather than at the end of treatment.

