#disc(titolo: [Adapting Communication to DISC Profiles], profili: (
  (lettera: "D", nome: [Dominance], corpo: [
#leva[Main motivators: time and control.]

With a Dominance\-style patient, I would avoid lengthy discussions about how many hours they have actually worn their orthodontic elastics. Instead, I would show them what is not happening clinically and connect it to the possibility of needlessly prolonging treatment.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“The aligners are doing their job, but the Class II relationship isn’t correcting as it should. If this part falls behind, we’ll end up with straight teeth but still have part of treatment to complete, and we’ll lose time. Starting today, we need to wear the elastics as consistently as we agreed.”];]
  ]),
  (lettera: "I", nome: [Influence], corpo: [
#leva[Main motivators: simplicity and the outcome.]

With an Influence\-style patient, I would focus above all on making the routine simple and compatible with an active social life, using a few concrete rules rather than lengthy technical explanations.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“Let’s keep it simple: one pack with you at all times and one at work or school, and one firm rule. When you finish eating, brush your teeth and put your aligners and elastics back on. That way you don’t have to think about it every time, and both parts of treatment can work together.”];]
  ]),
  (lettera: "S", nome: [Steadiness], corpo: [
#leva[Main motivators: routine and reassurance.]

With a Steadiness\-style patient, I would avoid letting the lack of correction feel like a treatment failure and focus on building a predictable routine that is easy to repeat.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“The aligners are working well, and we don’t need to overhaul anything. We simply need to make orthodontic elastic wear more consistent. Let’s look together at when you tend to forget them, build a routine that becomes automatic, and then check how the correction changes.”];]
  ]),
  (lettera: "C", nome: [Conscientiousness], corpo: [
#leva[Main motivators: clinical evidence and precision.]

With a Conscientiousness\-style patient, I would show them the sagittal relationship directly and clearly distinguish what the aligners are accomplishing from what depends more heavily on the orthodontic elastics.

#battuta-disc[#emph[“Look here: alignment is progressing well and the aligners fit properly, but the sagittal relationship hasn’t changed as much as we expected. These are two different parts of treatment, which is why we need you to wear the elastics more consistently as prescribed. We’ll check your next scans to see whether the correction resumes as expected.”];]
  ]),
))

