#capitolo(numero: "8", etichetta: [Chapter 8], titolo: [The Routine Refinement], epigrafe: [“All models are wrong, but some are useful.”], fonte: [George Box])

Whenever I plan the first set of aligners, I always add #strong[four passive aligners]; at the end of the sequence. We call them passive because, at least on paper, they contain no newly programmed movements, but I have always considered that definition a little simplistic. If the movements programmed into the preceding aligners have not been fully achieved, even an aligner labeled passive in the digital treatment setup may not be entirely passive clinically. It can continue to hold what we have achieved and, in some cases, give the preceding movements more time to finish.

But that is not the main reason I include them.

The most important reason concerns something we have discussed many times in this book: #strong[behavior];. I do not want to reach the end of the active phase of the first set, take a new scan, and tell the patient that they now have to wear the same aligner for two or three weeks while we wait for the refinement to be planned and manufactured. For months, I have asked them to build a specific routine: each week, they take a new aligner, replace the previous one, and continue treatment. If I suddenly interrupt that routine and tell them to keep wearing the same aligner for weeks, I am changing the very habit that has helped me maintain their adherence up to that point.

This is not just my clinical impression. Research on habits shows how repeated behaviors gradually become associated with the cues and context in which they occur. Wood and colleagues observed that, when those elements remain stable, a habit is more likely to continue guiding behavior, whereas a change in context can interrupt its automatic expression. Verplanken and Wood later developed this concept, showing how a disruption in the cues that support a habit can be one of the moments when the behavior becomes more dependent on deliberate thought.

That does not mean, of course, that if a patient goes a few weeks without changing aligners, they suddenly forget everything they have done over the preceding months. It means something much simpler and, to me, clinically relevant: #strong[if a routine works, I prefer not to interrupt it without a reason.];

That is why I plan for four passive aligners from the outset and schedule the appointment for when the patient reaches the first passive aligner. At that point, I check the bite, assess which movements have been achieved as planned and which need to be fine\-tuned, and, if we are looking at what I consider a normal routine refinement, I take a new scan. While I plan the next phase and we wait for the new aligners to arrive, the patient simply continues their routine: they move on to the second passive aligner, then the third, and finally the fourth, changing aligners at the same frequency as before.

It may sound like an organizational detail, but to me it is anything but. #strong[I am not using passive aligners only to maintain the position of the teeth: I am also using them to maintain the patient’s behavior.];

And it is at this appointment, when the patient has reached the first passive aligner and the active phase of the first set is over, that I have been asked the same question countless times over the years. The patient sits down in the chair knowing this is an important appointment. They have gone through thirty, thirty\-five, perhaps forty aligners, one after another. They look at their teeth and, in most cases, see a better smile, very different from the one they started with. I check the bite, assess which movements have been achieved as planned and which need to be fine\-tuned, then explain that we will take a new scan. That is when the question comes:

#dialogo[#emph[“Wait, we’re not done?”];]

At first, it surprised me. At the initial consultation, I had explained that treatment would take, for example, fifteen to eighteen months. That was only an estimate, of course: some treatments may take twelve months, others twenty\-four or more. But we had discussed the overall duration, and the patient had accepted it.

Then the aligners had arrived. A long sequence of aligners, each with its own number printed on it. And from that moment, a new countdown began, almost without the patient realizing it: 12 of 40, 18 of 40, 25 of 40, 32 of 40... Each week, the patient finished another one and moved a little closer to the final number they could see right in front of them.

Meanwhile, the fifteen to eighteen months we had discussed at the initial consultation faded further and further into the background. Not necessarily because the patient had forgotten what I told them, but because that estimate in months had gradually been replaced by something much more concrete, something they could see, touch, and count every week. Treatment was no longer a span of time; it had become a sequence with a beginning and, above all, a clearly visible end.

As they approached the last few aligners, in their mind they were not simply finishing the first set of aligners: they were finishing treatment. So when I explained that we would take a new scan to order a refinement, their surprise was entirely understandable. At that point, I could have simply reminded them of what I had said months earlier:

#dialogo[#emph[“Remember? We talked about fifteen to eighteen months.”];]

And for a while, that is exactly what I did. Then I realized I was trying to correct, at the end, an expectation I had allowed to build for months. If that reaction kept coming up, the problem was not finding a better answer when the patient reached the end of the set. It was preventing them from reaching that point convinced that the last aligner meant the end of treatment.

So I needed to address it when I gave the patient the first set, as they were beginning to form an idea of how long their treatment would last. I needed a simple way to help them understand that those aligners represented only the first major phase. That is how I started talking about 80%.

#sezione[80%]

So I began changing what I said when I handed over the aligners. I no longer wanted the patient to equate the number of aligners they received with the total duration of treatment, and I needed a concept simple enough for them to remember many months later. I decided to use a number because, over the years, I have noticed that numbers make information that might otherwise remain vague feel more concrete. They also give it a greater sense of precision.

Saying #emph[“we’ll probably still have a few things to do after this first set”]; is very different from saying #emph[“we’ll do about 80% of the work with this first set.”]; The second sentence is more immediate and, above all, easier to remember.

I deliberately chose a simple number: 80%. It is not a scientific finding, nor is it meant to predict mathematically how much we will achieve with the first set. It is a communication tool I use to convey a very simple idea: #strong[the first box is not the entire treatment];. When I hand over the aligners, I always say:

#dialogo[#emph[“These are the first aligners of your treatment. We’ll do the most important part of the work with this first set, and our goal is to get roughly 80% of the way to the final result. When we reach the end, we’ll take a new scan, see which movements have been achieved exactly as we wanted, and use a second set to work on the details and on any movements that need greater precision.”];]

Since I started introducing this idea early, the conversation at the end of the first set has become much easier. When the patient reaches the last part of the sequence, the refinement is no longer a surprise: they have known from the beginning that the first set represents the most important part of treatment, but not necessarily the last.

Bringing up the refinement from the outset also lets me clarify another important point: #strong[a refinement does not, in itself, mean that treatment has failed];. No biological system necessarily carries out every movement with the same precision with which we programmed it on the screen. Some teeth follow the sequence perfectly; others may need further correction. Some movements are fully achieved; others less so. That is exactly what the refinement is for: starting with what we have actually achieved and using a new plan to complete what remains and fine\-tune the result.

The problem is that, just as we reach this phase, something happens that can put adherence at risk.

#sezione[The Refinement Paradox]

The patient looks at their teeth and, very often, is already happy. What they see is dramatically different from where they started: their incisors are aligned, their smile has changed, and friends and family may already have told them their teeth look beautiful. We, on the other hand, still see what is missing: a rotation that has not been fully achieved, torque that needs fine\-tuning, a vertical movement that needs to be completed, some contacts that need refinement.

This is where an important difference emerges between what we see and what the patient sees. We know we are entering the phase when we need to be even more precise; the patient may feel that the work is practically done.

And this is often where the patient’s behavior changes, too. If I feel that the most important part of the work has already been done and only a few details remain, I will naturally place less value on every hour of wear. The twenty\-two hours that seemed essential during treatment start to become twenty, then eighteen. Maybe one evening I leave the aligner out a little longer because, after all, my teeth are already straight.

The problem is that, clinically, we should think almost the opposite way. If some movements remained incomplete after the first set, those are precisely the ones we need to focus on during the refinement. I cannot expect a movement that has already proved less predictable to be achieved more successfully while the patient is also cutting back on the hours I am applying the necessary force.

This is the idea I want to turn around in the patient’s mind: #strong[the refinement is not the time when they can be less consistent because so little remains; it is the time when they need to be more consistent precisely because what remains requires greater precision.]; And it is this paradox that gives rise to two of the objections I have heard most often over the years. The first almost always comes when the patient sees the new set:

#dialogo[#emph[“But why are there still so many?”];]

The patient received, say, forty aligners in the first set, and now we hand them a refinement with fifteen, eighteen, or twenty. They look at them and make an entirely understandable calculation:

#dialogo[#emph[“But if we did almost everything with forty aligners, why do I need so many now to do so little?”];]

Over time, this question, too, has forced me to make my explanation more precise.

The first reason is that, in the refinement, I am working on the very movements that were hardest to achieve. If a movement did not follow the first digital treatment setup as I expected, it would make little sense simply to try it again at the same rate. I may decide to slow it down, spread it over more steps, and apply the force more gradually. As a result, a seemingly small movement may require more aligners than the patient imagines just by looking at the position of their teeth.

The second reason is that I am not thinking only about the teeth that still need to move. While I work on the remaining details, I also want to maintain and consolidate everything I have already achieved. So the refinement period is not exclusively about producing new movements; it is also about preserving those that have already been achieved as planned.

Then there is a third reason, much simpler and almost a matter of arithmetic, that can significantly affect how the patient perceives the total number of aligners. It relates directly to the four passive aligners we discussed at the beginning of the chapter. If the first set contains forty aligners and four are passive, that means thirty\-six are intended for the active phase: the four passive aligners account for 10% of the entire sequence, so their effect on the total number is relatively small. In a short refinement, however, that proportion changes completely. If, for example, I had twelve active aligners and added four passive ones, the sequence would grow to sixteen, and a quarter of the aligners I gave the patient would be passive. The patient sees sixteen aligners and thinks all of them will be needed to move the few teeth that still need work, when part of the sequence serves a completely different purpose. That is why I explain the number, too:

#dialogo[#emph[“Don’t let the number on the box scare you. It doesn’t necessarily mean you’ll have to wear every one of these aligners. There are only a few movements left to make, but we’ve slowed them down because they require greater precision. I’ve also added four passive aligners at the end. They have no new movements programmed into them and are mainly there to keep your routine going while we prepare the next phase, if one is needed. So the number you see isn’t the number of active aligners between you and the end of treatment.”];]

This explanation has another advantage: it takes away another possible reason for the patient to be less consistent. Fifteen or twenty new aligners do not mean that treatment has suddenly gone backward; they mean we have entered a different phase, in which we are working more slowly and toward more specific goals.

But there is another interpretation that can lead to exactly the same result. This time, it does not come from the number of aligners, but from what the patient feels when wearing them:

#dialogo[#emph[“Doctor, these aligners don’t feel tight.”];]

Or:

#dialogo[#emph[“Doctor, these aligners feel much looser.”];]

At first, my answer was purely technical. I explained to the patient that this was normal because the new set had been made from the scan we had just taken. The first aligner therefore started from a position extremely close to the actual position of their teeth and would not necessarily produce the same sensation they remembered from other phases of treatment.

The explanation was correct, but at some point I realized it did not fully solve the problem.

Over the months, the feeling of pressure had become a kind of physical proof to the patient that the aligner was working. When they felt it pulling, they thought the aligner was doing its job; when they felt almost nothing, they could easily reach the opposite conclusion. And if something does not seem to be working, it becomes much easier to give yourself permission to wear it less.

So I began being much more explicit about this point, too:

#dialogo[#emph[“At first, these aligners may feel looser, and that’s normal. They were made from the scan we just took, so the beginning of the sequence starts from exactly where your teeth are today. But the movements we need to achieve are the very ones that needed more help in the first set. That’s why, in this phase, wearing them for twenty\-two hours is even more important than it was before.”];]

This explanation lets me head off the wrong interpretation before the patient can turn it into a reason to cut back on wear time. Over the years, I have also looked for a solution that goes beyond communication.

Since I began using a system that lets me choose among materials with different properties, I have used the stiffer material for refinements. For me, the choice also has a biomechanical rationale, especially when I need to work on root control. But over time, I noticed another interesting effect: the patient was more aware of the aligner in their mouth and continued to feel that it still had a job to do.

Of course, this does not mean that an aligner that #emph[“pulls harder”]; is necessarily more effective, or that the sensation the patient reports can be used as a measure of orthodontic force. The advantage that interests me here is primarily behavioral: being able to feel the aligner reinforces the patient’s sense that treatment is still active and, consequently, that they need to keep wearing it just as carefully.

It is one of those observations that came more from the treatment chair than from books, and over time I have come to sum it up in a very simple sentence: if the patient cannot feel the aligners working, they may stop seeing them as important and become less consistent about wearing them.

And it is precisely during a refinement, when the patient can already see the result and may naturally be inclined to be less consistent, that I do not want this to happen.

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

All these observations, accumulated over the years, have gradually changed the way I hand over a refinement. Today I do not wait for the patient to tell me they thought they were finished, that there are too many aligners, or that they do not feel them pulling. If I know these objections come up repeatedly, I would rather address them first.

The conversation might go something like this:

#script[
#emph[“We’ve reached exactly the phase we talked about at the beginning. With the first set, we did the biggest part of the work and got to roughly 80% of the result we wanted. Now we need to work on the details, especially the movements that were not achieved with the same precision as the others.];

#emph[That is exactly why this phase is no less important than the first. In fact, some of the movements we need to make now are the very ones that have already shown they need more attention, and we have deliberately slowed them down to make them more gradual. To achieve them, we still need the same thing we have needed all along: consistent force, which means twenty\-two hours of wear.];

#emph[You’ll also see that the number of aligners may seem high compared with the few things we still need to fix, but that doesn’t mean we have to redo half the treatment. We’ve spread some movements over more aligners precisely because we want to make them more slowly. I’ve also added four passive aligners at the end so you can keep up your routine while we prepare the next phase, if one is needed.];

#emph[At first, you may also feel that these aligners aren’t as tight. That’s normal, because they were made from a scan of your teeth as they are today. But don’t take that to mean you need to wear them any less: the movements we’re working toward now are precisely the ones for which I want you to be as consistent as possible.];

#emph[We’ve already completed the biggest part of the journey, and you can see the result. It would be a shame to ease up now, on the very movements that will take us from a good result to the result we planned.”];
]

This conversation did not come from any single theory of communication. It is the result of many small objections I have heard over the years and the realization that I needed to stop waiting until they came up to respond to them.

Over time, I have learned that when an objection comes up often enough, getting better at answering it is not necessarily the best solution. I would rather make it less likely to come up in the first place.

If I know the patient might think they are finished, I set that expectation when I hand over the first set. If I know they might think fifteen or twenty aligners are too many, I explain where that number comes from. If I know they might take a weaker feeling of pressure to mean they do not need to wear them as much, I clear up that ambiguity before it becomes a reason to change their behavior.

Ultimately, that is the real goal of the protocol: #strong[to leave as little room as possible for interpretations that could justify wearing the aligners less consistently, while making clear how much of the result still depends on the patient’s behavior.];

A refinement is not a failure, nor is it a phase when we can finally relax. It is when we move from major changes to the details, and because those details often involve the movements that have been hardest to achieve, the patient’s consistency remains crucial.

The patient sees that only a little remains. Our job is to help them understand how much that little matters.

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[Framing and Managing Expectations]

The refinement is not presented as #emph[“another set because we aren’t finished,”]; but as an expected phase of treatment in which we move from major movements to precision. The clinical facts do not change, but the frame through which the patient interprets them does: we are not starting over; we are completing what we planned.

The approximate 80% figure reinforces that expectation. Saying #emph[“we’ll probably still have something to do after this first set”]; leaves the patient with a vague piece of information that is easy to forget or reinterpret as the months pass. Saying instead #emph[“this first set will do about 80% of the work”]; gives them a concrete reference point that is easier to remember. The 80% figure is not used as a scientific finding, but as a communication anchor: when the patient approaches the end of the first set months later and the number printed on the aligners suggests that treatment is finished, we can bring them back to the expectation we established from the start.

#perche-voce[Preventing Ambiguity]

The main objections are addressed before they arise. The patient already knows that the end of the first set may not be the end of treatment, why a refinement may contain a seemingly large number of aligners, and why the new aligners may feel different.

Explaining these things in advance leaves less room for interpretations that could become a reason to wear the aligners less consistently. If I know an objection comes up often enough, I would rather prevent it than keep getting better at answering it.

#perche-voce[Goal Gradient]

The literature on the #emph[goal gradient]; shows how getting closer to a goal can increase the effort people put into reaching it. In my clinical experience, however, a different problem can arise during a refinement: if the patient thinks the result is already good enough, they may begin to ease up just as they approach the end.

The protocol therefore aims to prevent the patient from seeing how close they are to the result as a reason to relax. Having only a little left to do does not mean that little matters less. On the contrary, because we are working on the movements that have been harder to achieve, the final details require the same consistency as the previous phase, if not greater care.

#perche-voce[Commitment and Consistency]

During a refinement, we are not asking the patient to adopt a new behavior, but to continue one they have maintained for months. The twenty\-two hours of wear remain the same and are presented as a natural continuation of the effort that allowed them to achieve the result they see in the mirror.

The same principle explains why I add four passive aligners rather than leave the patient wearing the same aligner for weeks. If a routine is working, I would rather protect it than interrupt it unnecessarily. When the patient starts the first passive aligner, I perform the checkup and take the scan; while we wait, the patient moves on to the second, third, and finally fourth. The weekly changes continue and, from the patient’s perspective, their behavior stays essentially the same.

#perche-voce[Making the Outcome Salient]

At this point, the patient can already see much of the result, and what they have achieved can itself become the protocol’s main motivator. The message is not simply that a few details remain, but that those details matter and that we need to finish what we have already begun.

Simply saying #emph[“you need to keep wearing your aligners for twenty\-two hours”]; states a rule. Explaining instead that the remaining movements are the very ones that have been harder to achieve makes clear why that rule still matters. The perspective changes: we are not asking the patient to start over, but to complete the result they have already spent months working toward.

A refinement, then, must not become the point at which the patient feels treatment is slowly winding down. In how we communicate it, we need to achieve exactly the opposite: help them understand that we have entered the phase where the result is close, but where every detail matters precisely for that reason.

When a refinement is not routine, but has become necessary because the patient has not followed the treatment plan, the problem is entirely different. In that case, we will need to do more than explain a new series of aligners: we will need to manage it without teaching the patient that failing to follow the plan has no consequences.

But that deserves a protocol of its own.

