#capitolo(numero: "9", etichetta: [Chapter 9], titolo: [When Refinements Teach the Wrong Lesson], epigrafe: [“The consequences of an action influence the probability that it will be repeated.”], fonte: [B. F. Skinner])

A refinement does more than correct teeth; unfortunately, it can also teach the patient that following the treatment plan is not essential.

When widespread loss of tracking appears for the first time, I accept that there may be different explanations and try to get the patient back on track without demanding a confession, as I explained in Chapter 6. But if the patient has already stopped wearing the aligners in several sequences, received new sets, and keeps returning with the same problem, I am no longer dealing only with poor adherence.

I am also dealing with what the patient learned from previous refinements: that a new scan will erase the consequences and treatment can start over under the same terms. This is where a clinically useful option risks teaching the wrong lesson.

#sezione[Alessandro’s Case]

About ten years ago, I began treating Alessandro, a thirteen\-year\-old boy who was missing both upper lateral incisors and had a full Class II malocclusion. The goal of treatment was to correct the Class II malocclusion and create the space needed to replace the missing teeth when the time was right. This plan required consistent adherence because the result depended not only on wearing the aligners, but also on wearing orthodontic elastics.

Alessandro, however, almost never wore his aligners. I saw him every four months, and we always found ourselves in the same situation: tracking had broken down, the planned movements had not occurred, and we could not continue with the set we had given him. We stopped, took another scan, and prepared a new treatment plan, each time starting over with the same explanation and his promise that this time he would follow the plan seriously.

#strong[During the first two years of treatment, we had already prepared five refinements, or at least what I still thought of as refinements at the time.]; Each new scan seemed to give us a chance to start over, but in reality it did not change Alessandro’s behavior. He had gradually learned that if he did not wear his aligners, we could simply stop that set and make another.

After nearly two years, something finally changed. Alessandro began wearing his aligners more regularly, and over about six months, his teeth became visibly straighter. For the first time, we had achieved a significant clinical result, although we still needed to complete the Class II correction because he had not worn his orthodontic elastics as consistently. I therefore decided to do a refinement, explaining that the new set would need to consolidate what we had achieved and complete the remaining movements.

When I saw him again four months later, I was surprised by the clinical picture. Not only had the teeth failed to stay in the positions we had achieved with the previous refinement, they had actually become more crooked. After finally managing to straighten them, we were practically back where we had started because Alessandro had not worn any of the new aligners.

As we looked at the photographs together and compared the scan with the previous one, his father made an observation that would change the way I handled these situations forever:

#dialogo[#emph[“Wow, this time his teeth are even more crooked than before. I imagine there will be a cost now, because the company will see that Alessandro didn’t follow the instructions. I don’t think it can keep making aligners for free if the patient doesn’t wear them.”];]

That was when a lightbulb went on for me. Until then, I had continued to present each new scan as an option included in the treatment, without realizing that this apparent lack of limits had helped erase the consequences of failing to follow the plan. Alessandro’s father, on the other hand, had spontaneously identified something I had not yet managed to turn into a communication strategy: if a new set produces no significant improvement, or if the situation has actually worsened, it cannot continue to be treated as a normal refinement included in the treatment plan. I replied:

#dialogo[#emph[“That’s exactly right. The company will compare this scan with the previous one and see that the last set produced no significant improvement. If we need to make more aligners, there will be a cost for the new phase.”];]

That answer came to me spontaneously during the conversation, but it helped me understand something much more important. Up to that point, Alessandro had been given five new chances, each accompanied by advice and promises, but none had been tied to a concrete consequence. For the first time, the possibility of a cost made it clear that a new scan could not once again erase what had happened over the preceding months.

So it was not an economic theory that suggested this strategy to me, but the spontaneous observation of a father who, seeing that his son’s teeth had gotten worse, thought it entirely natural that poor adherence should finally have a consequence.

#sezione[When a Second Chance Becomes the Rule]

Some patients admit they have not followed the plan very well and accompany each request for another chance with a credible explanation and a seemingly sincere promise. Others continue to insist they have worn their aligners correctly even when a comparison of the scans tells a completely different story. After repeated fresh starts, however, determining which account is more sincere becomes less important than breaking the pattern by which both automatically lead to the same outcome: another set of aligners.

The honest patient may learn that all they need to do is acknowledge their difficulty and promise to change, while the less honest one may realize that continuing to deny it leads to no different consequence. The problem, then, is not just what the patient says, but how our practice responds. #strong[If every explanation, whether true or false, is followed by a new scan and another fresh start under the same terms, we are the ones making the patient’s adherence matter less and less.];

The first time, we may have offered a second chance; by the second or third time, though, we may have unwittingly begun to teach a rule: #strong[following the treatment plan is certainly preferable, but it is not really essential, because starting over will always be an option.];

This is where the problem also becomes interesting from a behavioral perspective. Every clinical decision we make has a consequence, and that consequence can influence what happens next. When we say #emph[“Don’t worry, we’ll start over,”]; we want to reassure the patient and preserve the therapeutic relationship, but the message they may take away is different: what happened over the preceding months has no concrete consequence, because a new set will let them start over anyway.

This is one of the principles underlying #strong[operant conditioning];: the consequences of a behavior help determine how likely it is to be repeated. Of course, we are not intentionally rewarding poor adherence, but without realizing it, we can create a system in which its consequences are erased each time by a new scan. The same general principle underlies #emph[contingency management]; interventions, used in other health care settings to promote adherence through consequences established in advance and linked to verifiable behaviors. This does not prove that introducing a cost will automatically improve aligner wear, but it supports a much more cautious principle that is useful for our protocol: #strong[consequences work best when they are clear, communicated in advance, and tied to something verifiable.];

When treatments include additional sets without a new production charge, a second problem arises. Clinically, having those sets available is a tremendous advantage, but from the patient’s perspective, it can gradually make the difference between completing a set and having to repeat it because they did not actually wear it less clear. Research on the #emph[zero\-price effect]; shows that something priced at zero is not simply perceived as costing very little; it is evaluated differently. This does not mean that something free is automatically considered worthless and, above all, it does not mean that a patient wears their aligners less because they know refinements are included. The problem is subtler: #strong[if every fresh start seems free, automatic, and potentially unlimited, the concrete consequence of not completing the previous set becomes less apparent.];

A concept used in health economics that can help us understand this mechanism is moral hazard. The classic example is insurance: when someone knows they are protected from the financial consequences of a possible loss, they may have less incentive to prevent it than when they know they will bear those consequences directly. This does not mean they intentionally cause the loss or act in bad faith; it simply means that having protection can change behavior. In our case, the mechanism may be similar. A patient does not necessarily decide to wear their aligners less because they know they can receive more, but they may gradually learn that poor adherence does not really change what happens: if they do not wear one set, another will simply be made. #strong[The problem, then, is not that patients knowingly take advantage of the system, but that the system ends up making poor adherence almost consequence\-free.];

And it would be too easy to place all the responsibility on the patient, because we clinicians are often the ones who unwittingly reinforce this pattern. We schedule a ten\-minute appointment to check tracking and discover that the patient has not followed the plan, the teeth have not improved, and they cannot continue with the set. At that point, we should stop, document the situation, show the comparison with the previous scan, and have a potentially uncomfortable conversation. But to avoid it, the easiest solution is to take another scan. It takes only a few minutes, seems to resolve the problem, and lets us put everything off until the new set arrives. #strong[Taking a scan is easy; changing behavior is much harder.]; And so what seems like the quickest solution during the appointment may become the least efficient one over the following months: the patient avoids facing the consequences of not following the plan, we put off the problem, and a few months later we risk finding ourselves in exactly the same situation.

This does not mean it is wrong to offer a second chance. Orthodontic treatment can last many months, and during that time, personal, family, or work problems can temporarily make it harder to follow the plan. Being understanding also means recognizing these circumstances and accepting that one difficult period need not jeopardize the entire treatment.

#strong[The problem is not offering a second chance. The problem is offering one without making it clear that it is a second chance.];

If all we do is take another scan and ask for another promise, nothing distinguishes that fresh start from any that may follow. Instead, we can accept the patient’s explanation while introducing the rule that will guide the rest of treatment:

#dialogo[#emph[“I understand you’ve been through a difficult time, and this time we can start over. But I need to explain that this new scan became necessary mainly because you didn’t wear the aligners as planned. When you finish the next set, we’ll compare the new position of your teeth with where they are today, and we’ll need to see a significant improvement consistent with following the treatment plan. If we don’t see that improvement, we won’t be able to automatically treat another set as a normal refinement that’s included in your treatment.”];]

This wording does not blame the patient or turn the refinement into a punishment. #strong[It gives them another chance while establishing that the chance has a limit and, above all, defining in advance how we will determine whether they made use of it.];

#sezione[From the Patient’s Account to a Verifiable Result]

Alessandro’s case, and especially his father’s words, helped me turn an insight into a very simple rule that I began using in these situations and have used ever since: when I offer another chance, I no longer want to assess only the patient’s promise, but the result that promise will have produced. That is why I explain from the outset that we will compare the next scan with the starting scan, and that we will be looking not for every individual movement to be perfect, but for clinically significant overall progress consistent with wearing the aligners as prescribed.

After all, we know that even a patient who follows the treatment plan perfectly can reach the end of a set with an incomplete rotation, a movement still to be completed, or a tooth that has lost tracking. Likewise, a patient who previously almost never wore their aligners may genuinely have increased their wear time and feel that they are now following the plan adequately. That improvement deserves recognition, but the measure cannot be only how much the patient has improved compared with before. It must also be whether their behavior now meets the demands of treatment. Someone can have come a long way from where they started and still not be wearing their aligners enough for treatment to progress as it should.

This led me to a distinction that has become essential. If the next scan shows clear overall progress and only a few movements remain incomplete, I am looking at a normal refinement: the patient has done their part, and now it is up to me to complete the treatment. If, instead, I see only marginal movement, no substantial progress, or even a worsening, the situation is entirely different. I am no longer refining treatment the patient has followed; I am trying to repeat a phase they did not really carry through. #strong[The criterion, then, is not whether every movement was perfect, but whether the patient actually wore the set and achieved a result consistent with following the treatment plan adequately.];

Comparing the scans does not, of course, tell me how many hours the patient actually wore their aligners, nor is it meant to. But it lets me shift the conversation to something else: I no longer have to debate whether they wore them for eighteen, twenty, or twenty\-two hours, or try to determine how truthful their account is, because I can compare where they started with where they ended up. In other words, #strong[I stop debating the promise and start looking at the result.];

Once we have established that the new scan must show significant improvement, we also need to make clear what will happen if it does not. #strong[We cannot continue indefinitely to treat every new set as a normal refinement included in treatment];, and this rule can be tied to the aligner manufacturer or presented as a clear practice policy. In the first case, we reduce the chance that the patient will interpret the decision as an arbitrary punishment imposed by the clinician: the new scan is presented not simply as a picture we can use to start over, but as objective documentation that can be compared with the previous scan to assess the result achieved.

The rule can be explained this way:

#dialogo[#emph[“When we send in the next scan, the company will be able to compare it with today’s scan and assess the overall result. Every tooth will not need to have completed every planned movement perfectly, but there will need to be significant improvement consistent with wearing the aligners as prescribed. If that improvement is clear, we can address any incomplete movements through a normal refinement. But if progress is only marginal, there is no substantial improvement, or your teeth have actually gotten worse, the company may not consider the new set a normal refinement included in treatment, so additional aligners could carry an extra cost.”];]

Alternatively, the clinician can present the rule as a practice policy:

#dialogo[#emph[“This time, we can take a new scan and start over, but at the end of the next set, we’ll need to see significant improvement consistent with wearing the aligners as prescribed. If treatment has progressed and only a few movements remain incomplete, we can complete them through a normal refinement. But if there is no substantial progress, we cannot keep repeating the same phase. We’ll have to consider either stopping treatment or continuing with a new plan and an additional cost.”];]

Charging an additional fee is not a punishment. It makes a concrete distinction between two situations that are already different clinically: completing a treatment the patient has actually followed, and repeating a phase that produced no results because the aligners were not worn as prescribed.

As we saw in Chapter 6, when we first encounter a widespread loss of tracking, we focus primarily on making the impact on #strong[time]; tangible: if weekly changes are not enough, the time needed for each aligner may increase, along with the overall length of treatment. But when a patient has already started over two, three, or four times, time as a motivator may gradually lose its effectiveness, because waiting longer and starting over have become routine. This is where the #strong[investment]; as a motivator comes into play—not to threaten the patient or make them pay for not following instructions, but to make it clear that another fresh start is not automatic and that repeating a phase they did not complete is not the same as finishing a few remaining movements.

For this consequence to be fair and effective, however, it must be communicated #strong[before]; the new set, not introduced at the next appointment. If the patient knows about it before starting over, it is a clear condition of the new chance we are offering them; if they learn about it only after once again failing to follow the plan, they may see it as a rule invented after the fact. The crucial step, then, is to replace a promise with a verifiable condition. We no longer ask, #emph[“Do you promise you’ll follow the plan this time?”];—because we have probably heard that promise before. Instead, we explain that continuing under the same terms will depend on the result we can see in the next scan.

A new scan captures the current position of the teeth and allows us to prepare more aligners, but it does not restore the time lost and, above all, it does not, by itself, change the behavior that caused the problem. If we keep starting over without changing the conditions, we repeat the same treatment while relying once again on the same promise. A refinement retains its full value when it completes a course of treatment the patient has actually followed. When it is used to repeatedly redo phases the patient never completed, it ceases to be only a tool for clinical precision and risks becoming a way to compensate for the patient’s failure to follow the plan.

#strong[Included refinements are meant to complete a treatment the patient has followed, not to indefinitely replace one they never really followed.];

Being understanding does not mean eliminating every consequence. We can offer a second chance, and in many cases we should. But for it to remain a second chance, it cannot become an unlimited number of chances.

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

At this point, the conversation with the patient must turn what we have just explained into a simple, understandable rule established before the new set begins. We should not ask for another promise or try to get the patient to admit they have not followed the plan. We need to make clear what result we expect and what will happen if they do not achieve it.

#script[
#emph[“This time, we can take a new scan and start over, but I need to explain an important rule. When you finish this set, we’ll compare the new scan with today’s scan, and it will need to show significant improvement consistent with wearing the aligners as prescribed. That doesn’t mean every tooth has to reach its planned position perfectly, because some movements can be less predictable. But the treatment as a whole will need to have made real progress.];

#emph[If you’ve followed the plan and only a few movements remain incomplete, we can proceed with a normal refinement. But if there’s no substantial improvement, or your teeth have actually gotten worse, the company may not consider the new set a normal refinement included in treatment, so additional aligners could carry an extra cost. This isn’t a punishment. It simply means we wouldn’t be completing a treatment you’d followed; we’d be repeating, yet again, a phase you hadn’t carried through.”];
]

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[It Breaks the Cycle of Unintended Reinforcement]

If every time a patient fails to follow the plan, they automatically receive a new set, #strong[the chance to start over without any concrete consequence may encourage the same pattern to continue];. Establishing a limit in advance breaks that pattern without turning the appointment into a punishment.

#perche-voce[It Makes the Consequence Clear]

A general recommendation to follow the plan more closely remains abstract, whereas #strong[the possibility that another set may no longer qualify as a normal refinement included in treatment]; makes the difference between completing a treatment and having to repeat a phase immediately clear.

#perche-voce[It Shifts the Focus from Promises to Results]

Comparing the scans keeps us from basing the decision solely on what the patient says and introduces an observable clinical criterion, without claiming to measure wear time directly.

#perche-voce[It Uses the Investment as a Motivator]

When time as a motivator is no longer enough to change a repeated behavior, the possibility of an additional cost gives the new chance weight and makes it harder to take for granted.

#perche-voce[It Preserves the Therapeutic Relationship]

The consequence is communicated in advance, tied #strong[to criteria established before the new set];, and presented as a condition for continuing treatment, so the patient does not interpret it as a personal decision or an emotional reaction on the clinician’s part.

