#capitolo(numero: "10", etichetta: [Chapter 10], titolo: [The Patient Who Disappears], epigrafe: [“Lost time is never found again.”], fonte: [Benjamin Franklin])

Some patients decide to stop or suspend treatment and tell us so openly: they come to the office, explain why they do not want to or cannot continue at that point, and we decide together how to handle the situation. #strong[In my office, when a patient decides to suspend treatment, we have them sign a treatment suspension consent form that makes it explicit that treatment is being interrupted and defines the conditions under which it may be resumed.]; That leaves no ambiguity: we know treatment is suspended, and the patient knows they are not simply missing a few appointments. Then there is another category of patients, probably much more common than we would like, who never actually stop treatment, at least not in words, but simply stop coming in at some point and disappear.

At first, it does not even seem like a particularly serious problem. The patient misses an appointment, the front desk calls them, and often gets a perfectly reassuring response: #emph[“Yes, I know. I’m sorry, things are just crazy for me right now. I’ll call you as soon as I can.”]; Of course, they do not call, and after a few weeks the office tries to reach them again. This time they might say: #emph[“Look, I really can’t right now. Maybe next month.”]; Another month passes, then two. At first we keep trying to reach them, but eventually the attempts inevitably become less frequent, because we have other patients to care for, other calls to make, and other problems to solve. Without anyone ever formally deciding to stop treatment, that name simply disappears from the schedule and, as time goes by, from our day\-to\-day work as well.

A year may pass, or two, three, or even four, until one morning the receptionist comes into my office and says: #emph[“Remember Mario Rossi? He called because he’d like to make an appointment to continue treatment.”]; And that word, #emph[continue];, contains almost the entire problem this chapter addresses, in my opinion.

From his point of view, treatment may never really have ended. It was simply suspended, almost like a movie he paused and now thinks he can resume from exactly the same frame. He knows he has not come in for three years, of course, but that does not necessarily mean he saw that long absence as the end of treatment. He may believe that what he bought and paid for is still there, waiting for him.

The problem is that his mouth did not stay on pause during those three years. His teeth may have moved, his bite may have changed, he may have worn his last aligners or retainer unpredictably, restorations, cavities, or periodontal problems may have appeared, and even what he wants may differ from what we agreed on at the start. The clinical, logistical, and financial conditions under which we planned that treatment may also have changed.

#strong[Time does not freeze orthodontic treatment.];

Beckwith and, later, Skidmore showed that the duration of orthodontic treatment is also influenced by factors related to patient adherence and continuity of care; Melo and colleagues highlighted the impact of missed appointments among adult patients, while Bichara and colleagues observed treatment times nearly a year longer in patients who had missed more than two appointments. The literature thus confirms something we know well from clinical experience: losing continuity is not simply a scheduling problem.

But one question remains that the literature cannot answer for us, and it is the real problem this chapter addresses: #strong[how long can a treatment remain open when the patient has simply disappeared?]; When that patient reappears after three years saying he wants to “continue,” before we even decide which aligners to order, we need to understand something more important: #strong[are we really continuing the old treatment, or has what we are dealing with become a new treatment in the meantime?];

#sezione[The Five\-Year Misunderstanding]

In the world of aligners, there is a very concrete reason this expectation may have developed, and if we are being fair, at least some of the responsibility is probably ours. Many commercial packages allow the practice to request additional sets of aligners at no extra cost within a specified manufacturer’s ordering window, under terms that vary from company to company. In some cases, that window may extend to five years, for example.

The problem arises when we decide to explain this to the patient and, as inevitably happens in everyday communication, try to simplify it. Many of us have probably said at least once something like, #emph[“With this treatment, your aligners are included for five years,”]; or, even more simply, #emph[“You have five years of treatment.”]; We know what we mean, but a statement that, to us, describes a commercial term can easily turn into something very different in the patient’s mind: #emph[“I paid for a treatment that lasts five years, so until then, whatever happens is included.”];

It is understandable, then, that a patient might think they can stop for a few months, then a year, perhaps disappear altogether, and finally reappear before the deadline simply asking to resume. This is where the misunderstanding lies, because #strong[the manufacturer’s ordering window available to the practice and the clinical duration of treatment are not the same thing];. The first determines how long the practice can request additional sets of aligners at no extra cost; the second is the time needed to complete treatment.

Imagine a patient who reappears after four years with a package that has a five\-year window. We might still be able to order a new set, but we need to reassess the patient, perform a new intraoral scan, replan the case, and, above all, determine how much treatment remains. If another eighteen months are needed, that window could close while the patient is still in treatment.

That is why I think the real mistake is telling patients they have “five years of treatment,” because #strong[we are not selling them five years of time];. Those five years represent a manufacturer’s ordering window, not the duration of treatment.

#sezione[When They Return, We Don’t Yet Know Whether We’re Continuing Treatment]

When a patient reappears after three or four years, the most natural impulse is to reopen the old treatment plan immediately and figure out which aligner they can start with. After all, we already know that patient, and we still have photos, scans, and the digital treatment setup. The problem is that everything we see on the screen belongs to the patient as they were three or four years ago, while the patient in the chair is the patient as they are today.

During that time, their teeth may have shifted, their bite may have changed, dental or periodontal problems may have developed, and often we do not know exactly what they did with their last aligners. They may have stopped wearing them after a few weeks, kept wearing the same one for years, worn them only at night as a sort of retainer, or continued switching on their own to the aligners they still had at home. What they want may have changed, too: three years ago, we might have been close to finishing and they were satisfied; today, they may return asking us to change something that was not even part of the goals agreed on at the start.

So the first question should not be #emph[“Which aligner can we start with?”]; but rather, #strong[“Where are we today, and what do we need to do from here?”]; Before deciding whether we are continuing the old treatment, we need to examine the patient again, piece together what happened during the absence, assess the current clinical situation, and compare it with the situation when we last saw them.

The old scan, the old digital treatment setup, and all the files remain extremely useful because they allow us to reconstruct where we started and how far we got. #strong[It is not the file that has “expired”; it is the clinical situation that may have changed.]; That is why I consider an appointment after a long absence a full reassessment, not simply the first checkup after a break: #strong[first we reassess the patient, and only then do we decide whether what we are about to do can still be called “continuing.”];

At this point, we might be tempted to establish a rule that applies to everyone: after twelve months, treatment is considered interrupted; after two years, resuming it carries a new charge; after three, we start from scratch. It would be simple, but I don’t think the time elapsed can be the only criterion, because three years away can tell completely different stories. One patient may have struggled with adherence from the start, repeatedly missed checkups, and gradually abandoned a treatment that was already progressing with difficulty; another may have followed the plan perfectly until an illness, a serious family problem, or a move made it genuinely impossible to continue. The same three years may have passed on the calendar, but that does not necessarily mean we should make the same decision.

This is not about deciding who deserves to be rewarded or punished, but about understanding #strong[how much of the work I need to do today can still reasonably fall within the commitment I made at the start, and how much requires a new commitment from the practice];. This is also how we need to approach the financial question: #strong[any new charge is not meant to make the patient pay for what happened in the past, but for the work needed from today onward];.

In my practice, I group these returning patients into three broad scenarios, not as rigid rules, but as criteria that help me stay reasonably consistent in my decisions.

In the #strong[first scenario];, I still consider it reasonable to resume treatment under the original financial terms. This might be a patient who followed the plan well before the interruption, had to stop for a serious and unforeseen reason, and returns with a clinical situation that is still fairly easy to get back on track. I may decide to take on the work required because I consider what I need to do still close enough to the commitment I made at the start.

In the #strong[second scenario];, the original treatment can still be brought back on track, but the situation has become more complex. Several years have passed, I need to replan a substantial part of the case, or I am approaching the end of the original manufacturer’s ordering window. I may decide to restart with very clear limits or ask the patient to contribute an amount proportionate to the new work required. I don’t think it makes sense to set a universal amount: what I consider essential is that the patient know the terms #strong[before]; starting again.

In the #strong[third scenario];, finally, the connection to the original treatment has become too tenuous to call it simply a continuation. Many years may have passed, the clinical situation may be significantly different, much of what we achieved may have been lost, or I may need to redo the diagnosis, treatment planning, and treatment to a substantial degree. In that case, I think it is more appropriate to propose a new course of treatment and establish a new financial agreement. Even in this third scenario, the message remains the same: I am not asking the patient to pay because he disappeared, but because #strong[what I need to do today is no longer simply a continuation of what we left unfinished back then];.

#sezione[Warn the Patient as They’re Drifting Away]

So far, we have focused mainly on what to do when the patient reappears, but an important part of the problem begins much earlier, while they are gradually dropping out of follow\-up care. That is precisely when we can prevent many future misunderstandings.

Of course, our first goal must be to get the patient back into a regular treatment routine. A systematic review and meta\-analysis by Mohammed and colleagues showed that reminder systems can improve attendance at orthodontic appointments and reduce the risk of missed appointments. But when absences start to recur, what we say on the phone needs to change: it is no longer enough to ask when the patient wants to schedule the next checkup. We need to explain that a long interruption has consequences. The front desk staff might say:

#dialogo[#emph[“I understand that it’s hard for you to come in right now, but we can’t leave your treatment on hold without checkups for too long. If the interruption continues, the doctor will need to reassess your situation when you’re ready to resume, and the financial terms of your treatment may change as well. That’s why it’s best to work out together now how to handle this period.”];]

I would rather the patient hear this while the problem is developing than have to explain it three years later. It is not a threat, but advance notice of a real consequence that lets them decide whether to make arrangements to continue or agree with us on a formal suspension. #strong[If they decide to suspend treatment, at my practice we formalize that decision by having them sign a treatment suspension consent form];, so it is clear to both of us that treatment is not simply being left open until the patient decides on their own when to return. In fact, we should explain this from the very beginning:

#dialogo[#emph[“If something comes up during treatment and you need to stop for a while, that’s no problem, but don’t disappear. Call us, and we’ll decide together how to handle it, because leaving treatment on hold for a long time without checkups can change both the clinical situation and the financial terms under which we can resume.”];]

The problem, then, is not that a patient may need to stop, but that they do so without telling us. If they let us know, we can assess the situation, protect what we have achieved, and agree on a suspension. If, instead, they keep missing appointments and gradually stop responding as well, their silence cannot become an implicit way to keep treatment open indefinitely.

After making reasonable attempts to bring the patient back, I think it is useful to send a written notice making clear that, without the necessary checkups, we are no longer able to actively supervise treatment, and that returning after a long interruption will require a new clinical evaluation and a review of the terms under which treatment can resume. In the same notice, we can tell the patient what to do with any aligners and other appliances they still have and remind them not to continue treatment on their own. All attempts to contact the patient and all important communications should be documented in the patient’s chart. If the patient returns after three years, we need to be able to reconstruct when continuity of care was lost, what attempts were made to restore it, and what information the patient was given as treatment was being interrupted.

#strong[The interruption needs to be made clear while it is happening, not reconstructed three years later.];

#sezione[When the Patient Reappears]

When, perhaps after three years, the patient calls saying they want to #emph[“continue treatment,”]; the front desk staff should not decide whether that treatment is still open, whether it is still covered, or how much it will cost to resume, because at that point we do not yet have the information to know. Likewise, I would avoid getting into a discussion on the phone about what was said years earlier or why the patient disappeared. The response can be very simple:

#dialogo[#emph[“Of course, let’s schedule an appointment with the doctor. It’s been quite a while since your last checkup, so first we need to reassess the situation. Then the doctor will explain how we can resume and under what terms.”];]

When the patient comes in, I piece together what has happened since I last saw them, assess the current clinical situation, compare it with the situation when we left off, and determine how much of the original treatment can still be brought back on track. #strong[Only then do I decide what solution to propose and under what terms we can restart.]; That way, any new charge is presented not as a consequence of the patient’s disappearance, but as a charge for #strong[the work needed today to complete treatment.];

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

Once I have decided which of the three scenarios applies, I try to follow the same sequence every time: I start with the situation today, explain what needs to be done to finish treatment, and only then clarify the terms under which we can restart.

If we can resume under the original terms:

#script[
#emph[“I’ve reassessed everything, and we can still resume treatment under the terms we agreed on at the start. From this point on, though, we need to keep treatment on track. If something happens that prevents you from continuing, call us and we’ll decide together how to handle it, but we can’t leave treatment on hold for years again.”];
]

If the original treatment can be brought back on track but we are approaching the limits of the original terms:

#script[
#emph[“We can still resume, but we’re now close to the end of the manufacturer’s ordering window under your original package. If we can complete treatment within that window, nothing changes. If you need more aligners after that date, though, there may be additional costs. I’d rather tell you now, before we start again.”];
]

If what we need to do has become a new treatment:

#script[
#emph[“Several years have passed, and the situation today is different from where we left off. To achieve the outcome, I can’t simply order more aligners and pick up where we stopped. I need to replan the case and begin a new course of treatment. That’s why what I’m proposing today is a new treatment, with a new financial agreement.”];
]

In none of these three cases do I feel the need to begin by reminding the patient how many appointments they missed, how many times the front desk staff called, or how many times they promised to return. That information needs to be documented in the patient’s chart, but using it to open the conversation only risks turning it into a debate over who is right and who is wrong. #strong[What matters to me is bringing the conversation back to the present: this is the situation we have today, this is what we need to do, and these are the terms under which we can do it.];

After all, one of the principles we have already encountered applies here, too: #strong[a confession doesn’t move teeth];. I don’t need the patient to admit they were wrong; I need them to understand where we stand today and, if we decide to restart, know exactly under what terms we are doing so.

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[It Shifts the Discussion from the Past to the Present]

We do not try to determine who was right about what happened during the years the patient was away. We start with the current clinical situation and what needs to be done from this point forward, so the conversation does not turn into an argument about who was responsible for what happened in the past.

#perche-voce[It Removes Ambiguity About Restarting]

The fact that treatment was never formally concluded does not mean it can be restarted at any time under the original terms. The reassessment establishes how much of the original treatment can still be brought back on track and under what terms we can restart.

#perche-voce[It Ties the Cost to Future Work]

Any new charge is presented not as a penalty for disappearing, but as a consequence of the clinical and administrative work, and the expense, required from that point forward. This lets us use the investment as a motivator without turning it into a punishment.

#perche-voce[It Gives Advance Notice of the Consequences]

Warning the patient while they are falling out of a regular treatment routine lets them know that a prolonged absence may change both the clinical situation and the terms under which they can resume. A consequence communicated in advance is very different from a condition imposed when the patient returns years later.

So when I see that name on my schedule again after years have passed, I do not automatically consider the treatment we left unfinished to be reopened. Instead, I consider a possibility reopened: the possibility of seeing the patient again, understanding where we stand today, and deciding together whether and how we can restart.

#strong[The patient can disappear. Treatment cannot remain open forever.];

