#capitolo(numero: "11", etichetta: [Chapter 11], titolo: [The Patient Who’s Never Satisfied], epigrafe: [“Nothing is enough for the man to whom enough is too little.”], fonte: [Epicurus])

Over the years, I have learned to pay particular attention to a certain kind of patient. They are not necessarily difficult patients. They are not the ones who fail to wear their aligners, nor are they the ones who miss appointments or routinely forget their orthodontic elastics. In fact, they are often extremely attentive to their treatment: they constantly examine their teeth, compare photos taken weeks apart, and arrive at appointments knowing exactly which detail they want to show me. The problem is that they are never completely satisfied.

Once we have corrected what brought them to us, their attention tends to shift to increasingly small details that they probably had not even noticed at first. Almost without realizing it, we risk turning treatment into a pursuit of perfection that becomes harder and harder to achieve and, sometimes, impossible.

In my experience, aligners have made this risk even more apparent. Digital simulations allow patients to examine the outcome in detail, photos are compared constantly, and the ability to plan further tooth movements can easily give the impression that there is always something more to improve. There is also a very simple difference from fixed braces: after many months of treatment, a patient with braces generally cannot wait to get them off, while continuing with aligners for a few more months is often seen as a much smaller sacrifice. The desire to finish, then, can weaken just as attention to ever smaller details grows.

For a long time, I thought this way too. If a patient showed me a detail that could be improved and I thought I could improve it, I tried to accommodate them. It seemed like the most logical choice: improve the outcome further and, at the same time, increase their satisfaction. Over time, I came to understand that those two things do not necessarily go together and that, with some patients, each additional correction may simply shift their attention to the next detail.

The question, then, is not just #strong[how much more we can improve an outcome];, but #strong[when continuing to pursue improvements stops being genuinely useful];.

#sezione[When Improvement Isn’t Enough]

Stefano was about forty when he came to my office, after consulting several other orthodontists. His malocclusion was far from simple: a Class II subdivision malocclusion, with Class I on the right and full Class II on the left, an almost 100% deep bite, and significant crowding in the lower arch.

I planned treatment with aligners and orthodontic elastics and explained that we would need approximately twelve to fifteen months. Stefano followed the plan, and by the end of the first set of aligners, the result was very good: we had achieved a perfect Class I relationship on the left as well, opened the bite, and obtained excellent alignment. At the time, I was still early in my experience treating complex cases with aligners, and that result meant something special to me. I was proud to have corrected a malocclusion that many colleagues would not even have considered treating with aligners.

Stefano, however, pointed out a small detail involving his lower left lateral incisor. He was right: that tooth could be improved, so we decided to do a refinement. I wanted to complete treatment as well as possible, but I also wanted Stefano to be fully satisfied. I thought the two went hand in hand: #strong[the more I improved the outcome, the more satisfied he would be.];

By the end of the first refinement, that detail had improved, but Stefano had begun focusing on another one. So we did another scan, then another refinement, and then yet another. Each time, we managed to improve a detail, but his attention shifted to something new. In the end, we did eight refinements, and treatment, which was supposed to take approximately twelve to fifteen months, stretched to twenty\-eight.

The paradox was clear: #strong[the clinical result kept improving, but Stefano’s satisfaction did not increase along with it];. Every time we corrected an imperfection, his attention shifted to an even smaller detail. Even after the eighth refinement, he was not completely satisfied. He said something like:

#dialogo[#emph[“We’re almost there now. There’s still one little thing we could improve, but treatment is taking too long, and I don’t want to keep going like this.”];]

So treatment did not end because Stefano was finally satisfied. It ended because, after twenty\-eight months, #strong[he was no longer willing to invest any more time in it];. If continuing had not come at that cost for him, we probably would have found something else to change.

By then, I was tired and frustrated too. We had achieved the clinical result we were aiming for long before, but every new detail had become reason enough to continue. I realized I had made a mistake: #strong[I had kept asking myself what else we could improve, without asking whether it really made sense to do so.]; Stefano taught me a lesson I have never forgotten:

#strong[Listening to the patient does not mean handing them the steering wheel for their treatment.];

#sezione[When the Point of Reference Changes]

At the start of treatment, a patient may be concerned about significant crowding, a deep bite, or an incisor that is noticeably out of position. Once those problems are corrected, though, their point of reference changes. As the months pass, they also tend to forget how different things looked at the start and end up comparing the result not with the smile they had when they first came to us, but with what could still be improved. A detail they probably would not even have noticed at first can become the new problem to correct.

The literature on smile perception also shows us that orthodontists and patients do not necessarily place the same importance on the same details, and that their thresholds for detecting small dental and gingival asymmetries differ. Studies using smile photographs, and more recently eye\-tracking systems, have shown just how selective our attention can become when we focus on increasingly small details.

In clinical practice, this has led me over the years to distinguish between two very different ways of looking at the same result, which I call #strong[social esthetics]; and #strong[inspection esthetics];. These are not terms from the literature, but two expressions I use to describe something I see constantly in my patients.

#strong[Social esthetics]; is how a smile looks in real life: when a person talks, laughs, moves, and interacts with others. No one examines a single incisor from a few inches away; we perceive the smile as a whole.

#strong[Inspection esthetics];, by contrast, comes into play when we stop looking at a smile and start scrutinizing it. The patient moves closer to the mirror, tilts their head, changes the lighting, enlarges a photo, or compares two selfies, looking for a difference. The longer they examine a detail, the more important that detail may become.

#strong[The problem is that correcting that detail does not necessarily put an end to this kind of scrutiny.]; Attention may simply shift to the next detail, exactly as it did with Stefano. The result keeps improving, but at the same time, it becomes increasingly difficult to reach a point where the patient can find nothing else to change.

That is why #strong[improving the result further does not necessarily increase satisfaction]; as well.

One situation in which this gap between the clinical result and the patient’s perception becomes especially clear is one I encounter fairly often at the end of treatment. I show the patient the final photos or digital scan, and as we look at them together, they too acknowledge that their teeth are properly aligned. Then they look in the mirror and tell me:

#dialogo[#emph[“They look straight on the screen, but when I look at myself, I still don’t like them.”];]

Simply telling them that their teeth are straight does not help. Their dissatisfaction is real, but that does not necessarily mean it still comes from the position of their teeth. What continues to bother them may have to do with the shape of the teeth, wear along their edges, anatomical differences between the two sides, how the teeth look in relation to the lips, or simply how they perceive their smile as a whole.

This is where it becomes essential to distinguish between two statements that sound similar: #emph[“My teeth aren’t straight”]; and #emph[“I don’t like my smile.”]; In the first case, we should be able to identify a tooth, its position, and the movement needed to change it. In the second, we are describing a broader esthetic perception that may not be improved by moving the teeth further. That is why, before planning another refinement, I ask the patient:

#dialogo[#emph[“Can you show me exactly which tooth bothers you and what you’d like to change? What would we need to see differently at the end of another refinement to say it worked?”];]

The answers to these questions change the situation completely. If we can identify a tooth, a movement, and an outcome we can assess, we can consider whether it makes sense to try to achieve it. But if the request remains #emph[“I’d like them to look better,”];  #emph[“I’d like them to be even straighter,”]; or #emph[“I’d like to like the way I look more,”];  #strong[we still have not identified an orthodontic goal on which to base a new phase of treatment];.

We can plan a tooth movement, but we cannot plan how the patient will see themselves in the mirror. Continuing to move teeth in the hope of changing a feeling we have not been able to translate into a precise goal may simply lead us to another refinement and, a few months later, the same conversation. So in these cases, I try not to dismiss what the patient is feeling, but to clarify the limits of what another set of aligners can actually do:

#dialogo[#emph[“I’m not questioning what you see when you look in the mirror. But we need to understand exactly what bothers you and whether it has to do with the position of your teeth. If we can identify a specific movement that might improve it, we can consider it. But if we can’t define what we would need to change, another set of aligners might move your teeth further without changing what you don’t like right now.”];]

Listening to the patient’s request and trying to understand it is essential. In recent years, we have rightly worked to involve patients more and more in treatment decisions: they need to understand the goals of treatment, be able to express their expectations, and discuss with us the options for achieving those goals. Involving them, though, does not mean that every request must automatically become a new phase of treatment. We remain responsible for assessing whether what they want to correct is a clinically appropriate goal and whether the expected benefit justifies continuing treatment.

For a long time, at the end of a refinement, I too would ask almost automatically: #emph[“Is there anything else you’d like to change?”]; It seems like an innocent question, but it already contains a suggestion: #strong[that there is still something to look for];. For a patient who pays particularly close attention to details, it can almost become an invitation to examine their smile even more closely until they find a new imperfection.

Over time, I have changed how I handle the final checkups. First, I perform my clinical evaluation and determine whether we have achieved the agreed\-upon goals, checking the occlusion, alignment, overjet, overbite, contacts, and all the other elements needed to consider the active phase of treatment complete. #strong[Only then do I involve the patient in the final assessment];, show them what we have achieved, and listen to anything they still want to discuss.

The difference may seem small, but it completely changes the meaning of the conversation. The patient remains involved and still has all the space they need to express what they see and how they feel, #strong[but we are not asking them to look for something wrong];. Our responsibility remains to assess whether what they are showing us is still a clinical goal that justifies continuing treatment.

One of the things I now do most often with these patients is #strong[take them back to the starting point];. With Stefano, I should have done it much sooner: instead of examining every new detail with him, I should have stepped back and shown him how much the result had changed from where we started.

So I bring out the initial photos and compare them with the current ones. I do not do this to prove to the patient that they should be satisfied, because satisfaction cannot be imposed, but #strong[to put the detail they are now examining from a few inches away alongside the overall problem we had decided to treat at the start.];

#dialogo[#emph[“Let’s look at the result as a whole. This is where we started, and these were the goals we set. We’ve achieved them. I see the detail you’re showing me too, but we need to ask whether changing it would provide a real benefit or whether we’re trying to improve something that is already clinically sound.”];]

That way, I do not dismiss what the patient sees or tell them the detail does not exist. #strong[I help them see the treatment as a whole again];, after months in which their attention has gradually narrowed to smaller and smaller details.

At this point, though, we need to understand what the patient’s request to continue really means, because not all requests mean the same thing.

If one of the goals we agreed on has not been achieved, there is no question in my mind: treatment needs to be completed, and if another refinement is necessary, I am the first to propose it.

Then there is the opposite situation, in which the patient’s request #strong[does not correspond to a specific orthodontic goal or would require a movement I consider unnecessary, unpredictable, or clinically inadvisable];. In that case, I do not proceed, regardless of their willingness to continue.

But there is a third possibility, and it is the one that most often creates difficulties with a patient who is never satisfied. #strong[The treatment goals have been achieved, and I do not consider another refinement necessary, but the patient points out another detail—one that is real, can be changed, and is clinically acceptable—that they still want to address.];

This is the hardest situation to manage, because I cannot tell the patient that the detail they are showing me does not exist or that it cannot be changed. I can, however, explain that #strong[completing the treatment goals is one thing; deciding to continue beyond those goals in pursuit of further improvement is another];.

#sezione[When Explanations Are Not Enough]

For a long time, I tried to handle these situations primarily through communication. I showed the initial photos, reminded the patient of the goals we had agreed on, compared where we started with the result we had achieved, and explained why another refinement would not necessarily produce a significant improvement. All of this remains essential and is still the first part of my approach today, but I realized it was not enough with some patients. I could spend a great deal of time explaining that we had achieved the goals and that the detail we were looking at did not warrant another phase of treatment, but in the end, the question was almost always the same:

#dialogo[#emph[“I understand, Doctor, but if we can still try, why wouldn’t we?”];]

From the patient’s perspective, it was a perfectly logical question. If continuing meant no additional payment, if wearing aligners for a few more months was no longer a particularly significant sacrifice, and if we could produce another set anyway, #strong[why stop?]; Even if the expected benefit was small and uncertain, the perceived cost of trying was practically zero.

That was the part I had failed to understand for a long time. I kept looking for better arguments to explain why stopping made sense, while the patient continued to see a chance for improvement that, from their perspective, had almost no downside. As long as the two options were #emph[stop]; or #emph[try again for free];, even a very small detail could seem like reason enough to choose the latter. I realized, then, that in this specific situation, explanations alone were not enough. #strong[I had to make the decision to continue concrete.];

In my practice, I chose to make that decision concrete by introducing a fee for the additional phase of treatment, generally #strong[1,000 euros.]; The amount itself is not the point, of course, and I do not mean to suggest a rule that applies to everyone: each colleague can set an amount that fits their own practice. What matters is that the amount be significant enough to turn what had, until then, seemed like a simple opportunity to #emph[“try again”]; into a real decision. I want to be very clear about this, because #strong[I do not introduce the fee to sell another refinement; on the contrary, when I introduce it, I hope the patient will decide not to proceed. Its purpose is deliberately to discourage them];.

Until then, the patient had been asking themselves, #emph[“If we can still try, why should we stop?”]; Once continuing requires a new investment, the question becomes, #emph[“Is this detail really important enough to justify another 1,000 euros and a few more months of treatment?”]; That is exactly the shift I am looking for: the detail is the same, and the movement we might try to achieve is the same, but #strong[the value the patient places on that detail changes when continuing is no longer without consequences];. In fact, very often, after I explain the fee, the patient looks at their smile again and asks me, #emph[“But do you really think it’s worth it?”]; To me, that is the moment the strategy has achieved its goal, because the patient is finally asking not just whether they can continue, but how important continuing really is to them.

Of course, a willingness to pay this fee does not make every request acceptable. This option applies only to the situation we have just described: the treatment goals have been achieved, and the requested change is not necessary, but it is specific, clinically acceptable, and reasonably achievable. If I consider the movement unnecessary, unpredictable, or potentially harmful, I do not proceed, regardless of the patient’s willingness to pay. Likewise, if the patient continues to say only #emph[“I don’t like them”]; without being able to identify what they want to change, we still do not have a goal on which to base a new phase of treatment. A fee does not make an inadvisable movement advisable: it comes into play only after we have established that the change can be made but is not needed to complete treatment.

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

When I consider the treatment goals achieved but the patient points out another detail they would like to change, the conversation might go something like this:

#script[
#emph[“Let’s look together at where we started and the goals we set. I see the detail you’re showing me too, and we can try to change it, but from my point of view, another refinement is not needed to complete treatment, because we’ve achieved the goals we agreed on.];

#emph[If I thought there was still something we needed to correct, I would be the first to propose another set of aligners, and of course there would be no additional charge. In this case, though, we’re talking about a further improvement you’d like beyond the result we’ve already achieved.];

#emph[If this detail is important enough to you that you want to continue, we can consider it, provided we can define exactly what we want to change and the movement is clinically acceptable and reasonably predictable. In my practice, this additional phase costs 1,000 euros.];

#emph[I’m not recommending that you do it; on the contrary, my recommendation is that we stop. I’m simply giving you the option to decide whether this detail is important enough to you to justify a new investment and a few more months of treatment.”];
]

If, at this point, the patient asks me, #emph[“But do you really think it’s worth it?”]; my answer must be consistent with what I have just explained. If I consider the goals achieved and the expected benefit marginal, I tell them exactly that. #strong[The fee is not meant to sell the refinement, but to make the choice to proceed with it real.];

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[It Brings the Comparison Back to the Starting Point]

Over time, patients tend to get used to the result they have achieved and gradually focus more on what could still be improved. Bringing out the initial photos is not meant to convince them that they should be satisfied, but to keep the last small detail from becoming the only measure by which they judge the entire treatment.

#perche-voce[It Avoids Suggesting That There Must Be Something to Correct]

The question “Is there anything else you want to change?” implicitly invites the patient to look for something. That is why I prefer to perform my clinical evaluation first and only then listen to any concerns the patient may have. I do not reduce their involvement, but I avoid turning the final checkup into a systematic search for the next imperfection.

#perche-voce[It Turns an Option into a Decision]

When another refinement seems available with no consequences, even a very small benefit can seem like reason enough to try. Introducing a fee changes that assessment because it brings back into view certain factors that the option to continue had gradually pushed into the background: time, effort, and above all, how much the patient truly values the detail they want to change.

The point is not to persuade the patient with money, but to interrupt the automatic “we can try again” response. That is why the amount must be significant enough to require a deliberate choice, while remaining, of course, a practice decision that each professional must adapt to their own circumstances.

#perche-voce[It Keeps Desire Separate from Clinical Indication]

The patient’s willingness to pay the fee does not change our clinical judgment. First, we must establish that the request is specific, clinically acceptable, and reasonably achievable; only then can we discuss a possible additional phase. #strong[The availability of more aligners is not, by itself, an indication to use them.];

