#capitolo(numero: "12", etichetta: [Chapter 12], titolo: [The 95% Patient], epigrafe: [“The best is the enemy of the good.”], fonte: [Voltaire])

Some patients make it clear as soon as they sit down in the chair that we have a complex treatment ahead of us. Then there are others who give us exactly the opposite impression. They smile, and we almost wonder why they came in: their teeth are already straight, their bite is good, their smile is attractive, and they may even have had orthodontic treatment before. At first glance, there seems to be very little to correct. Then the patient points to a slightly rotated incisor, a small space, or a tooth just out of position and says:

#dialogo[#emph[“Doctor, I just want to fix this one little thing.”];]

Over time, I’ve learned that the word #strong[just]; should immediately make us pay closer attention.

We see a small movement and naturally think of a small treatment: a few aligners, a short time, little difficulty. But that is not necessarily how it works. In fact, some treatments that seem simplest from a biomechanical standpoint can be among the most delicate to manage, because when the starting point is already very good, even the smallest detail takes on an entirely different importance.

Barry Glaser has described this situation very well with the expression #strong[“Tiny Touch\-Up Patient.”]; Starting from that same observation, over time I began calling these patients #strong[95% patients];: patients who come in already very close to the result they want and ask us to address only the small gap that still separates them from their goal.

Imagine two patients. The first comes to us with an obvious problem: visibly crooked teeth, significant crowding, or another situation they clearly want to change. At the end of treatment, the result will never be perfect, because perfection is not natural and, in fact, does not exist. But the improvement will be so clear that the patient will not have to look for it: they need only compare the before and after to see how much their smile has changed. The second patient, by contrast, comes into our practice with an already beautiful smile and teeth that are practically straight. They are not looking for a major transformation: they came in for that one small detail they keep noticing and want to correct.

And that is the difference that completely changes the picture. For the first patient, the improvement is substantial, obvious, and spread across many aspects of the smile. For the 95% patient, by contrast, the entire value of treatment is concentrated in one very small detail. What may be a minor correction to us is the whole reason they decided to begin treatment: #strong[that 5%, to them, is 100% of the problem];. That is why I consider the 95% patient potentially more delicate to treat than many apparently complex cases: #strong[we have little room for improvement and a great deal to preserve];.

And it is precisely that word, #emph[just];, that can influence us too. If the patient considers the problem small, we can easily start to consider the treatment small as well. #emph[“It’ll be easy,”];  #emph[“it’ll only take a few aligners,”]; and #emph[“it’s nothing”]; are phrases that sound reassuring but that I have stopped using. Once we say something will be easy, we are no longer just describing the biomechanics: we are creating an expectation. The patient may take that statement as a guarantee that the result they want will be simple to achieve and perfectly predictable, when we know very well that a biological movement does not become perfectly predictable simply because it is small.

There is another point worth clarifying from the start: the fact that the patient sees only one tooth out of position does not necessarily mean treatment will consist simply of moving that tooth into the right place. To rotate an incisor, we might need to create space; to close a small diastema, we might need to control the inclination of the neighboring teeth; and to maintain a good bite, we might need to involve other teeth or even both arches. A tooth may also fail to respond exactly as expected, a refinement may be needed, and torque, contacts with neighboring teeth, periodontal tissues, or anatomical limitations may come into play—factors the patient cannot know about when they ask us to #emph[“move this tooth just a little.”]; Explaining this beforehand matters, because our goal remains to correct that detail, but doing it properly may require us to move teeth the patient considers perfectly fine as they are. If we have not made that clear from the start, any movement beyond that one tooth may feel like something they never asked for. #strong[How small a problem looks does not, by itself, tell us how easy it will be to achieve exactly the result we have in mind.];

The same misunderstanding can arise over cost. The patient sees only one tooth that needs moving and may think that, because it is a small change, treatment must necessarily cost very little. Of course, each colleague is free to set their own fee structure, but a few aligners do not automatically mean little value or little responsibility: even a small treatment requires diagnosis, planning, and clinical responsibility. #strong[The patient is not buying four, six, or ten aligners: they are undergoing orthodontic treatment.];

#sezione[Define the Goal Before Starting]

With these patients, I think it is essential to slow down precisely when everything seems to suggest moving quickly. Before starting, I need to understand exactly what the patient wants to change—not in general terms, but very specifically. I look at the photos, the scan, and the clinical situation with them, ask them to point out exactly what they do not like, and try to understand the result they imagine. Then I ask a question I consider essential: #strong[“If we could correct exactly this one thing and everything else stayed just as it is today, would you be satisfied?”];

It is a very simple question, but it often tells us immediately whether the request really is limited to that detail. If the answer is an unequivocal yes, we have identified a specific, shared goal. But if the answer is #emph[“Yes... but maybe, while we’re at it, we could also...,”]; that probably means the small correction is not the only thing the patient would like to change, and it is much better to find that out before starting. In more delicate cases, I think it is useful for what we have agreed on to be #strong[put in writing and signed by the patient];—not to turn the doctor\-patient relationship into something legal or defensive, but simply so that, as time passes, the reason we began treatment and the result we set out to achieve remain clear.

If the patient wants to rotate an incisor slightly, that rotation will be the goal; if they want to close a small space, closing that space will be the goal. It may seem like an obvious distinction, but defining the endpoint precisely also means defining when treatment can be considered complete. #strong[If we do not know what has to happen before we can say “we’re done,” every improvement risks becoming merely the starting point for a new request.];

Once the initial goal has been reached, the patient’s attention may shift to something they had not noticed before. That does not necessarily mean we are dealing with someone who is chronically dissatisfied: it may simply be a consequence of the change we achieved. The point is to distinguish what remains to be done to reach the agreed\-upon goal from a new request. If the patient came in to close a space and that space is not completely closed, we are still completing treatment; if, on the other hand, we have closed it as agreed and now another detail comes up, we are talking about something different. Patients rarely put it in those terms. More often, they say: #emph[“While we’re at it...”]; And it is precisely that #emph[while we’re at it]; that can turn a small touch\-up into a process with no real end.

One case probably taught me more than any other how dangerous it can be to confuse the desire to accommodate a patient with the clinically sounder choice. I still consider it one of the most stressful episodes of my professional career. Nadia was the mother of a young patient I had treated for a Class III malocclusion with a maxillary expander. She was very pleased with her son’s result and, during one of his checkups, said to me, almost jokingly: #emph[“Doc, sooner or later we need to do something about my teeth too.”]; I asked her what she didn’t like, and she explained that, overall, she was happy with her smile, but she felt her upper arch was a little narrow. Above all, she didn’t like the position of her upper right lateral incisor (FDI 12), which sat slightly lingual to the other teeth. In photos, that tooth fell more into shadow and looked darker, and that was the detail that bothered her.

Since she was already in the office, I suggested doing an intraoral scan to get a better sense of the situation. That scan showed us that the case was less straightforward than it appeared if we looked only at the lateral incisor: Nadia had a tendency toward a Class III malocclusion, a tendency toward a crossbite on the right side around the premolars and first molar, and some crowding in the lower arch. I explained that if we wanted to plan comprehensive orthodontic treatment, we would need to consider both arches and correct the overall occlusal relationship. But she was very clear: she didn’t care about the lower crowding because #emph[“you can’t see the lower teeth anyway,”]; while what she really wanted was to move that upper lateral incisor farther labially so it would no longer fall into shadow in photos. So I agreed to treat only the upper arch with a treatment package limited to 20 aligners. The goal was specific and relatively simple: correct the position of the upper right lateral incisor (FDI 12) without turning her request into comprehensive orthodontic treatment, which she did not want to undergo at that point.

After about ten to twelve aligners, in just over three months, we achieved exactly what Nadia had asked me to do. The lateral incisor was in the right position, her smile had improved, and she was very pleased. I asked her explicitly whether she liked the result, and she confirmed that she did. With respect to the goal that had brought her in, treatment could have been considered complete. But it was precisely then that Nadia began to look at her smile differently: she started noticing the right posterior area more and told me she wanted the arch to look a little wider around the premolars, reducing what she saw as an overly noticeable buccal corridor. I explained why I did not think expanding only the upper arch was a good idea: for one thing, we would need to maintain a proper relationship with the lower arch, or we risked creating premature contacts; for another, expanding the premolars would inevitably create spaces that would be difficult to manage. But Nadia persisted. She didn’t want a lot of expansion, she told me, #emph[“just a little, whatever you can do.”];

And that is where I made my mistake. I felt I knew her, I had treated her son, our relationship had been excellent up to that point, and Nadia had always seemed reasonable to me. So I decided to accommodate her and proposed a new scan, explaining that I would prepare an extremely conservative simulation, with the least expansion I thought compatible with her bite. When she came back to see the digital treatment setup, something happened that I now recognize immediately as one of the most important warning signs with a 95% patient: she looked at the simulation and told me she wanted a little more expansion. I adjusted the digital treatment setup slightly and showed her that increasing the movement could create spaces and change the relationship with the lower arch, but she asked for another small increase. I showed her again what the consequences might be, emphasizing the risk of creating premature contacts and the need to find a compromise, until she told me that amount of expansion seemed sufficient, and I approved the refinement.

Three months later, Nadia returned extremely dissatisfied. Expanding the premolars had created small spaces where food tended to get stuck, and in trying to manage the arch form, the upper right canine had moved slightly inward and developed a premature contact with the lower canine. At that point, I tried to correct the problem with a second refinement, moving the canine farther labially and changing the position of the premolars again. But each time I corrected one aspect, another issue emerged: the canine improved, but the posterior contacts changed; we fixed the contacts, and some other detail appeared that Nadia found bothersome. What had begun as a small touch\-up was gradually turning into a treatment with no real end. In a further attempt to satisfy her, I even opened a new case at my own expense so I would have additional sets of aligners available and could keep looking for a solution that would finally make her happy. What had initially been a treatment lasting about three months, completed with a patient pleased with the result for her lateral incisor, thus became a process lasting fifteen to eighteen months.

Each appointment grew progressively more difficult, and a visit that would normally have taken ten minutes could turn into an hour or even an hour and a half of discussion. Nadia was increasingly angry, I was increasingly stressed, and the relationship that had been excellent at the start deteriorated visit by visit. I still remember exactly how I felt when I saw her name on the schedule: I knew I was in for a long, difficult appointment, and even before she arrived at the office, I could feel the tension building. It stayed with me all day. I kept reminding her that, before we began that second phase, I had explained several times why I did not think it was advisable to expand only the upper arch. But her response was probably one of the most important lessons I have ever learned from a patient:

#dialogo[#emph[“Yes, Doc, but you’re the doctor. You should have told me no.”];]

Over time, I had to admit that Nadia was right. Not because I had failed to understand the biomechanics of the case—I understood them perfectly and had explained them to her—but because, once I had determined that her request was not clinically advisable, the responsibility for holding that line remained mine. Patients can insist, tell us they accept the risk, ask us for #emph[“just a little,”]; but we are still the ones who decide whether to make that movement. The situation came very close to becoming a malpractice dispute: Nadia sought another opinion and was told that properly correcting the situation would require treating both arches and planning the case comprehensively—in essence, the very treatment I had proposed at the start and she had declined.

In the end, to keep the situation from deteriorating further, I decided to give her a full refund. The toll was heavy in every respect: I had personally covered the cost of two cases, refunded the entire fee, used an enormous amount of clinical time and staff energy, and turned a treatment that could have ended successfully after three months into one that lasted more than a year. Nadia was dissatisfied, and so was I. A patient who had initially placed enormous trust in our practice was unlikely to keep speaking about us with the same enthusiasm she had shown after her son’s treatment. But the greatest cost, at least for me, was not financial: it was the stress of a situation I had helped create by trying to accommodate a patient, even though I had known from the start that expansion limited to the upper arch was not the solution I would have chosen clinically.

In hindsight, it is easy to pinpoint when I should have stopped: at the appointment after the first set of aligners, when Nadia was satisfied and the lateral incisor she had come in about was in the right position. We had achieved the agreed\-upon goal, and everything that followed was not the completion of that treatment but the start of a new one—a treatment I myself did not consider clinically sound but accepted anyway in an attempt to please her. That is precisely the danger of the 95% patient: not so much the small movement they initially come to us for, but the possibility that, once we have achieved that goal, the target shifts and we keep chasing it, until we lose sight of the line between listening to what the patient wants and going along with a request we no longer consider clinically sound.

Today, faced with the same situation, my answer would be very different: #emph[“I completely understand what you’d like to improve, and I understand why, now that you’re looking at your smile differently, this bothers you more. The problem is that, to try to make that change, I would have to alter a balance that works well today, and I’m not sure that would be the best choice for you. If you’d like, we can discuss a more comprehensive treatment that also addresses this concern; otherwise, given the goal we started with, I think we can consider treatment complete.”]; This experience taught me a rule I now consider essential when managing 95% patients: #strong[listening to patients means taking what they want seriously, but it does not necessarily mean doing what they ask us to do.];

#sezione[Preserving the 95%]

Nadia’s case profoundly changed how I approach these patients. Before asking myself how to achieve the movement they are requesting, I now try to understand what I risk changing in the process. A very good starting point presents a paradox: we have little to gain, because the flaw we want to correct is small, but we may have a great deal to lose, because the teeth, contacts, and occlusal relationships around that flaw already function properly. Every movement must therefore offer a benefit that justifies what we have to change to achieve it.

The first thing to check is whether orthodontic treatment is indicated, because what looks like a problem with tooth position does not always stem from the tooth’s position. A difference between two incisors may be due to the shape of their crowns, their incisal edges, the gingiva, wear, color, or simply the way light falls on two different surfaces. In these cases, continuing to move a tooth may not resolve what bothers the patient at all, while a small restorative correction, reshaping the incisal edge, or, in selected cases, treatment of the gingival tissues may produce a result much more proportionate to the problem. The question we need to ask ourselves, then, is: #strong[am I trying to correct something with orthodontics that is actually an orthodontic issue?];

Even when the answer is yes, however, a small movement is still a biological movement. With aligners, we can plan rotations of a few degrees, extrusions of a few tenths of a millimeter, changes in torque, or extremely precise space closures on a screen, but what we see in the software remains a plan, and the clinical response does not become mathematically predictable simply because the movement is small. This matters even more when that movement is the entire reason the patient decided to start treatment.

We also need to think about stability from the outset. In a patient who has corrected a significant problem, a tiny change in a tooth’s position may even go unnoticed; in a patient who came in solely to close half a millimeter of space or correct a slight rotation, the same relapse may mean the exact problem that led them to seek treatment has returned. #strong[Retention, then, cannot be a detail we discuss at the end; it must be part of the initial conversation.];

#strong[The patient is asking us to correct the 5%. We are responsible for not damaging the 95% they already have.];

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

With this type of patient, I prefer to address the issue before we even begin, because being clear at the outset almost always prevents much more difficult conversations at the end:

#script[
#emph[“Your starting point is already very good, and that’s a positive thing. Precisely because of that, we need to be very clear about what you want to change: you’re asking me to address a small detail in a situation that already works well. First, we need to find out whether what bothers you really comes down to the tooth’s position. If it does, that will become our goal. Keep in mind, though, that even if you want to correct just one tooth, we may need to move other teeth, create space, or work on both arches to do it properly without compromising what already works well.];

#emph[If we decide to start, let’s agree on the result we want and on when we can consider treatment complete. If we reach that goal, we’ll have achieved what we set out to do. If you notice another detail along the way, of course we’ll assess it together, but it will be a new request, and we’ll need to decide whether intervening really makes sense. And precisely because we’re correcting something very small, we also need to think from the outset about how to maintain the result over time.”];
]

The tone should remain positive. We are not trying to scare the patient or put together a series of defensive disclaimers; we are simply making clear that we are agreeing to pursue #strong[a defined goal];, not to begin a process of improvement with no end.

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[It Defines the Target at the Outset]

For the 95% patient, the detail they want to correct makes up a tiny part of their smile but accounts for almost the entire reason they decided to start treatment. Defining it precisely allows the clinician and the patient to know from the outset what they are trying to achieve and, above all, to recognize when they have achieved it.

#perche-voce[It Puts the Word “Just” in Perspective]

A small problem does not necessarily mean simple treatment. Explaining from the outset that we may need to involve other teeth, create space, or treat both arches keeps each necessary movement from coming as a surprise. #strong[The size of the problem as the patient perceives it does not necessarily determine the complexity of the treatment needed to correct it.];

#perche-voce[It Protects What Already Works]

Starting from a very good situation changes the balance between the expected benefit and what we need to preserve. Before agreeing to the request, then, we must determine not only whether the movement is possible, but also whether it is orthodontically indicated and whether the expected benefit justifies the changes required to achieve it.

#perche-voce[It Defines Both the Result and Its Stability]

For the 95% patient, a small relapse may amount to the return of the entire problem as they perceived it. Discussing retention before treatment therefore makes clear that correcting the detail is only one part of the process; the other is maintaining the result.

