#capitolo(numero: "13", etichetta: [Chapter 13], titolo: [When Finishing Doesn’t Mean You’re Done], epigrafe: [“The end crowns the work.”], fonte: [Medieval Latin proverb])

For years, I noticed something in my patients that I couldn’t fully explain. I had the sense that some results achieved with aligners tended to relapse more readily than results I had achieved with fixed appliances. It didn’t happen in every case, and it didn’t affect every type of movement in the same way, but in my experience, rotations and certain vertical movements, such as intrusion and extrusion, were what caught my attention most.

At first, it was only a clinical impression, and for that very reason, I couldn’t automatically turn it into a conclusion. The first explanation I considered was the most obvious one: perhaps I wasn’t seeing a stability problem, but simply movements that were harder to achieve fully with aligners. The literature reminds us that treatment is not equally effective for every type of movement, and that controlling certain rotations, torque, and vertical movements can be more challenging than other types of tooth movement.

That explanation, however, didn’t fully account for what I was seeing, because I wasn’t just looking at teeth that had failed to reach their planned positions. In many cases, they had reached those positions correctly; the difficulty came later, when it was time to maintain them.

So I began to wonder whether I had been looking at the problem from the wrong angle. Perhaps the question wasn’t just #strong[how I had moved that tooth];, but #strong[how much time I had given it to stabilize after moving it];.

When we move a tooth orthodontically, we aren’t simply changing the coordinates of its crown within the arch. We are altering a biological system made up of bone, the periodontal ligament, and gingival tissues, which must gradually adapt to the new position. The end of orthodontic movement and the end of biological reorganization, then, do not necessarily coincide.

Classic studies of post\-treatment stability have shown that different tissues do not all reorganize at the same rate. The periodontal ligament tends to reorganize over approximately three to four months, while gingival collagen fibers may take four to six months. Some components of the supracrestal fibers may take even longer; classic histologic studies described them as not yet fully reorganized #strong[232 days after orthodontic movement ended];, especially following the correction of rotations.

That last finding in particular caught my attention: #strong[232 days is almost eight months];. That does not mean every tooth needs almost eight months to become stable, nor does it mean the literature recommends the protocol I later developed. It did, however, help me bring a fundamental point into focus: #strong[a tooth may have finished moving long before the tissues around it have completed their adaptation to its new position.];

At that point, I also began to think about a very simple difference between fixed appliances and aligners. With fixed appliances, a rotation may be fully corrected in the early stages of treatment, just as a curve of Spee may be leveled or a vertical movement completed long before the appliances are removed. But treatment continues: we move on to stiffer archwires, sagittal mechanics, space management, and refinement of the occlusion, while that tooth remains for months in a system that limits its movement.

By the time we finally remove the appliances, a long time may have passed since that particular movement was completed. This is where I began to form a possible explanation for what I was seeing: #strong[with fixed appliances, some stabilization may take place while treatment is still underway.];

With aligners, by contrast, that interval can be much shorter. A rotation, intrusion, or extrusion may be programmed through the last aligners and, if necessary, corrected again during a refinement. We may therefore reach the end of active treatment at virtually the same time that we are completing one of the very movements we consider most delicate.

This led me to look at treatment duration differently. #strong[Eighteen months of treatment does not necessarily mean eighteen months of stabilization for every movement we have made.]; Two patients may both have undergone eighteen months of treatment, but an incisor may have been in its final position for nine months in the first patient and only a few weeks in the second.

#strong[This remains my clinical hypothesis, not a mechanism demonstrated by the literature.]; Together with what we know about how long tissues take to reorganize, however, it led me to change the way I concluded my treatments.

#strong[My stabilization protocol grew out of that reflection.];

#sezione[Nine Months That Weren’t There Before]

In the past, when I was satisfied with the result, the transition was very simple: I ended active treatment, took a scan for the retainers, and asked the patient to wear them at night, following the protocol I’ll describe in the next chapter. In essence, I went almost directly from about twenty\-two hours of aligner wear to about ten hours of nighttime retainer wear.

At some point, I stopped doing that. If the tissues needed time to reorganize, it seemed inconsistent to complete the final movements and then immediately, drastically reduce the hours those teeth were held in their newly achieved positions. So I added a phase between active treatment and definitive retention that had not existed before: #strong[the stabilization phase];.

In my practice, it lasts a total of nine months. That duration does not come from a study showing that nine months is the ideal period. It is a clinical decision I reached over time, drawing on what I observed in my patients and the long biological window described for the reorganization of some supracrestal tissues—up to the #strong[232 days]; we discussed.

But I still had to decide how to organize those nine months. I did not want them to be merely a long waiting period. I wanted them to guide the patient gradually from about twenty\-two hours of wear during active treatment to about ten hours of nighttime retainer wear. So I divided them into #strong[three consecutive three\-month phases];, each with a specific goal.

#strong[Three months to stabilize. Three months to taper. Three months to allow the occlusion to settle.];

That is how I developed the sequence I still use today, the #strong[Pasciuti 3\-3\-3 Stabilization Protocol];, which calls for a total of 24 stabilization aligners. I reserve it, however, for patients who have suitable clinical conditions and are sufficiently adherent. If a lack of adherence makes another nine months of aligners unrealistic, I end the active phase and move directly to retention.

#sottosezione[The First Three Months: Stabilize]

The first twelve aligners are worn exactly like the active aligners that preceded them: one per week, about twenty\-two hours a day. To the patient, very little appears to change, because they continue the routine they became accustomed to during treatment. From a therapeutic standpoint, however, the goal changes completely. I am no longer trying to modify the occlusion or complete new, clinically significant movements. I am maintaining what we have achieved and giving the tissues an initial period to adapt to the position reached.

#sottosezione[The Second Three Months: Taper]

After the first twelve aligners, I begin to reduce wear time gradually. Aligner number 13 is worn twenty\-one hours a day, number 14 for twenty hours, number 15 for nineteen, and so on, decreasing by one hour with each weekly change until aligner number 24 is worn for ten hours a day.

This way, the transition from nearly continuous wear to nighttime wear does not happen abruptly. Each week, we add one more hour without an aligner, gradually guiding the patient toward the next phase.

#sottosezione[The Last Three Months: Allow the Occlusion to Settle]

When the patient reaches aligner number 24, the plan changes once again. They no longer replace it, but continue to wear #strong[that same aligner for the next three months];, only at night, for about ten hours.

At this point, six months have passed since active movements ended, and I no longer want only to hold the teeth in the positions they have reached. I also want to give function more room to play a role, so the occlusion can gradually find its balance.

I have always thought that an orthodontist’s job is not to build an occlusion on a screen and then try to hold it indefinitely in that position. Our goal is to put the teeth in the best possible position so that, once orthodontic movements are complete, chewing, contacts, and function can also contribute to their settling.

That is why, during the last three months, there is no longer any material between the arches during the day. The teeth come back into contact during function, promoting the gradual process of #strong[occlusal settling];.

The decision to keep wearing the same aligner for all three months is intentional. A thermoplastic material worn repeatedly does not necessarily retain the same mechanical properties over time that it had when it was new. Viscoelastic phenomena such as #emph[stress relaxation]; cause the forces exerted by the material to change progressively.

I do not consider this gradual change in the aligner’s mechanical properties to be the cause of #emph[occlusal settling];, nor has my protocol been studied experimentally to demonstrate such a relationship. #strong[The decision to keep wearing the same aligner is instead part of a strategy in which the aligner gradually stops trying to rigidly control every detail of the occlusion];, while still protecting the position achieved at night.

By the end of this phase, #strong[nine months have passed since active movements ended];. Only then do I consider the stabilization period complete and take a new scan to make the definitive retainers.

#sottosezione[In Practice: How I Obtain the 24 Stabilization Aligners]

There is, however, a very practical problem: deciding to use twenty\-four stabilization aligners does not necessarily mean I can order them as twenty\-four passive copies of the same final position. Commercial systems may limit the ability to request such long sequences without programmed movements.

Over time, I have developed a planning strategy that allows me to obtain the sequence the protocol requires. At the end of treatment, I take a new scan and request a refinement. When I receive the new digital treatment setup, I remove all the proposed tooth movements and essentially return the plan to the position achieved at the end of the previous treatment.

That, however, would produce only one aligner per arch. To obtain twenty\-four aligners, I select two posterior\-most teeth, usually second or third molars, one in each arch, and intentionally program a clinically insignificant movement for each, such as #strong[0.2 mm of intrusion]; or a minimal root movement involving torque or tip.

The software thus recognizes that a movement is present and generates a sequence of aligners. I then adjust the staging, intentionally slowing that minimal activation so it is distributed across the #strong[twenty\-four aligners]; the protocol requires.

From a digital standpoint, these aligners are therefore not completely passive, because the digital treatment setup retains a programmed micro\-activation on one posterior\-most tooth in each arch. #strong[Clinically, however, that micro\-activation is irrelevant to the treatment outcome:]; I include it solely as a planning workaround to obtain the number of aligners the protocol requires.

This procedure is a technical solution I use in my practice with the system I work with. It is not a procedure recommended by the manufacturer or a protocol validated by the literature, and it must, of course, be adapted to the characteristics of the system being used and the individual case.

But one problem remains, and it is behavioral rather than biomechanical. The patient is sitting across from us, looking at their smile and happy with it, and we are happy with it too. After months of treatment, we finally say the words they have been waiting to hear: #emph[“We’re done.”]; A few minutes later, though, we explain that they will need to keep wearing aligners for another nine months.

Their reaction is understandable: #emph[“What do you mean, another nine months? I thought we were done?”];

The problem lies in the word #emph[done];. #strong[We are done moving the teeth, but we are not done with treatment.]; We have achieved the result, but we still need to protect it while the tissues adapt to the new position.

If we present stabilization as something that comes after treatment, almost an add\-on that the patient can approach more casually, we risk undermining their adherence ourselves. That is why I prefer to present it from the start as a phase that is still fully part of treatment: #strong[we no longer need to achieve the result; we need to avoid losing what we have just achieved.];

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

When I explain the stabilization phase to a patient, my first priority is to prevent a misunderstanding: I am not telling them that treatment has failed or that we need another nine months to achieve the result. On the contrary, I begin with the fact that we have already achieved it.

#script[
#emph[“Today we achieved the result we wanted. Your teeth have reached the positions we wanted them to reach, so from this point on, we do not need to move them anymore. But there is something important to understand: just because a tooth has reached the right position does not mean it is already stable there. The tissues around it need time to reorganize, and especially at first, if we suddenly stopped protecting it, we might allow it to start moving again.];

#emph[It would be a shame to come this far, after all the time and effort we have invested, achieve the result we wanted, and then risk losing part of it right now. That is why I consider these nine months part of treatment: we no longer need to move the teeth, but we do need to preserve what we have achieved while it becomes stable.];

#emph[We’ll do this gradually. For the first three months, you’ll keep wearing your aligners twenty\-two hours a day, just as you have until now. Over the next three months, you’ll reduce your wear time by one hour each week until you reach ten hours. For the last three months, you’ll wear the final aligner only at night.];

#emph[So every three months, we’ll take another step: first we stabilize, then we gradually reduce wear time, and finally we give the occlusion more room to settle. After nine months, we’ll take a new scan and, if everything has stayed in the positions we achieved, we’ll move on to definitive retention. We’ve done the hard work. Now we need to make sure we don’t lose what we’ve gained.”];
]

In this way, I am not simply asking the patient to keep wearing aligners; I am giving a clear purpose to the behavior I am asking of them. Simply saying #emph[“you’ll need to continue for another nine months”]; could make stabilization seem, to the patient, like an inexplicable extension of a treatment they believe is already over. The message I want to convey is different: #strong[we have achieved the result, but it still needs to be stabilized];.

As with the other protocols, I can tailor the message here using the three motivators of #strong[outcome, time, and investment];, without changing what I am asking the patient to do. At this stage, the outcome is probably the most natural motivator: #emph[“We’ve achieved the result. Now we need to protect it while it becomes stable.”]; For some patients, I can emphasize the time they have already devoted to treatment: #emph[“We’ve worked for months to bring your teeth into these positions. It wouldn’t make sense to risk losing some of what we’ve achieved in just a few weeks.”]; For others, I can bring up their overall investment—not just the money, but also their consistency and effort: #emph[“You’ve invested a lot to get this far. These nine months are about protecting that investment.”]; The clinical implications do not change. What changes is what I choose to emphasize for the person in front of me.

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[It Turns the Result into Something to Protect]

During stabilization, the result is no longer something to achieve; it is something the patient already has. That brings #emph[loss aversion]; and the #emph[endowment effect]; into play: #strong[we are no longer asking patients to cooperate to gain something, but to avoid losing what they have gained.];

#perche-voce[It Reframes the Nine Months]

The #emph[framing]; changes the meaning of the same request. #strong[“Another nine months of treatment”]; can sound like an extension of therapy; #strong[“nine months to protect the result”]; instead makes that period a phase with a clear purpose.

#perche-voce[It Maintains Continuity in the Patient’s Routine]

For the first three months, patients continue the same routine they followed during the active phase. Only after that do they reduce wear time, by one hour a week. The change is therefore not sudden or left to vague instructions, but #strong[gradual, predictable, and measurable];.

#perche-voce[It Breaks a Long Period into Understandable Goals]

Nine months can seem long. Three consecutive three\-month phases, on the other hand, each have an immediately recognizable goal: #strong[stabilize, taper, allow the occlusion to settle];. Patients always know where they are in the process and what comes next.

#bibliografia(titolo: [References])[

  #voce[Melrose C, Millett DT. Toward a perspective on orthodontic retention? #emph[Am J Orthod Dentofacial Orthop.]; 1998;113(5):507–514.]
  #voce[Haouili N, Kravitz ND, Vaid NR, Ferguson DJ, Makki L. Has Invisalign improved? A prospective follow\-up study on the efficacy of tooth movement with Invisalign. #emph[Am J Orthod Dentofacial Orthop.]; 2020;158(3):420–425.]
