#capitolo(numero: "14", etichetta: [Chapter 14], titolo: [Delivering the Retainers], epigrafe: [“An ounce of prevention is worth a pound of cure.”], fonte: [Benjamin Franklin])

Delivering the definitive retainers marks the beginning of long\-term maintenance, and it can happen at two different points. For suitable, adherent patients, it follows the nine months of the stabilization protocol; for patients for whom that phase is not indicated, it comes directly at the end of active treatment. In either case, it is a less dramatic milestone than removing the attachments or handing over the final aligner, but it is just as important. For months, sometimes years, we have worked together to achieve a result. We have asked the patient to wear their aligners, change them on schedule, wear orthodontic elastics, come to appointments, and follow our instructions, until at last the day they have been waiting for since the beginning arrives—the day we say something that sounds harmless:

#dialogo[#emph[“We’re done.”];]

To the patient, those two words may mean much more than we realize. They may hear them as the end of rules and schedules, as the moment aligners become a thing of the past for good, and, above all, as confirmation that the result is now secure and can no longer be lost.

That is why I try never to present the delivery of retainers as the end of treatment. Instead, I prefer to explain clearly that only one phase of the process is ending:

#dialogo[#emph[“Today, the phase before definitive retention comes to an end: for you, that was the nine\-month stabilization phase”—or, when the protocol was not indicated, “the active phase of treatment.” “From this point on, long\-term maintenance begins. Preserving the result will depend mainly on you and how consistently you wear your retainers.”];]

The difference may seem small, but it completely changes the meaning of what is happening, because from that day forward, the day\-to\-day responsibility for preserving the result gradually passes into the patient’s hands.

Precisely because I consider this transfer of responsibility a fundamental part of treatment, when I deliver the retainers, I have the patient read and sign a retention\-specific informed consent form. This is not simply an instruction included in the initial consent form for orthodontic treatment, or a recommendation given hastily at the end of the final appointment. It is a document specific to this new phase, which we give the patient and have them sign when we deliver the definitive retainers.

The form explains why retention is necessary, how the retainers should be worn, what to do if they are lost or broken, and the possible consequences of not wearing them enough. Above all, it formalizes the transfer of responsibility I have just explained to the patient, which I sum up in a simple sentence:

“Getting your teeth into the right positions was our job. Keeping them there will be yours.”

For me, then, the signature is not just a formality or a means of legal protection. It is, above all, the conclusion of a conversation in which I first explain what changes for the patient from that day forward, then give them the information they need to manage this new phase properly, and finally ask them to confirm that they understand it. This is not a way to assign blame in advance if something goes wrong. It makes their responsibility clear while we are still in the best position to do so: #strong[before a problem arises.];

Before the appointment ends, I check that the retainer seats fully and take several intraoral photographs with it in place. I keep them with the final scan to establish a documented starting point. If the retainer no longer fits properly in the future, we will not have to rely on conflicting recollections. We can see how it fit when it was delivered, determine what has changed, and decide what solution is clinically appropriate. The photographs are not meant to prove that the patient failed to wear the retainer, but to make our assessment of the current situation more objective.

All of this becomes even more important when we consider that orthodontic stability cannot be taken for granted once treatment is complete. There is a tendency toward relapse after treatment, and, independently of that, the dentition continues to change throughout life. Retention must therefore be considered an integral part of treatment, and the retention protocol must be tailored to the clinical situation, the tooth movements performed, and the individual patient’s characteristics.

The literature offers no single retention protocol that is universally valid for all patients, but there is growing recognition of the need for very long\-term maintenance. In one survey of specialist orthodontists, for example, 67–78% of respondents advised their patients to continue retention for life.

My protocol follows the same philosophy, so I consider retention a permanent phase of treatment. That does not mean, of course, that the same retainer must last a lifetime. It may wear out, become distorted, break, or get lost, and it will need to be replaced when necessary. What does not change is the need to keep protecting the result over time.

#strong[The retainer has a lifespan. Retention does not.];

#sezione[Ten Hours, Every Night]

When I give patients instructions, especially ones I am asking them to follow for life, I prefer to explain why. In my protocol, thermoplastic retainers must be worn #strong[every night for at least ten hours];, roughly from 9 p.m., after dinner, to 7 a.m., before breakfast. Here, too, the number is not based simply on clinical habit; it has some interesting support in the literature.

İshakoğlu and Çokakoğlu studied 42 patients wearing thermoplastic retainers equipped with microsensors. This allowed them to measure actual wear time objectively rather than relying solely on what patients reported. After twelve months, the patients were divided into two groups based on their average daily wear time: less than nine hours, or nine hours or more. Greater loss of alignment was observed in patients who wore their retainers for less than nine hours a day. The authors therefore concluded that an average wear time of less than nine hours was not enough to keep irregularity within clinically acceptable limits, and they recommended at least nine hours a day to maintain anterior alignment.

It is important to interpret this finding correctly. It does not mean there is an absolute biological threshold at which teeth move after eight hours and fifty\-nine minutes of wear but stay put after nine hours. The study also involved a specific sample of patients treated with four premolar extractions, and the statistically significant difference between the groups concerned lower anterior alignment. Still, it gives us clinically useful information, suggesting that #strong[an average wear time of less than nine hours may not be sufficient to maintain adequate lower anterior alignment];.

It is on the basis of this finding that I prescribe ten hours in my protocol—not because any study has shown it to be a universal biological threshold, but because I think it is reasonable to add a small margin to the nine hours indicated by the study while giving patients a simple, concrete instruction that is easy to remember: #strong[ten hours, every night, for life];.

That does not mean that, if ten hours is sufficient, wearing a retainer for twenty or twenty\-two hours must necessarily provide even greater stability. The available evidence does not show a significant advantage for full\-time wear over adequate part\-time wear. In a randomized clinical trial, Thickett and Power compared full\-time and part\-time wear of thermoplastic retainers and, after twelve months, found no statistically significant differences between the two protocols for most of the measures they examined. Reviews of the literature point in the same direction, indicating that part\-time wear of removable retainers may be sufficient, while emphasizing that the overall quality of the evidence remains limited.

The message, then, is not that ten hours is the perfect dose for every patient and every clinical situation. Retention remains an area in which there is no single universally accepted protocol. What I want patients to understand is much simpler: once active treatment is over, #strong[I am not asking them to keep wearing an appliance twenty\-two hours a day for the rest of their lives, as they did with their aligners];. I am asking them to make retention a sustainable nighttime routine.

But telling a patient #emph[“wear it at night”]; still leaves too much room for interpretation. For some people, a night lasts nine hours; for others, six. Some put their retainers in when they go to bed, others only just before they fall asleep, while still others may think nothing of skipping a night because, after all, they were simply told to wear them #emph[at night];. That is why I prefer to turn a general instruction into a precise routine: #strong[9 p.m.–7 a.m.]; The retainers go in at nine in the evening, after dinner, and come out at seven in the morning, before breakfast, completing the prescribed ten hours. Of course, I do not attach any biological significance to nine in the evening or seven in the morning. The benefit is mainly behavioral: tying an action to familiar, recurring points in the day makes it easier to turn into a habit.

This becomes particularly important when we consider that adherence to retention tends to decline over time. Kacer and colleagues examined retainer wear during the first two years after active treatment. They found that, while 69% of patients reported wearing their retainers every night during the first three months, between months 19 and 24 the percentage classified as adherent according to the study’s criteria had fallen to 45%, and 19% reported no longer wearing them at all. These were self\-reported data collected through a questionnaire, not an objective measurement of behavior. But the trend they show is one we readily recognize in clinical practice: #strong[adherence tends to decline over time];.

The real challenge, then, is not convincing patients to wear their retainers the night we deliver them, when they can see the result, the memory of treatment is still fresh, and the value of what they have achieved is unmistakable. We need to build a habit that will last when that smile has become normal, when treatment is a distant memory, and when we are no longer there every few weeks to remind them what to do. #strong[That is why I prefer a precise routine to a general recommendation.];

#sottosezione[You Can’t Make Up for Missed Hours]

There is another message I think it is essential to give patients on the day we deliver their retainers. They must not come away thinking of the ten\-hour prescription as a kind of bank account: if they skip a night, they can simply wear their retainers longer the next day and make up for the missed hours. The problem is not the hours themselves, but what may happen to their teeth while the retainers are out.

If one or more teeth start to shift, the position of the teeth no longer matches the shape the retainer was made to fit. When the patient tries to put it back in, it may feel tighter, or they may notice that it no longer fits the same way. That is precisely when an apparently logical but potentially dangerous thought can arise: #emph[“It feels tight, so I’ll wear it a little longer and it’ll move my teeth back into place.”];

I do not want patients to wear their retainers on their own as if they were new aligners, because those retainers were made #strong[to hold the teeth in position];, not to carry out a new, planned orthodontic movement. If the teeth no longer match the positions the retainer was made for, it may begin to exert forces that were not planned as part of treatment. That is why I introduce a very simple rule when I deliver it:

#dialogo[#emph[“If you don’t wear it for a few days and it feels very tight when you put it back in, don’t think you can make up for the missed time simply by wearing it longer. This retainer was made to keep your teeth in the positions they’re in today, not to make new orthodontic movements. If it no longer fits properly or you have to force it in, call us.”];]

There is no need to explain all the biomechanics that can come into play when a retainer no longer matches the positions of a patient’s teeth. It is much more important to keep patients from forming the wrong idea and to help them understand, from the day we deliver it, that #strong[missed hours cannot be made up: what we need to prevent is a loss of tooth position];.

#sezione[When a Retainer Is Lost or Broken]

Then there is a seemingly minor problem I have faced many times over the years: a patient loses their retainer, calls the office, and simply asks, #emph[“Can you make me another one?”]; Nothing unusual so far; the problem can arise when they learn that making a new retainer costs money.

To understand their reaction, we need to look back at how we educated them during active treatment. A patient treated with aligners may have received dozens of trays, lost or broken a few, and gone through one or more refinements without directly seeing the cost of each appliance, especially when the package they purchased included additional aligners. Over time, they may have formed a very simple association: #strong[tray \= included in treatment];.

When we give them a clear retainer, to them it looks extremely similar to what they have been wearing up to that point. To us, its function has changed completely, but to the patient it is still a tray. If we do not explicitly change that mental model, we cannot be surprised when they lose or break it and assume it, too, should be replaced for free.

Once again, the best time to address the issue is not when the patient calls to say they have lost it, but #strong[before they lose it];. The retention\-specific informed consent form I have patients sign clearly states that replacing a lost or broken retainer will cost money. But I do not want that cost to feel like a punishment or a penalty for being careless: the patient is simply paying for the production of #strong[a new appliance];.

During treatment, they purchased a course of care that included certain services and appliances, but that phase is over. It cannot become an implicit promise to produce replacement retainers for free for the rest of their lives. If patients are to wear retainers over the long term, we need to explain from the outset that individual appliances may wear out, break, or get lost, and that producing a new retainer when one needs to be replaced will cost money. This, too, is part of the transfer of responsibility we began when we delivered the retainer.

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

All these points must be scientifically sound, but they must not turn the final appointment into a college lecture. Patients should leave the office remembering a few things that are perfectly clear, so the conversation can be much simpler than the reasoning behind it.

#script[
#emph[“Today, the phase before definitive retention comes to an end: for you, that was the nine\-month stabilization phase”—or, when that protocol was not indicated, “the active phase of treatment.” “From this point on, long\-term maintenance begins, and keeping the result will depend mainly on you and how consistently you wear your retainer. This phase lasts for life; I always joke that retention is like diamonds: it’s forever.”];
]

At that point, I give the patient a concrete instruction, trying to leave them with a rule they can remember without any room for interpretation:

#script[
#emph[“The rule is very simple: ten hours, every night. The easiest thing is to put your retainer in at nine in the evening, after dinner, and take it out at seven in the morning, before breakfast. Nine to seven: that’s ten hours. You don’t need to wear it all day; you need to wear it consistently every night.”];
]

Right afterward, I explain what might happen if they do not stick to that routine, because I want patients to know what to do before they find themselves in that situation:

#script[
#emph[“And one more thing: you can’t make up missed hours. If you skip a few nights and your retainer feels very tight when you put it back in, or it no longer fits the way it used to, don’t think, ‘I’ll wear it longer and everything will go back to normal.’ This retainer was made to keep your teeth where they are today, not to carry out a new course of treatment. If it doesn’t fit properly or you have to force it in, call us right away.”];
]

At the same time, I explain what patients should do if a retainer is lost or broken and, above all, prevent any future misunderstanding about the cost of replacing it:

#script[
#emph[“If you lose your retainer or it breaks, call us right away. Don’t wait a few days thinking nothing will happen, because from that point on, your teeth are no longer being held in the positions we achieved. Remember, too, that if we have to make a new one, it will cost money. Active treatment is over, and from today on, managing your retention is part of your responsibility for maintaining the result.”];
]

Finally, I return to why I am giving them all these instructions. I do not want patients to leave remembering only a set of rules; more importantly, I want them to understand what those rules are protecting:

#script[
#emph[“The most important thing is this: today we’re giving you a result we achieved together. From this point on, your job is to preserve it. If you stop wearing your retainers and your teeth shift, simply making a new retainer may not be enough. If you want to regain the result we achieved, you may need another course of orthodontic treatment, with a new cost.”];
]

Only after this explanation do I have the patient read and sign the retention\-specific informed consent form. At that point, the signature is not the beginning of the conversation but its conclusion. The patient has seen the result and understands that the clinician\-guided phase has ended but maintenance continues. They know their responsibilities, how many hours a day and how long they need to wear their retainer, what to do if it is lost, breaks, or no longer fits properly, and the possible consequences of not following the instructions. We have addressed all these possibilities when we were still in the best position to do so: #strong[before they became a problem.];

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#strong[It changes what retention means.]; Telling patients that treatment is not over but a new phase is beginning changes how they see retention. It is not presented as an accessory to wear whenever they remember, but as the means by which they will protect, over time, a result they already have and have invested time, energy, and money in achieving. This brings together #strong[a shift in framing, the salience of the result, and loss aversion];.

#strong[It turns a recommendation into a specific behavior.]; #emph[“Ten hours, every night, 9 p.m.–7 a.m.”]; leaves much less room for interpretation than #emph[“wear it at night.”]; Tying putting the retainer in and taking it out to two recognizable points in the day also helps turn the instruction into a consistent routine. This brings together #strong[reduced ambiguity and implementation intention];, concepts we have already explained in earlier chapters and do not need to revisit here.

#strong[It transfers responsibility before a problem arises.]; From the day the retainer is delivered, patients know what to do if they skip a few nights, if the retainer feels tight, no longer fits properly, is lost, or breaks. They also know that any treatment needed to correct a relapse would be a new course of treatment. Establishing these rules while the result is stable and there is no conflict yet allows us to address future problems on the basis of responsibilities we have already agreed on.

#strong[It makes the commitment explicit.]; The retention\-specific informed consent form is signed only after the instructions, responsibilities, possible problems, and costs have been explained. The signature does not replace the conversation; it concludes and formalizes what we have discussed, including the fact that replacing a lost or broken retainer means paying for a new appliance, not a penalty.

#bibliografia(titolo: [References])[

  #voce[İshakoğlu S, Çokakoğlu S. Evaluation of relapse with thermoplastic retainers equipped with microsensors. #emph[Angle Orthod.]; 2022;92(3):340–346.]
  #voce[Thickett E, Power S. A randomized clinical trial of thermoplastic retainer wear. #emph[Eur J Orthod.]; 2010;32(1):1–5.]
  #voce[Kacer KA, Valiathan M, Narendran S, Hans MG. Retainer wear and compliance in the first 2 years after active orthodontic treatment. #emph[Am J Orthod Dentofacial Orthop.]; 2010;138(5):592–598.]
  #voce[Al\-Moghrabi D, Littlewood SJ, Fleming PS. Orthodontic retention protocols: an evidence\-based overview. #emph[Br Dent J.]; 2021;230:770–776.]
  #voce[Meade MJ, Millett D. Retention protocols and use of vacuum\-formed retainers among specialist orthodontists. #emph[J Orthod.]; 2013;40(4):318–325.]
]

