#capitolo(numero: "15", etichetta: [Chapter 15], titolo: [A Virtual Appointment Every Week], epigrafe: [“When you can measure what you are speaking about, and express it in numbers, you know something about it.”], fonte: [Lord Kelvin])

During clear\-aligner treatment, a lot can happen between office visits. Patients change trays on their own, wear them according to their own habits, and often have to judge for themselves whether they still fit their teeth properly. If an aligner begins to lose tracking—that is, it no longer fits the tooth surfaces precisely—the patient may not notice, or may decide to move on to the next aligner anyway, simply because the prescribed number of days has passed. Even a reduction in daily wear time may seem to have no consequences at first, especially when the patient is convinced they can make up for it by wearing their aligners more in the days just before their appointment.

But if we do not see the patient again for four or six months, that small loss of tracking may have carried through the entire sequence. At that point, we no longer have to correct only the first tooth movement that failed to occur, but also the effects of the subsequent aligners, which were designed on the assumption that all the teeth had reached their planned positions.

It is precisely to shorten this period without observation that I use #strong[DentalMonitoring]; in my practice, a system that makes it possible to follow the progress of orthodontic treatment remotely. Using an app installed on their smartphone and a special device that helps hold their lips and cheeks away from their teeth, patients capture a guided series of images of their mouths.

In everyday language, we call this a #emph[scan];, although it is not the same as the three\-dimensional intraoral scan we perform in the office to capture the arches and plan the aligners. The DentalMonitoring scan is, more precisely, a guided series of photographs captured by patients with their own phones. It allows the system and the clinical team to assess aligner fit and treatment progress remotely.

Under my protocol, patients take these smartphone scans once a week, before moving on to the next aligner. The images let us assess how well the aligners fit and whether the teeth are maintaining tracking—that is, whether their actual positions remain consistent with those planned in the treatment sequence. #strong[Based on what we see, patients are instructed either to move on to the next aligner or to keep wearing the current one for a while longer.];

Changing aligners weekly is therefore no longer just a date on the calendar. Patients do not automatically move on to the next aligner simply because seven days have passed; they wait for the remote check to confirm that the conditions for moving forward are in place.

DentalMonitoring does not replace an orthodontist’s assessment, and it cannot tell us everything happening in a patient’s mouth. It does, however, substantially shorten the time between the onset of a problem and our ability to detect it. An early loss of tracking can be addressed while it is still limited, before it affects more teeth and compromises a larger part of the sequence.

DentalMonitoring is, first and foremost, a clinical tool that lets us follow treatment more consistently even when in\-person appointments are spaced farther apart. As I used it, though, I realized that it also serves a second purpose, one particularly important in the context of this book: #strong[it makes some aspects of patient adherence more visible.];

When I give patients their aligners, I explain that remote monitoring will allow us, in many cases, to schedule office visits every four to six months, adjusting the frequency to the clinical needs of each case, of course. That does not mean treatment goes unchecked during that time: between appointments, we receive weekly images and can follow its progress consistently.

Each smartphone scan thus becomes a kind of brief virtual appointment. Patients have to remember to take it, follow the procedure correctly, and wait for instructions through the system before changing aligners. Traditional appointments are therefore supplemented by shorter but much more frequent checks, so fewer office visits do not mean less responsibility.

The weekly scans let me assess aligner fit, but also how reliably patients follow the protocol. How regularly they submit images, whether they follow the instructions they receive, and whether they respond to messages become behaviors we can document; likewise, their absence tells us something too.

More than once, I have given a patient the system and watched their scans, after an initial period of regular use, grow progressively less frequent until they stopped altogether. At the next appointment, the aligners no longer fit properly, yet the explanation was almost always the same:

#dialogo[#emph[“I wore them all the time.”];]

In the past, a statement like that could have started an argument that went nowhere. The patient would have continued to insist they had followed the treatment plan, and all I would have had to cast doubt on their account was the poor fit of the aligners. With remote monitoring, I can take a completely different approach, because #strong[I do not have to choose immediately between believing the patient and accusing them of being dishonest.];

#sezione[Pinpointing When Tracking Was Lost]

When a patient says they have worn their aligners as instructed, I do not open their DentalMonitoring scan history by saying, #emph[“Now let’s see if that’s true.”]; A remark like that would immediately turn the conversation into a trial, making the patient feel accused and pushing them to defend their version of events. I prefer to present the review of the scans as a clinical investigation we undertake together, looking for the period when treatment began to diverge from the plan:

#dialogo[#emph[“Let’s look together at all the scans you’ve taken over the past few weeks. That way, we can identify the week when tracking started to worsen and try to understand what might have happened.”];]

I often suggest a specific possibility, too, because I want patients to understand that I am not looking for a confession, but for an actual event that might help us interpret what we are seeing:

#dialogo[#emph[“Maybe we’ll find it happened during the week of your best friend’s wedding. Maybe that day you went without your aligners for many hours, and from then on, one tooth stopped tracking perfectly. Let’s see if we can piece together what happened.”];]

The wedding example gives the patient a way out of the standoff between #emph[“I wore them all the time”]; and #emph[“I don’t believe you.”]; A wedding, a party, a day of travel, a particularly long dinner, an illness, or a very difficult week may be easier to remember than the total number of hours the aligners were worn. A general question such as #emph[“Did you wear your aligners?”]; almost inevitably produces an equally general answer: #emph[“Yes, all the time.”]; But when I tie the question to a specific moment, the patient may realize, for example, that they took their aligners out during the wedding lunch and did not put them back in until late that evening. We are no longer discussing adherence in the abstract; we are reconstructing what they did at a recognizable moment.

I then open the DentalMonitoring history and review the preceding weeks with the patient. In some cases, we can pinpoint the period when tracking began to worsen and link it to a particular event, a reduction in wear time, insufficient use of aligner chewies, or a tooth movement that failed to occur despite apparently adequate adherence. At other times, though, we discover that the smartphone scans needed to reconstruct what happened simply do not exist: the history is empty or has obvious gaps. When the patient responds, #emph[“That’s impossible. I did every scan,”]; I do not need to accuse them directly, because the missing data show that the agreed\-upon protocol was not followed.

That does not necessarily mean the patient failed to wear their aligners properly. It does mean, however, that they interrupted the agreed\-upon channel of supervision. Even if they wore the aligners as instructed, without up\-to\-date data we could not check their fit, assess tracking, or confirm that the conditions for moving forward with the sequence were in place. At that point, I explain:

#dialogo[#emph[“That’s exactly the problem. If we had a scan for every week, we could pinpoint when tracking started to worsen and try to understand what happened. Because some scans weren’t taken, we lost the chance to spot the problem and intervene promptly. Now we need to assess how much tracking has been lost and determine whether we can still continue with these aligners or whether we’ll need to change the treatment plan. Either way, from this point on, you’ll need to resume both wearing your aligners as instructed and taking weekly scans. Otherwise, we risk ending up in the same situation again.”];]

I cannot know for certain whether the patient is lying intentionally or has reconstructed their behavior in a way they find more acceptable. They may remember the weeks when they used the system properly and forget the weeks when they did not, or they may confuse an intention to take a scan with a memory of actually having taken one. For clinical management, however, the result is the same: the agreed\-upon protocol was not followed, and we could not promptly identify when tracking began to worsen.

Remote monitoring, then, does not prevent a patient from lying, nor does it eliminate the ways memory reconstructs past behavior. But it reduces the room for the patient’s account to become the only version available.

There are also small details that, while not actual measures of adherence, can tell us something about a patient’s habits. One of the most interesting involves orthodontic elastics. When I prescribe them, I do not ask patients to keep them in during smartphone scans at home; in fact, removing them would be much easier, because they limit how wide patients can open their mouths and can make the process more uncomfortable. The most logical thing to do, then, would be to remove the orthodontic elastics, take the scan, and put them back in immediately afterward.

Some patients, however, take the scan with their orthodontic elastics still in place—not because they want to prove to me that they are following instructions, or because they were told to do so, but simply because they forget to remove them. The orthodontic elastics have become such a habitual part of their day that they start the process without even thinking about taking them out.

To me, this behavior is a very meaningful sign, although, of course, a scan taken with orthodontic elastics in place does not prove that the patient wore them for all the prescribed hours: at least in theory, they could have put them in just before the scan. But when I see someone spontaneously accept the added difficulty of taking a scan with orthodontic elastics in place, I consider that behavior a credible indication that wearing them really is part of their daily routine. This is not proof on its own, but a spontaneous sign that carries weight precisely because it does not appear to have been staged for the doctor.

DentalMonitoring lets me observe not only tooth movement, but also glimpses of the patient’s day\-to\-day experience with treatment. A scan taken on time, a prompt response to an instruction, or an orthodontic elastic left in place during a scan can all be small indicators of patient adherence. #strong[They are indicators, though, not measurements];, and it is important to be very clear about that limitation: DentalMonitoring does not have a timer inside the aligner and cannot tell us exactly how many hours a day it was worn. It cannot, therefore, tell us that a patient wore an aligner for eighteen hours rather than twenty\-two.

What we can consistently observe is aligner fit, treatment progress, and any emerging loss of tracking. If the aligners are worn less than necessary, some effects of that inconsistent wear may become visible over time, even though what we see does not allow us to calculate wear time mathematically.

When talking with patients, I use this capability to encourage them to take responsibility, because I want them to know from the start that what happens between appointments will not remain completely invisible. At the same time, I avoid claiming the system is more precise than it is, and I use wording that clearly distinguishes between directly measuring wear time and observing its possible clinical consequences:

#dialogo[#emph[“The system doesn’t have a timer, so it can’t tell us exactly how many hours you’ve worn your aligner. But the weekly smartphone scans let us keep track of fit and treatment progress. If the aligners are worn less than necessary, the effects of that inconsistent wear often become visible over time.”];]

This distinction also matters because a tooth’s failure to move cannot automatically be attributed to poor adherence. There may be biomechanical difficulties, a problem with an attachment, anatomical factors, or a biological response different from the one expected. Remote monitoring should therefore help us start a conversation and develop clinical hypotheses, not deliver an automatic verdict against the patient. #strong[Its real value lies not in proving that the patient did something wrong, but in helping us recognize sooner when something is not working as planned and, where possible, understand why.];

#sezione[The Protocol During Active Treatment]

At my practice, the protocol calls for patients to take a smartphone scan once a week, before changing aligners, and to wait for instructions before moving on to the next one. When fit is inadequate, they may need to keep wearing the same aligner for a few more days, increase their wear time, or use aligner chewies more often.

If the problem does not resolve, we can move up the office visit and assess whether it is still appropriate to continue with the sequence or whether we need to stop, take a new three\-dimensional intraoral scan, and plan a refinement. In\-person appointments are generally scheduled every four to six months, although the frequency is always adjusted to the needs of each case.

The same principle applies when a patient submits a smartphone scan but the images are not clear enough for a reliable assessment. If it happens occasionally, we go through the process together again and check for any technical difficulties. If the problem persists, the patient needs to be seen in the office. When remote monitoring is not workable, we schedule more frequent clinical visits and may give the patient fewer aligners at a time: #strong[patients cannot continue changing aligners on their own without a reliable channel of supervision.];

That way, the weekly aligner change is no longer just a date on the calendar. The patient’s instinctive thought, #emph[“A week has passed, so I can change aligners,”]; is replaced by a more accurate principle: #emph[“A week has passed, and I can change aligners if the clinical conditions are right for moving forward.”];

#sezione[Monitoring During Retention]

The same principle can continue after active treatment ends. As we saw in the previous chapter, giving a patient a retainer means gradually entrusting them with the responsibility of protecting the result they have achieved, but that does not necessarily mean they must be left entirely on their own from then on. At my practice, I offer the option of continuing monitoring during retention through an optional monitoring and guarantee program that combines remote checks with a form of guarantee for maintaining the result.

The program costs #strong[500 euros a year]; and can be continued for up to three years. The treatment packages I use allow additional aligners to be produced within five years of the start of treatment, and active treatment often lasts a year and a half or two years. That frequently leaves a period of about three years during which we can continue to follow the patient and, when the specified conditions are met, intervene if relapse occurs without a new aligner production cost, provided that production is still covered by the package.

The word #emph[guarantee];, however, must be used carefully, because it could suggest that the practice will automatically correct any future tooth movement, regardless of what the patient does. That is not what the program means. The guarantee exists within an agreement in which the patient also makes specific commitments: they must wear their retainers as prescribed, regularly submit the required smartphone scans through DentalMonitoring, attend scheduled checkups, and promptly tell us if a retainer is lost, breaks, or develops any fit problem.

During this phase, I require a DentalMonitoring smartphone scan once a month and include a professional dental cleaning every six months in the program. At that visit, the hygienists also take a three\-dimensional intraoral scan, which we can compare with the one taken at the end of treatment to check the stability of tooth positions over time. The patient is not simply buying a promise that, if something moves, it will be corrected for free. They are entering a system of supervision in which prevention, checkups, and personal responsibility continue to work together.

If the patient has met the agreed\-upon conditions and relapse nevertheless occurs that requires further orthodontic treatment, we can intervene without charging a new treatment fee, within the time and clinical limits set by the program, of course. If, instead, the retainers have not been worn as prescribed, the monthly scans have stopped, checkups have been missed, or a problem has been ignored for months before being reported, the basis on which that guarantee was built no longer exists.

This matters because even a guarantee program can become counterproductive if patients see it as shifting all responsibility to the practice. The message should not be #emph[“Whatever happens, we’ll take care of it,”]; but rather #emph[“If you keep doing your part, we’ll keep doing ours.”]; The guarantee thus becomes a shared responsibility: the patient protects the result by wearing their retainers, submitting scans, and attending the agreed\-upon checkups, while we maintain a system that allows us to spot any change early and intervene when necessary.

I also prefer to present the program this way when discussing its cost. The #strong[500 euros a year]; are not simply the price of a possible new treatment; they cover ongoing prevention and supervision during retention, as well as dental cleanings.

DentalMonitoring thus plays a role in two very different phases of treatment. During active treatment, it allows us to shorten the time a loss of tracking can go unnoticed; during retention, it can help us recognize a change in the result early. In both cases, though, the principle remains the same: #strong[monitoring works only if someone is watching and someone continues to provide the information needed for that monitoring.];

#script-titolo[Practical Script]

DentalMonitoring comes up in conversations with patients at different points in treatment, so the script needs to change with the situation. When we give patients their aligners, its main purpose is to establish the rules of the system; when tracking is lost, it should help us reconstruct what happened without turning the conversation into an accusation; if scans have not been submitted, it should make the consequences of the lack of supervision clear; and during retention, it should clarify that the monitoring and guarantee program works only when responsibility is shared.

#script-sub[When Giving Patients Their Aligners]

#script[
#emph[“Before each aligner change, you’ll need to take a smartphone scan and wait for instructions. If everything is progressing as it should, you can move on to the next aligner. If a tooth needs more time, we may ask you to keep wearing the current one and give you specific instructions.];

#emph[The system doesn’t have a timer, so it can’t tell us exactly how many hours you’ve worn your aligners. But the weekly smartphone scans let us keep track of fit and treatment progress. If the aligners are worn less than necessary, the effects of that inconsistent wear often become visible over time.];

#emph[You’ll have fewer appointments at the office, but you’ll have a very specific responsibility: the weekly smartphone scan is just as much a part of treatment as wearing your aligners.”];
]

#script-sub[When Tracking Has Been Lost]

#script[
#emph[“Let’s look at the scans from the past few weeks together. That way, we can pinpoint when tracking started to worsen and try to understand what might have happened. We may find that there was a wedding, a trip, or something else that week that kept your aligners out of your mouth longer than planned.”];
]

#script-sub[When Scans Have Not Been Submitted]

#script[
#emph[“If we had a scan for every week, we could pinpoint when fit started to worsen and try to understand what happened. Because some scans were not submitted, we lost the chance to notice the problem and intervene promptly.];

#emph[Now we need to assess how much tracking has been lost and determine whether we can continue with these aligners or need to change the treatment plan. Either way, from this point on, we’ll need to get back to both wearing the aligners correctly and submitting weekly scans. Otherwise, we could find ourselves in the same situation again.”];
]

#script-sub[When Offering the Program During Retention]

#script[
#emph[“From today on, maintaining your result will depend primarily on wearing your retainers. You can manage this phase on your own or enroll in our monitoring and guarantee program for the next three years.];

#emph[At my practice, the program costs 500 euros a year and includes two dental cleanings, one every six months, monitoring through a monthly smartphone scan taken at home, and periodic comparisons between your current tooth positions and those at the end of treatment.];

#emph[If you follow the protocol and we nevertheless detect tooth movement, we’ll promptly assess how to address it. When the program’s conditions are met, you won’t be charged for a new course of orthodontic treatment.];

#emph[To keep the program active, you’ll need to wear your retainers as directed, submit the monthly smartphone scan, and attend the two scheduled dental cleanings. It isn’t just financial coverage; it’s a system that lets us spot quickly if something is changing.”];
]

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[It Makes What Happens Between Checkups Visible]

The weekly smartphone scan turns the long interval between checkups into a series of brief digital check\-ins, so patients know their treatment progress can be observed regularly. At the same time, timely feedback creates a clearer link between behavior and its possible clinical consequences: if fit starts to worsen, we can intervene while the problem is still limited, rather than discovering it many months later.

#perche-voce[It Lets Us Reconstruct Events Instead of Assigning Blame]

When tracking is lost or scheduled scans have not been submitted, there is no need to turn the conversation into a contest between the patient’s account and the doctor’s. The available scan history lets us reconstruct what happened together. Connecting the change to specific events, such as a wedding, a trip, or an unusual week, also helps bring back more specific memories than a general question about wear time would. This way, patients can recognize that they may not have followed the plan as closely as intended without immediately feeling accused of lying.

#perche-voce[It Keeps the System’s Limitations Clear]

DentalMonitoring does not directly measure wear time, and a tooth’s failure to move does not automatically prove poor adherence. Being upfront about these limitations makes monitoring more credible and prevents it from becoming a tool for proving that the patient did something wrong. Instead, it preserves the system’s most useful function: spotting a deviation early and helping us understand its possible causes.

#perche-voce[It Makes Monitoring a Shared Responsibility]

Monitoring works only if both sides fulfill their roles: the practice must review the information it receives and intervene when necessary, while the patient must submit scans, follow instructions, and keep the channel of supervision open. This principle becomes even clearer during retention, when the guarantee does not eliminate the patient’s responsibility but makes it part of a mutual, verifiable agreement.

