#capitolo(numero: "16", etichetta: [Chapter 16], titolo: [When It’s Really Time to End Treatment], epigrafe: [“For everything there is a season, and a time for every matter under heaven.”], fonte: [Ecclesiastes 3:1])

For a long time, ending treatment before achieving every goal felt like admitting defeat. If something still needed correcting, I felt it was almost my duty to find another way to do it. My name was on the treatment, and I continued to see the incomplete result as my personal responsibility, even when the patient’s adherence had remained insufficient.

The greatest difficulty came when I knew what I wanted to achieve, but the patient continued not to wear their aligners or orthodontic elastics consistently enough. We had clarified the instructions, addressed the difficulties, and tried to get treatment back on track, without achieving a sufficiently stable change. I could plan another series of aligners, modify the attachments, or suggest more frequent checkups, but none of those measures could, on its own, make up for the adherence that was still lacking.

In those situations, each new attempt let me put off a decision I found hard to accept. As long as treatment remained open, the incomplete result still seemed provisional. Declaring it finished meant acknowledging that some goals had not been achieved and that I could not achieve them through clinical work alone.

This chapter addresses that very moment: when, after reassessing the causes of the lack of progress and trying to restore adherence, the patient’s commitment remains insufficient for treatment. There may still be an incomplete correction, a rotation, or contacts that need improvement: goals that still have clinical value but require a level of participation we cannot count on.

#strong[How do we decide whether to offer one last chance, change treatment methods, or end the active phase?];

The answer requires us to consider both what remains to be done and whether it is realistically achievable. We need to assess the importance of the remaining goals, the current clinical situation, and the behavior observed after our attempts to restore adherence. Knowing that a result could be better is not, by itself, enough to justify another phase if the necessary adherence is still lacking.

I have learned that making this decision is as much a part of the orthodontist’s responsibility as planning treatment at the outset. It requires us to be transparent about the result, discuss alternatives, and recognize what we can achieve under the present conditions, without treating every unmet goal as a personal failure.

When there is still a chance to restore adherence, we can offer one last opportunity. But it must have clear conditions and a set time for reassessment; otherwise, it risks becoming just another fresh start.

#script-titolo[The Practical Script]

The practical script has five parts: define a recovery window, assess whether treatment has resumed progressing, consider changing treatment methods, decide whether to end the active phase, and, if so, bring it to a close. What happens after reassessment depends on the clinical response and the alternatives appropriate for the patient.

#script-sub[1\. Define the Recovery Window]

Before proposing a recovery window, I reassess the possible causes of the lack of progress and confirm that the patient has received clear, consistent instructions. I need to distinguish insufficient adherence from difficulties related to treatment planning, biological response, or understanding the instructions.

With the patient, I define the behavior required, how long they need to maintain it, the clinical data we will use for reassessment, and the alternatives we will consider if progress remains insufficient. This is what I call a #strong[recovery window];.

Its length depends on the case and the movement we need to observe. We must agree on the end date from the start, along with specific instructions for wearing aligners and orthodontic elastics, changing aligners, submitting monitoring scans, and attending checkups.

The conversation can begin this way:

#script[
#emph[“I want us to find out whether we can get your treatment back on track, but this time we need to be clear about how we’ll assess the result. Until your next checkup, you’ll need to wear your aligners and elastics as directed, change aligners on schedule, and submit the scans we’ve agreed on.];

#emph[At the checkup, we’ll compare the fit and the movement achieved. If we see enough progress, we can continue. If we’re still in the same situation, we’ll need to consider a different treatment method or, if the clinical conditions allow it, ending the active phase.”];
]

That last part is essential. If we do not explain in advance what will happen if progress is insufficient, we will again be tempted to offer another chance at the reassessment. #strong[One last chance loses its meaning if it is automatically renewed.];

#script-sub[2\. When We Can Continue with Aligners]

At the checkup, I assess the fit, compare the scans, and observe the movement achieved, interpreting these findings alongside the other clinical information. What the patient tells me helps reconstruct the course of treatment, but it does not replace verifying its effectiveness.

If the patient’s behavior has changed and treatment has resumed progressing, we can continue with aligners. We do not need to expect the patient to have become perfect. We need to confirm that adherence is sufficient to achieve the remaining goals and that the progress justifies continuing.

A brief improvement in the days before the checkup may not be enough. The window must allow us to observe consistency appropriate to what we still need to achieve. If monitoring or more frequent checkups helped restore adherence, I keep that support in place for as long as needed to make the improvement stick.

#script-sub[3\. If We Need to Change Treatment Methods]

When a goal remains clinically appropriate but the patient cannot meet the demands of aligner treatment, we can consider a treatment method that depends less on wear time. A treatment can be clinically indicated yet prove difficult for a person to sustain in daily life. I prefer to explain it this way:

#script[
#emph[“Aligners can achieve the movement we need, but they require consistent wear, and so far we haven’t been able to maintain that consistency. We’ve tried several times to get treatment back on track, and I have to recognize that this method is proving difficult to fit into your daily life. If you still want to pursue the remaining goals, we can consider an appliance that depends less on wear time. I’ll explain what options it offers and what commitment it requires, so you can decide whether to take on this new phase.”];
]

Changing methods requires a new assessment. We need to discuss indications, limitations, risks, timelines, and cost, while also clarifying the adherence the new appliance will require. Replacing the treatment method does not automatically guarantee the result.

If we propose a new phase, whether through a different method or a fresh start with aligners, we must also clarify its financial terms. Scans, clinical reassessment, treatment planning, appliances, appointments, and team time all represent real work, even when the manufacturer’s package still allows us to order more series of aligners.

Any additional fee must be consistent with what was agreed upon at the outset and with the reasons for starting again. A routine refinement and the repetition of work rendered ineffective by persistently insufficient adherence call for different assessments.

There is no universal amount. The fee depends on the work required, the costs, the complexity of the new phase, and the terms communicated and agreed upon. It should not appear out of nowhere in the middle of a conflict: the possibility of an additional financial investment should be explained in advance and discussed precisely before proceeding.

Payment, however, does not prove that adherence will change. The patient may accept the cost and keep the same habits. The financial terms must therefore be accompanied by a concrete plan, with required behaviors and criteria for assessing progress.

#strong[Before starting again, we must clarify both the work required and what will make this phase different from the previous ones.];

#script-sub[4\. If We Need to End the Active Phase]

If we are considering ending the active phase, we must distinguish the reasons leading us to that choice.

In one situation, the result achieved is clinically acceptable, even if it does not perfectly match the ideal result we planned. What remains incomplete is of limited importance, and the expected benefit of another phase seems modest relative to the time and commitment it would require.

In another situation, clinically significant goals remain, but the patient is unable or unwilling to meet the conditions necessary to achieve them. They may refuse to change treatment methods, decline the additional financial investment, or be unwilling to make the required commitment.

We can then consider ending treatment with a compromise, provided the remaining condition is clinically acceptable and the risks have been assessed and explained. The difference lies in why we stop: in the first case, we believe what remains to be done does not justify another phase; in the second, we recognize the value of the remaining goals, but the patient chooses not to undertake the treatment needed to achieve them.

The compromise must be clear to the patient. We must describe what has been achieved, what remains incomplete, the alternatives proposed, and the possible consequences of the decision. If the remaining condition presents unacceptable risks, simply providing a retainer does not solve the problem: we need to propose an appropriate solution and manage any refusal while ensuring continuity of necessary care.

For this conversation, I would use a script like this:

#script[
#emph[“The result we’ve achieved still doesn’t fully match what we planned, and I want to be transparent about that. We’ve tried several times to continue, but at this point I don’t see enough reason to expect another series of aligners, worn in the same way, to produce a different result.];

#emph[Continuing would add more time to treatment without a reasonable expectation of improvement. That’s why I believe we need to choose a different path.];

#emph[If you want to pursue the remaining goals, we can consider another method. I’ll explain its indications, limitations, the commitment it requires, timeline, and costs. If instead we consider ending the active phase, we’ll need to confirm that the result we’ve achieved is clinically acceptable and clarify which goals remain unmet.];

#emph[I’ll show you what we’ve achieved, what remains to be done, and what consequences stopping could have. If we do end this phase, we’ll remove the active\-treatment components, provide an appropriate retainer, and schedule the necessary checkups.];

#emph[We’ll continue to care for you. Ending this phase means recognizing the limits of the current treatment plan and deciding how to protect the result we’ve achieved.”];
]

The patient may be disappointed, ask for another refinement, or say they are willing to continue for as long as it takes. We must listen and understand what they hope to achieve. Their willingness, however, does not automatically mean that continuing treatment is clinically appropriate.

The decision brings together two responsibilities: the patient decides what commitment they are willing to make and which proposals to accept; the clinician identifies the clinically appropriate options and explains the reasons for them.

The patient may also choose not to continue. In that case, they must understand what remains incomplete, the possible consequences, and the available alternatives. The refusal must be documented, and the patient must be given the instructions needed to manage the remaining clinical situation. Respecting that choice does not mean declaring the result achieved ideal.

#script-sub[5\. Bringing Treatment to a Close]

When the decision is to end treatment, the active phase must actually be brought to a close. It is not enough to stop ordering aligners or leave the patient wearing their last aligner while waiting to see what they will do.

I document the final clinical situation, the unmet goals, the alternatives proposed, and the patient’s choice. I remove the attachments and any other components that should not remain, provide a retainer and clear instructions, and schedule the necessary checkups.

When I end treatment because of insufficient adherence, I do not use the stabilization protocol described in earlier chapters. That protocol requires another nine months of consistent aligner wear. If that consistency was lacking during the active phase, I cannot base stabilization on the expectation that the patient will maintain it now.

Instead, I move directly to retention, adapting it to the remaining clinical situation. This phase also requires adherence: we must explain how it works, its limitations, and the checkups it requires, without implying that the result will be maintained regardless of the patient’s behavior.

Finally, the entire team must know the decision. Appointments and communications must reflect the transition from active treatment to retention. The patient must know what has ended and what care will continue.

#perche-titolo[Why This Protocol Works]

#perche-voce[A Commitment We Can Assess]

The recovery window turns a general promise into a defined behavior. The patient knows what is required of them, how long they need to maintain it, and when progress will be assessed. The orthodontist also has a set date to reassess the case, preventing the last chance from stretching on without a decision.

#perche-voce[Clinical Assessment Without Seeking a Confession]

Comparing fit, scans, and the movement achieved allows us to address treatment progress without making the patient’s honesty the focus of the conversation. What the patient says helps us reconstruct what happened, but the decision requires clinical assessment.

We must also interpret the findings in light of possible biological and biomechanical causes: a lack of movement does not automatically prove insufficient adherence.

#perche-voce[Consistency in Following Through]

If every deadline is pushed back and every reassessment leads to another fresh start, the announced consequence loses its meaning. Making a decision at the agreed\-upon deadline makes the boundaries of treatment credible.

This matters for the orthodontist, too: defining the alternatives in advance helps prevent a difficult conversation from automatically leading to another series of aligners.

#perche-voce[Clear Alternatives]

Presenting the different options helps the patient understand that the treatment plan can change. We can continue with aligners when progress has sufficiently resumed, consider another method, or end treatment when clinically appropriate.

Clear alternatives allow the patient to express their preferences and priorities. The orthodontist remains responsible for identifying appropriate options and explaining their benefits and limitations.

#perche-voce[An Informed Investment]

When a new phase involves a cost, clarifying it in advance makes the work required and the terms of starting again explicit. The patient can consider how important the result is to them and what commitment they are willing to make.

Payment does not guarantee better adherence. The additional investment must be accompanied by a concrete plan, with required behaviors and criteria for assessing progress.

#perche-voce[Continuity of Care]

Explaining what will continue after treatment ends helps reduce the fear of being abandoned. Retention, checkups, and appropriate instructions make continuity of care tangible.

Documentation preserves the reasoning behind the decision; communicating it to the team prevents inconsistent appointments or messages from leaving the patient with the impression that treatment is still open.

A treatment that remains open but can no longer make progress does not consume only time and resources. Little by little, it also begins to wear down the people who keep it going.

