  ]),
))

#capitolo(numero: "17", etichetta: [Chapter 17], titolo: [The Cost We Hadn’t Counted], epigrafe: [“The mind must be allowed to rest: after resting, it will rise refreshed and sharper.”], fonte: [Seneca])

Some days, everything seems to have gone well. You started on time, saw eighty patients, solved problems, delivered new aligners, checked on treatments progressing exactly as you planned, and met with satisfied parents, cooperative kids, and adults excited about their smiles. You may even have managed to finish almost on time, which on a day like that can count as a small victory.

Then you close the office door, say goodbye to the staff, get in your car, and head home. That’s when something strange happens: you saw eighty patients that day, and things went well with the vast majority of them, but they aren’t the ones on your mind. It’s the two or three who took up an amount of energy completely out of proportion to the time you spent with them.

There’s the one who assured you they’d worn their aligners consistently, while the fit told a completely different story; the one who, yet again, didn’t bring their aligners and forced you to piece together what might have happened over the past few weeks; the one who, after months of questionable adherence, demands another refinement as though it were the natural next step in treatment. Or there’s the patient who has just finished treatment with a result you consider clinically excellent. Looking at the photographs and the scan, she admits that her teeth look perfectly straight on the screen, but then adds that when she looks in the mirror, she still sees something she doesn’t like.

#strong[They all left the office a while ago, but some of them got into the car with you.];

This is probably one of the least discussed consequences of poor adherence. In the preceding chapters, we tried to measure many of the others, talking about time, appointments, scans, refinements, materials, costs, and staff hours. We saw how an additional series of aligners is not simply a box that arrives at the office, and how every treatment that has to be restarted involves a great deal of invisible work that rarely shows up in our accounts.

But there is a cost we haven’t counted yet, because it doesn’t appear in our practice management software, we can’t enter it on the practice balance sheet, and no one sends us an invoice at the end of the month: it’s the #strong[mental energy]; we use up every time we have to deal once again with situations we would rather not have had to handle. Every patient who needs an explanation repeated, every appointment where we have to piece together what really happened, every promise of adherence we know has been made before, every request we consider unreasonable, and every decision we are forced to make again uses up a little of our resources.

Any one of these situations means little on its own and is simply part of our work. The problem comes from the repetition and, above all, the disproportion. It isn’t necessarily the eighty patients we see in a day who wear us down: seventy or seventy\-five of them may leave us with more energy than we had in the morning, because seeing a treatment go well, a happy child, or a patient who finally smiles is one of the reasons we continue to love this work. Instead, it may be those two or three patients who, despite taking up only a small part of the schedule, occupy a huge part of our thoughts. When this pattern repeats over weeks, months, and years, what seemed like a mere daily annoyance can become something very different.

Dental research has examined occupational stress and burnout for many years, and it would be simplistic, as well as scientifically unsound, to look for a single cause. Hours worked, time pressure, financial concerns, practice management, relationships with coworkers, and clinical and administrative responsibilities are just some of the factors that can contribute to the demands of the job. Among them, though, one thing directly related to the subject of this book comes up repeatedly: the difficulty of dealing with certain patients.

Studies of dental teams have shown that so\-called difficult patients can represent a real job demand, and that greater exposure to these relationships is associated with emotional exhaustion and burnout. In a study of more than six hundred dentists, the practitioners even estimated that about a quarter of their patients could be considered problematic or difficult to manage in some way, and perceiving a higher proportion of such patients showed a particularly significant association with burnout.

Of course, this does not mean that a difficult patient causes burnout, much less that every patient with poor adherence should be considered a difficult patient. The research does not support that conclusion, and it would make no sense in our day\-to\-day practice, because most patients who forget an orthodontic elastic, fall a few hours short of the prescribed wear time, or go through a period of reduced adherence will never pose a significant problem for us.

It becomes more interesting when certain situations stop being occasional and begin to recur, continually demanding time, attention, decisions, negotiation, and sometimes conflict management. At that point, they are no longer just clinical events; they become part of the workload. An aligner that isn’t worn doesn’t mean only a few fewer hours in the patient’s mouth: it can mean a longer appointment, another explanation, an unplanned scan, a schedule change, a phone call from the front desk, a conversation with a parent or the patient, a new treatment plan, and sometimes a discussion about who should pay for what needs to be done.

A seemingly simple behavior can therefore set off a cascade of consequences that involves many people and uses up far more resources than we see when we look only at the patient’s mouth.

Before the problem even reaches us, someone may have had to answer the phone, find a new appointment in an already full schedule, call a patient who missed an appointment, or remind them what had been agreed upon. In many cases, the front desk is the first to hear a patient’s dissatisfaction, complaint, or frustration, before we even know there’s a problem.

Research on dental teams shows that receptionists, assistants, hygienists, practice managers, and clinicians can be affected in different ways by difficult relationships with patients. Qualitative studies describe something many of us have probably observed without ever giving it a name: the tension generated by a single patient can spread through the practice. The receptionist takes a difficult call and tells the assistant about it; the assistant warns the doctor before the appointment; the doctor enters the room already knowing a difficult conversation lies ahead; and if the appointment runs longer than expected, the delay affects the patients who follow.

In this way, a problem that began in one person’s mouth can change the day for many others. That is why protecting the team cannot simply mean teaching them to tolerate difficult patients better. Above all, it means avoiding, whenever possible, having to handle each situation as though it were happening for the first time.

Starting from scratch each time means asking the team to find an answer and the doctor to make another decision. I believe a substantial part of the strain comes from this: besides managing the consequences of a patient’s behavior, we have to decide how to proceed every time. The patient hasn’t worn their aligners, and we have to decide whether to let them continue or stop; they haven’t worn their orthodontic elastics, and we have to figure out how much we can trust what they tell us; the fit is off on just one tooth, and we have to choose whether to take a new scan immediately or continue; they disappeared for a year and call today saying they want to start again, so we have to decide where to pick up and what costs they will have to cover; they’ve already had several refinements and keep asking for changes to details we consider clinically irrelevant, forcing us once again to decide where to draw the line.

Taken individually, these are simple problems, and an experienced clinician is perfectly capable of solving them. The problem arises when we have to solve them hundreds of times, perhaps trying to remember what we decided six months earlier with a similar patient, explaining to the staff why we’re doing something different this time, and later wondering whether another approach would have been better.

This is where a model widely used in the literature on work\-related stress becomes relevant: the Job Demands–Resources model. Put very simply, it distinguishes between the demands our work continually places on us, or job demands, and the resources we have available to meet them, or job resources. Demands require and consume energy, while resources help us manage those demands while maintaining control, motivation, and effectiveness. This model has also been studied specifically in dentists, and some longitudinal studies involving thousands of clinicians have shown an association between high job demands and burnout and, on the other hand, the positive role of job resources in supporting work engagement.

Reading these studies, I realized that, without claiming a scientific significance for the term that the studies have not directly established, we can also think of a protocol as a resource in our day\-to\-day practice: if a situation recurs and we’ve already decided how to handle it, we don’t have to start from scratch every time.

#sezione[That’s Why This Book Has Protocols]

At this point, we can finally look back and see everything we’ve built in the preceding pages from a different perspective. If you’ve read this book from the beginning, you’ve encountered many protocols, many scripts, and probably a few rules that may have seemed rigid to you, at least at first. We’ve talked about the patient who doesn’t wear their aligners, the one who doesn’t wear their orthodontic elastics, the one whose aligners lose their fit or who doesn’t wear their retainer, the one who disappears and then returns a long time later, the one who needs a routine refinement and the one whose lack of adherence forces us to repeat a phase of treatment, all the way to the patient who keeps asking for something more when we believe we’ve already achieved the result.

For each of these situations, we’ve tried to decide in advance what to do—not because we want to turn medicine into a rulebook, or because every patient should be treated the same way. A protocol should never replace a doctor’s judgment, and there will always be situations where making an exception is the right thing to do. The fundamental difference is that the exception should be a conscious choice on our part, not the result of our having failed to decide what to do.

After so many years in practice, this distinction has become essential to me, because a protocol doesn’t just tell me how to manage the patient; it saves me from having to reinvent how I handle the same problem every time. Likewise, a script doesn’t just help me find the right words: it makes sure those words don’t depend on how tired I am, the time of day, whether I’m running late, or how much that patient has managed to get on my nerves. Above all, it helps everyone in the practice speak the same language.

If the front desk already knows what happens when a patient disappears, they don’t have to ask me every time; if the team knows how we handle a refinement needed because of poor adherence, they don’t have to guess what to do; if the patient knows from the outset what the consequences of certain behaviors will be, the later conversation becomes easier. We aren’t making up a rule when the problem arises; we’re applying something we’ve already discussed with them.

None of this eliminates difficult patients or prevents poor adherence, but it can eliminate a substantial part of the effort involved in managing those situations. And that may be why, now that I’ve reached the end of this book, I see scripts and protocols not just as organizational or clinical tools, but as a form of protection: they let the pilot and crew know what to do even when a passenger decides to behave in a way we had hoped they wouldn’t.

That protection also begins with recognizing the limits of our control. With aligners, we’ve developed extraordinary tools for planning tooth movement: we plan in tenths of a millimeter, degrees of torque, intrusion, expansion, sequences, attachments, and staging, spending hours in front of a simulation trying to predict what will happen months later in someone’s mouth.

We can design the best possible treatment, but we can’t wear the aligners for the patient; we can explain how important orthodontic elastics are, but we can’t be there every evening to check that they’ve put them on; we can remind them of appointments, but we can’t force them to show up; we can show them photographs, scans, and results, but we can’t decide for them whether they’ll be satisfied. Above all, we can’t make them tell us exactly what happened every time.

I used to believe that getting better at this work meant being able to control more and more things. To some extent, I still believe that, because clinical experience is precisely what improves our ability to predict, prevent, and manage what might happen. But there is another kind of experience, one that probably takes longer to acquire: learning to recognize what we will never be able to control and no longer spending energy trying to control it.

When I started writing this book, I thought it would be primarily about patient adherence. By the time I reached the end, I realized it had also become a book about us and what we can do to make a treatment easier when, by its very nature, it depends to a considerable extent on another person’s behavior.

None of the scripts, rules, costs, protocols, or strategies described in the preceding pages is meant to punish patients who don’t follow instructions or to create a practice where there are no exceptions. They are meant to establish clear boundaries, understandable responsibilities, and predictable consequences, so that our relationship with the patient can remain as positive as possible even when something doesn’t go as planned. They certainly help protect the clinical result, the practice’s time, and the team’s work. But having written this book, I believe they also help protect something I took almost for granted for many years: the pleasure of doing this work.

Because in the long run, the risk isn’t just losing a few hours or having to order a few more aligners. The risk is allowing a very small percentage of our patients to take up too large a share of our energy, until we forget that the vast majority of the people we saw that same day gave us a sense of satisfaction, trusted us, followed the treatment plan, and perhaps reminded us exactly why we chose this profession.

After eighty patients, then, I’d like to be able to go home remembering them above all. Not because the others don’t exist or because problems should be ignored, but because those problems need a place, a time, and a response. Once we’ve built a system that can provide one, we can lighten the burden we keep carrying home even after our work is done.

Perhaps the real success of all the protocols described in this book isn’t measured only by the number of refinements they help us avoid, the time they give back to the schedule, or the costs they help contain. It’s also measured by the decisions we no longer have to make, the discussions we no longer have to improvise, the calls the staff already knows how to handle, and the peace of mind that comes from accepting that part of the treatment will never be entirely up to us.

In the end, perhaps this whole book can be summed up like this: we can’t stop patients from forgetting, not following instructions, disappearing, changing their minds, or giving us an account of what happened that doesn’t match reality. But we can decide in advance what to do when it happens, protecting the treatment, our practice, the people who work with us, and, above all, the pleasure of continuing to do this work.

#strong[Because we can’t stop a patient from lying, but we can keep that lie from coming home with us.];

